window.HOLISTICRX_DATA = {"note":"Transcribed from Nourhan Shams's copy of The Holistic Rx: Your Guide to Healing Chronic Inflammation and Disease (Madiha Saeed, MD; Rowman & Littlefield, 2021) — the foundations chapters on inflammation, digestive health and detoxification, the Four Big S's, and the per-condition Holistic Rx protocols from its cardiology through urology chapters. Every recommendation here is the book's; none of it was written for this tool, and none of it is peer reviewed. It sits alongside the Master Compendium under each condition's From your notes section, credited separately.","source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD","chapters":[],"topics":[{"name":"Graves' disease","blurb":"Autoimmune hyperthyroidism from overproduction of thyroid hormone."},{"name":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis","blurb":"Autoimmune thyroiditis and the most common cause of hypothyroidism."},{"name":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","blurb":"Chronic autoimmune disease that can attack skin, joints, brain, heart, lungs and blood cells."},{"name":"Hyperlipidemia","blurb":"Elevated cholesterol/triglycerides — a major modifiable cardiovascular risk factor addressed with diet, targeted supplements, and lab-guided therapy."},{"name":"Tooth decay","blurb":"Dental caries and enamel demineralization driven by mineral and fat-soluble-vitamin deficiency, excess sugar, and phytic acid — addressed with a remineralizing diet, targeted supplements, and homeopathy."},{"name":"Toothache","blurb":"Acute tooth or gum pain, matched here to individual homeopathic remedies by its exact character, location, and triggers."},{"name":"Rosacea","blurb":"Chronic inflammatory facial redness with flushing, bumps, and visible blood vessels, often triggered by diet, stress, or Candida."},{"name":"Tinnitus","blurb":"Ringing, buzzing, or roaring sounds in the ear, often from inner-ear damage, blockage, or infection."},{"name":"Adrenal fatigue and HPA dysregulation","blurb":"Adrenal exhaustion, cortisol dysregulation, and adaptogen support."},{"name":"Diverticulosis","blurb":"Pouches that form in the colon wall and can inflame or infect."},{"name":"Peptic ulcer disease","blurb":"Erosions of the stomach, duodenal, or esophageal lining, often from H. pylori or NSAIDs."},{"name":"Uterine fibroids","blurb":"Benign uterine growths that can cause heavy, prolonged periods and pelvic pain."},{"name":"Irregular periods and abnormal uterine bleeding","blurb":"Menstrual cycle irregularities, missed periods, and abnormal bleeding from hormonal imbalance."},{"name":"Cancer (general oncology support)","blurb":"General nutritional, supplement, and immune-support protocol to use alongside conventional cancer treatment."},{"name":"Brain health and neuroinflammation (general)","blurb":"Gut-brain axis, BDNF, and general brain-protective diet and lifestyle strategies underlying every neurologic protocol."},{"name":"Dementia and Alzheimer's disease","blurb":"Progressive memory, behavior, and thinking decline; supplement, homeopathic, and lifestyle support for cognitive protection."},{"name":"Headaches (tension, cluster, and migraine)","blurb":"Recurring head pain — tension, cluster, and migraine subtypes — with targeted supplement and homeopathic protocols."},{"name":"Peripheral neuropathy (nerve pain)","blurb":"Pins-and-needles, burning, or electric-shock nerve pain from diabetes, deficiency, trauma, toxins, or infection."},{"name":"Restless leg syndrome","blurb":"Uncontrollable urge to move the legs with tingling, cramping, or creeping sensations, worse in the evening."},{"name":"Cataracts","blurb":"Clumps of protein clouding the eye's lens, the leading cause of vision loss after age forty."},{"name":"Diabetic retinopathy","blurb":"Diabetes-related damage to the blood vessels of the retina that can progress to blindness."},{"name":"Dry eye","blurb":"Inadequate tear production or drainage leaving the eyes under-lubricated and irritated."},{"name":"Glaucoma","blurb":"Slow-progressing build-up of eye fluid pressure that damages the optic nerve and vision."},{"name":"Macular degeneration","blurb":"Age-related retinal breakdown (dry or wet AMD) causing progressive loss of central vision."},{"name":"Pediatric foundations and gut health (general)","blurb":"General diet, stress, supplement, and modality guidance for supporting a child's growth, gut health, and resilience."},{"name":"Autism spectrum disorder","blurb":"A neurobehavioral disorder of childhood with social, communication, and sensory features that respond to targeted testing and biomedical support."},{"name":"PANDAS (pediatric autoimmune neuropsychiatric disorders associated with streptococcus)","blurb":"Sudden-onset tics, obsessions/compulsions, and mood changes triggered or worsened by streptococcal infection in children."},{"name":"Colic","blurb":"Excessive infant crying and fussiness often tied to gut immaturity or maternal-diet food sensitivities passed through breast milk."},{"name":"Failure to thrive","blurb":"Inadequate weight gain or growth in a child, often driven by poor absorption or ongoing gut inflammation."},{"name":"Picky eating","blurb":"Selective or limited eating in children that can leave nutrient gaps needing targeted supplementation and consistent family habits."},{"name":"Dyspraxia","blurb":"A developmental coordination disorder affecting motor planning and movement in children."},{"name":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)","blurb":"A pattern of unreasonable, intrusive fears (obsessions) that drive repetitive behaviors (compulsions) interfering with daily life."},{"name":"Eating disorders","blurb":"Anorexia, bulimia, and binge eating disorder — nutrient repletion, appetite support, and psychological care alongside professional treatment."},{"name":"Schizophrenia","blurb":"A chronic thought disorder with hallucinations and delusions — adjunctive nutrient and homeopathic support alongside psychiatric care."},{"name":"Substance abuse and addiction","blurb":"Drug, alcohol, and nicotine dependence — nutrient repletion and withdrawal support alongside professional treatment."},{"name":"Muscle cramps and spasms","blurb":"Involuntary muscle contractions from poor circulation, dehydration, mineral deficiency, overexertion, or medication side effects."},{"name":"Leaky gut syndrome","blurb":"Increased intestinal permeability that lets undigested food particles, toxins, and microbes cross into the bloodstream, driving systemic inflammation and a wide range of downstream disease."}],"sections":[{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_general_dd","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Autoimmune conditions — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Autoimmune disease is the immune system's misguided attack on the body: excess inflammation confuses its ability to tell self from nonself, autoantibodies form against normal cells, and regulatory T cells fail to keep the response in check. There are more than eighty known autoimmune diseases, and having one makes you three times more likely to develop another.","Diet and detox special considerations: avoid nightshades (tomatoes, eggplant, potatoes, peppers), legumes/lentils, eggs, caffeine, nuts, and seeds — common food sensitivities in autoimmune disease. Fix deficiencies in calcium, iron, vitamin D, zinc, B6, B12, and folate. Eat vegetables at every meal, and favor sulfur-rich foods (cabbage, onions, garlic, leeks, cauliflower, asparagus) to help reduce symptoms; use coconut oil and olive oil for healthy fats. Raw, unprocessed honey can also boost immunity.","For thyroid autoimmunity specifically, increase fatty acids, iodine (seaweed, saltwater fish, or a multivitamin), tyrosine (red meat, chicken, seaweed, seafood), selenium (red meats, liver, chicken, turkey, fish, shellfish), zinc (red meats, chicken, seafood, seaweed), iron, omega-3s, vitamin D3, B vitamins (leafy greens, broccoli, beets, red meat, liver), and vitamin A (orange fruits/veggies, liver, kale). Kale and broccoli are goitrogens, but their benefits outweigh that concern. Avoid IV detoxification in all cases of autoimmunity.","The Four Big S's: stress management is key to healing autoimmune disease as with any chronic condition, including choosing gentler exercise — jogging and heavy weightlifting can be too harsh on the joints. Flexibility, strengthening, aerobic exercise, and body-awareness practices like tai chi, yoga, and Pilates improve posture, coordination, balance, and joint/muscular mobility. An exercise physiologist can help find what works best for you."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_infections_ldn","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Autoimmune conditions — infections and low-dose naltrexone","body":["If symptoms — especially fatigue and brain symptoms — persist after three months of the foundational GutRx, consider that an infection may be contributing. A functional medicine practitioner can test for the microbe associated with your specific condition; leaky gut, stress, and inflammatory food can reactivate dormant infections and trigger the immune system. Look for coinfections (parasites, Candida, bacterial overgrowth) alongside the primary infection, and expect treatment to take time, especially with more than one infecting agent.","Table 5.1 (Autoimmunity and Infections) pairs specific autoimmune diseases with implicated microbes: autoimmunity in general with E. coli, Proteus, Klebsiella; ankylosing spondylitis with Klebsiella; Graves' disease and Hashimoto's thyroiditis with herpes, EBV, MAP, H. pylori, Yersinia, and Campylobacter; Guillain-Barré syndrome with Campylobacter, cytomegalovirus, and EBV; multiple sclerosis with Chlamydia pneumoniae, EBV, and measles; rheumatoid arthritis with Citrobacter, Klebsiella, Proteus, Porphyromonas, EBV, E. coli, hepatitis C, and mycobacterium; rheumatic fever with Streptococcus pyogenes; and SLE/lupus, autism, uveitis, keratitis, autoimmune hepatitis, polymyositis, psoriasis, ITP, IgA nephropathy, glomerulonephritis, pemphigus, Sjögren's syndrome, type 1 diabetes mellitus, and myasthenia gravis with various combinations of EBV, cytomegalovirus, herpes 1/2/6, measles, coxsackievirus, and hepatitis C.","Viral infections: chronic viral infections (herpes 1, 2, 6; Epstein-Barr virus) can hide dormant and trigger inflammation. For EBV activity, check EBV EA-D IgG (active/reactivated infection), EBV VCA IgG/IgM (past or reactivated infection), and EBV EBNA-1 IgG (past infection, or active replication in B cells when combined with a positive VCA). Treat with Lauricidin (1 tsp three times daily, an acid derived from coconut oil) and humic acid (2 pills/day) to lower viral load; L-lysine 500 mg for prevention, 1–4 g/day with food during an outbreak. Herbal options: Lomatium (can cause a one-time rash), Cordyceps (750 mg, two capsules, three times daily for ninety days), or olive leaf extract (one capsule twice daily for sixty days). Add NAC (1,800 mg/day), thymic protein A, adaptogens, vitamin D, vitamin C (500–3,000 mg/day), or lysine for additional support.","Bacterial infections: implicated bacteria include Yersinia enterocolitica (specialized stool test), H. pylori (breath, stool antigen, or blood test), SIBO (breath test), Chlamydia pneumoniae, and Bartonella or Mycobacterium avium subspecies paratuberculosis (MAP). Treatment combines antibiotics, probiotics (dosed apart from the antibiotic), caprylic acid, and an enzyme blend of cellulase, hemicellulase, xylanase, and beta-glucanase to prevent yeast overgrowth. H. pylori: zinc, berberine, bismuth citrate, mastic gum, oil pulling, and cranberry juice. Yersinia: sweet wormwood, grapefruit seed extract, berberine, black walnut, uva ursi, or doxycycline. Garlic, licorice, quercetin, CoQ10, turmeric, and oil of oregano help broadly. SIBO: rifaximin (Xifaxan), adding neomycin or tinidazole if methane-predominant. Chlamydia pneumoniae: minocycline 100 mg twice daily for three weeks.","Lyme disease: if symptoms don't improve, consider Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi), which can mimic autoimmune symptoms and affect multiple organs. Ask for advanced testing (iSpot Lyme, ELISA, western blot, Lyme PCR); antibiotic treatment may follow a positive result. Supportive foods/herbs: thyme, lemon balm, zinc, licorice root, L-lysine, lomatium root, reishi mushroom, silver hydrosol, astaxanthin, and Nascent iodine.","Fungal infections: systemic Candida is common in autoimmune disease — test with blood work and stool IgG/IgM. If positive: Diflucan 100 mg twice daily for three weeks, or nystatin 500,000 units, two capsules, three times daily for thirty to ninety days.","Parasitic infections: test for toxoplasmosis (blood) and Blastocystis hominis (stool). Treat blastocystis with Alinia 1,000 mg twice daily for three days, repeated after two weeks and again two weeks later, or nystatin as above (for fungal overgrowth alongside or after Alinia). Herbal options: CandiBactin (two capsules three times daily for sixty days), a wormwood-containing antiparasitic (600 mg twice daily for seven days, repeated in two weeks — avoid with a history of liver problems), or oil of oregano (150 mg, two capsules, three times daily for sixty days) combined with Saccharomyces boulardii (5–15 billion CFU, two to four times daily for sixty days).","Low-dose naltrexone: as an opioid antagonist, naltrexone can enhance immune function — increasing endogenous adrenaline production, reducing inflammation, and increasing regulatory T-cell cytokines including IL-1 and TGF-B — helpful for those with multiple autoimmune conditions or symptoms and very high antibodies. For autoimmunity, use only low doses, 1.5–4.5 mg/day, starting at 1.5 mg at night. It should not be a first line of defense — use it only after implementing the foundational recommendations and if symptoms persist."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_celiac","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Celiac disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Celiac disease is an autoimmune reaction to gluten. Typical symptoms: recurrent bloating, cramping, abdominal pain, brain fog, weight changes, insomnia and other sleep issues, fatigue, headaches, delayed puberty, pale foul-smelling stool, peripheral neuropathy, joint pain, mood changes, thinning hair, pale mouth sores, tingling of hands/feet, vomiting, unexplained weight loss, unexplained infertility or recurrent miscarriage, and dermatitis herpetiformis (skin rash). Testing: IgA and IgG anti-gliadin antibodies, IgA anti-endomysial antibodies, tissue transglutaminase antibodies, total IgA, and genetic testing (HLA DQ2, HLA DQ8).","Supplements: supplements that heal leaky gut are key — digestive enzymes with amylase (a pancreatic enzyme, to enhance digestion), probiotics, B vitamins (B2, B3, B6, B9, B12), calcium, magnesium, zinc, and other gut-healing agents like L-glutamine, N-acetyl-D-glucosamine, and gamma oryzanol. In children, start with digestive enzymes and probiotics.","Homeopathy: early constitutional treatment from an experienced homeopath — matched to the person's most distressing symptoms — can help with celiac-related constipation, increased frequency of bulky/offensive-smelling stools, allergies, gastroenteritis, and emotional issues.","Acupressure: P6, SP6, CV12.","Aromatherapy: caraway, lavender, and bitter orange help harmonize gut bacterial balance. Peppermint oil and cumin can ease digestive symptoms; ginger and turmeric protect the stomach and support digestion. Peppermint and ginger work well together for nausea."],"conditions":["Celiac disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_graves","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Graves' disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder causing overproduction of thyroid hormone. Symptoms: mood changes, racing heart, muscle pain, insomnia, digestive issues, hand/finger tremors, increased perspiration or warm moist skin, thyroid gland enlargement, irregular periods, skin-texture changes (thickened skin on the lower legs or red bumps — pretibial myxedema), and eye problems (bulging/exophthalmos, pain, redness, vision loss). Tests: TSH, free T4, free T3, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI), and TSH receptor antibody.","Supplements: B-complex (50 mg twice daily). L-carnitine (start 2,000 mg/day, work up to 4,000 mg/day as needed) can reduce heart palpitations, tremors, insomnia, and anxiety. CoQ10 (100–400 mg/day with a fat-containing meal) helps muscle weakness and pain. Plant sterols to balance the immune system. Selenium (200 mcg/day) may slow progression of eye symptoms. Magnesium (400 mg/day) helps prevent arrhythmias. Calcium citrate 1,000 mg (ages 19–50) or 1,200 mg (women 51+ and men over 71), alongside optimized vitamin D. Bugleweed (start 2 mL three times daily, increasing to 4 mL after three days and 6 mL three days later) can prevent thyroid damage and calm the thyroid, keeping antibodies from binding to it. Motherwort liquid extract (start 2 mL daily, increase to 4 mL after five days) helps palpitations, sleep issues, appetite issues, and anxiety — talk to your doctor, and don't combine with sedating medications. Lemon balm (start 300 mg, increase to 600 mg after seven days) inhibits antibodies from binding to the thyroid gland. Glucomannan (start 1.5 g twice daily, work up to 3.0 g twice daily) decreases circulating thyroid hormone levels.","Homeopathy: Iodum 30C for someone who feels very hot, is always in a hurry, obsessive, dark-haired, and dark-eyed — also indicated for weight loss despite an increased appetite, extreme heat sensitivity, and seeking coolness. Natrum mur 30C for palpitations and constipation, in someone who looks anemic, is weak, and craves salt. Lycopus 30C for a racing, pounding heart from nervous irritation, with exophthalmos. Sulphur for heat intolerance. Pulsatilla for heat intolerance with no thirst. Lachesis for hot flashes with intolerance of tight clothing. Lycopodium for weight loss (more upper body), craving warm drinks and sweets. Phosphorus for extreme weakness with excessive, offensive stool. Others that can be used: Kali iodatum, Ferrum metallicum, Lycopodium clavatum, and Spongia tosta.","Acupressure: GV17, ST9, P6, HT7, LI4, ST30.","Aromatherapy: frankincense (two drops daily on the roof of the mouth) and myrrh (applied over the thyroid area twice daily) can help, along with anti-inflammatory oils like lavender, rose, and tea tree — combined with coconut oil, these can soothe irritated skin."],"conditions":["Graves' disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_hashimotos","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Hashimoto's is autoimmune thyroiditis. Symptoms: fatigue, depression and mood disorders, weight gain, feeling cold easily, digestive issues, muscular pain, joint stiffness and swelling, hair loss, frequent urination and excessive thirst, and menstrual changes. Assessment: TSH, free T4, free T3, antithyroglobulin antibody, and antithyroid peroxidase antibodies (which can appear decades before TSH is affected), plus reverse T3 and ferritin; vitamin A, selenium, and zinc can also be checked. Landmarks of healing: symptom improvement or resolution, antibody count dropping first under 100 and then under 35, and regeneration of thyroid tissue.","Supplements: probiotics. Multivitamin with vitamin A (5,000–10,000 IU/day). Vitamin E (400 IU). B vitamins: riboflavin (50 mg/day), niacin (200 mg/day), B6 (50 mg/day), folate (800 mcg/day), B12 (1,000 mcg/day of methylcobalamin), biotin (5,000 mcg/day), and B1 (600 mg/day specifically for fatigue). Iodine (150–300 mcg/day). Zinc (25–50 mg/day). Magnesium (500 mg/day). Vitamin C (1,000 mg/day). Iron glycinate (25 mg/day; keep ferritin between 50 and 100 if still menstruating). Omega-3 fish oil (1,000 mg twice daily with food). Selenium (200–400 mcg) has been shown to help regulate the hormones. Others: Antabine, shown to reduce TPO antibodies by preventing the inflammatory immune system from responding; also curcumin, Thytrophin PMG, or Drenatrophin PMG. Children with hypothyroidism: iodine (50–900 mcg/day), selenium (20–200 mcg/day), tyrosine (500 mg/day), vitamin A (200,000 IU as one dose), and seaweed/kelp (2 tbsp/day).","Homeopathy: Arsenicum 30C every twelve hours for up to five days while a constitutional remedy is being found. Calcarea carbonica for constipation (hard-then-soft stool) and heavy menses. Sepia for weak patients with a pale yellow face, feeling cold, and a sensation of bearing-down; helps control excessive hair loss — these patients crave acidic foods and pickles. Lycopodium for gastric problems with excessive flatulence, craving hot foods, weakness, and irritability. Graphites for those who tire easily, are obese, become sad when listening to music, and are constipated and timid. Nux vomica for hypothyroidism with people who are easily offended, constipated, bloated after eating, crave stimulants, and crave fatty and spicy foods; these people also feel cold.","Acupressure: ST36, P6, KD3, GV7, KD7, SP6, LI10, LI11.","Aromatherapy: frankincense can help lower thyroid antibodies when used alongside other modalities. Peppermint oil can help reduce brain fog, depression, headaches, and digestive issues. Lavender and myrtle may help restore hair and calm anxiety. Others that help hypothyroidism symptoms: lemongrass, myrrh, clove, basil, marjoram, rosemary, grapefruit, and clary sage."],"conditions":["Hashimoto's thyroiditis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_ms","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Multiple sclerosis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Multiple sclerosis (MS) occurs when the immune system attacks the myelin sheath covering nerve fibers, permanently damaging the nerves and disrupting nervous-system communication. Symptoms depend on the location and extent of the damage and may include fatigue, dizziness, slurred speech, prolonged double vision, involuntary eye movement (nystagmus), tremor, bowel and bladder problems, poor coordination, numbness or weakness in one or more limbs or the trunk (often one-sided), and electric-shock sensations with certain movements (Lhermitte's sign). Diagnosis: imaging and spinal tap.","Supplements: vitamin E (400 mg of mixed tocotrienols or mixed tocopherols daily). High-potency B-complex (B-100) daily, including B6, B7 (biotin), and B12. Cyanocobalamin (1,000 mcg sublingually each day). L-methylfolate (levomefolic acid, 1,000 mcg daily). CoQ10 (200 mg daily; doses up to 2,400 mg/day have been well tolerated, though above 600 mg/day some note heartburn, digestive upset, and headaches — it may also reduce the effectiveness of blood-thinning medications). Digestive enzymes with protease, bromelain, amylase, and lipase. Algae (two to eight capsules, or up to one teaspoon of powder, daily). Alpha lipoic acid (300–600 mg/day) for nerve pain; aged garlic to lower oxidative stress and improve blood flow.","Homeopathy: Agaricus for uncoordinated eye movement, muscle spasms, twitching, poor coordination, sharp shooting pains, and weak, shaky movements. Alumina for weakness, progressive paralysis, heaviness of the lower extremities, confusion, constipation, and dry skin or mucous membranes — better in open air and damp weather, worse in the morning and in a warm room. Argentum nitricum for progressive paralysis, poor coordination, loss of balance, trembling, always feeling in a rush, and multiple phobias. Arsenicum album for anxious, pessimistic, restless people with progressive paralysis and burning sensations. Causticum for slow, progressive paralysis of the limbs, numbness of hands and feet, problems with breathing, swallowing, and speech, incontinence, and restlessness at night. Cocculus for paralysis with dizziness when trying to focus on moving objects, painful contracture of the limbs and trunk, and feeling too weak to talk loudly. Conium for paralysis of the eye muscles and ascending paralysis beginning with weakness and heaviness in the thighs and lower limbs, worse in the morning. Gelsemium sempervirens for trembling and weak muscles, heavy eyelids, double vision, numbness of the face and tongue, improvement after urination, and dizziness/drowsiness. Ignatia amara for twitching and spasms especially when MS presents after grief, with emotional upset and heightened senses. Kali phos for weakness in the back and limbs worse with exercise, leaving pain, fatigue, and possible inability to move the extremity. Lachesis when jealousy or anger worsen symptoms, more left-sided, worse premenstrually or at the start of menopause. Natrum muriaticum for optic neuritis (eye pain and temporary vision loss) with body numbness, worse in the sun, coming on after grief, low mood, and awkwardness such as dropping things. Nux vomica for those who crave alcohol and stimulants, are irritable, highly driven, and intolerant of contradiction. Magnesia phos for painful spasms of twitching or jerking, improved by pressure, friction, and warmth. Phosphorus for vision problems and incontinence with numbness in the hands and feet, craving ice-cold drinks, exaggerated reflexes, fainting easily, and episodes linked to emotional upset. Plumbum for progressive paralysis, heaviness, and wasting of the muscles, especially in the legs. Tarentula for very jerky movements of the hands, feet, and tongue.","Acupressure: GV20 (headaches, poor memory, mental disorders, ringing in the ears, fatigue, and poor sleep); GV26, P6, LU9, GB34, LU1 (anxiety or depression); GV26 (reduces muscle spasms and relieves dizziness); ST36 (improves digestion); SP6, H7, B2, B23, B13, GB20, and LV3.","Aromatherapy: relaxing oils and those that support the neurological system are important — find what works best and rotate as needed. Frankincense and helichrysum support the neurological system; frankincense can be taken internally, two drops three times daily for three weeks, then one week off, repeating the cycle, while helichrysum can be applied to the neck or temples twice daily. Basil oil and cypress oil can reduce MS symptoms by improving circulation and muscle tone. Other good choices: lavender, jasmine, neroli, geranium, ylang-ylang, and rose."],"conditions":["Multiple sclerosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_lupus","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Lupus is a chronic autoimmune condition in which the body attacks its own tissues, potentially affecting the skin, brain, heart, lungs, and blood cells. Presentation varies widely and may come on suddenly or develop slowly, and may be mild or severe, temporary or permanent. Common signs and symptoms: fatigue, joint pain, dry eyes, sun sensitivity, skin lesions that worsen with sun exposure, chest pain, a butterfly-shaped rash across the bridge of the nose and cheeks, fingers and toes that turn white or blue with cold or stress (Raynaud's phenomenon), headaches, confusion, and memory loss. Diagnosis is made via history, physical exam, and laboratory testing.","Supplements: plant sterols and sterolins (20 mg three times daily on an empty stomach). Methylsulfonylmethane (MSM) (2,000–8,000 mg daily) for its natural anti-inflammatory benefits — reduce the dose if diarrhea occurs. Digestive enzymes (1–2 capsules of a full-spectrum enzyme product with each meal). Green superfood supplements chlorella and spirulina are key for alkalizing and detoxifying the body. Boswellia (1,200–1,500 mg of a standardized extract containing 60–65 percent boswellic acids, two to three times daily) for its anti-inflammatory effects. DHEA to support adrenal hormones — have DHEA-S measured in the blood and dose to bring levels above 100 mcg/dl; the usual recommended dose is 25 mg, but confirm the right dose against your own DHEA-S level. Indole-3-carbinol (DIM), broccoli extracts, and sulforaphane all help detoxify estrogens.","Homeopathy: Arsenicum album for anxious, restless individuals with a burning sensation in the joints that feels better with warmth. Belladonna when pain and swelling come on suddenly, with hot, red joints and burning pain worse with motion. Sepia for women whose lupus flares near their menstrual cycle, with constipation, depression, irritability, a tendency to feel cold, and cravings for sweet, salty, and sour foods. Pulsatilla when symptoms improve with cold and pain wanders from joint to joint. Rhus toxicodendron for pain that increases with rest and is worse in cold and damp, with joint stiffness that improves with movement and warmth. Sulphur for strong cravings for spicy foods and ice-cold drinks, getting overheated easily, and burning pain relieved by cold applications.","Acupressure: S13 (stiff neck, headaches, ringing in the ears, weakness of the arms, and back muscle spasm/ache); GV26, SP10, SP4, SP6, SP21, LI4, LI11, LV3, LV5.","Aromatherapy: use essential oils that promote relaxation, help digestion, and support the neurological system — good starting choices are lemon verbena, lavender, rose, jasmine, and geranium. Black pepper and ginger encourage blood flow and help revive tired joints and muscles; for digestive issues, ginger oil can be taken internally, three drops, two to three times daily. Frankincense essential oil is effective at reducing inflammation and immune reactions, and helichrysum oil supports the nervous system. For skin inflammation, lavender and geranium oils work well; lemon balm or juniper in a hot bath can help detoxify the body and lower inflammation."],"conditions":["Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_autoimmune_ra","chapter":"autoimmune","title":"Rheumatoid arthritis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) occurs when inflammation attacks the lining of the joints, in addition to a wide variety of other body systems including the eyes, lungs, heart, blood vessels, and skin. Signs and symptoms: fatigue, fever, weight loss, joint stiffness (usually worse in the morning and after inactivity), and tender, warm, swollen joints — early on it can affect the smaller joints and then progress to larger joints like wrists, elbows, shoulders, knees, and hips. Diagnosis is made via history, a physical exam, and lab work.","Supplements: gamma linolenic acid (GLA) (450–500 mg/day). Curcumin and boswellia reduce pain and inflammation in the joints — can be used alone or in a combination product. Turmeric (1,000 mg once a day). Fish oil (1–3 g of EPA/DHA per day). Others: bromelain, MSM, glucosamine, vitamin C (500–1,000 mg/day), selenium (200–400 mcg/day), vitamin E (200–400 IU/day), ginger (100–200 mg three times daily), and one of grapeseed extract (100–300 mg/day), pine bark extract (100–300 mg/day), or another antioxidant. Juvenile RA: 5-MTHF (200–400 mcg/day), vitamin C (500–1,000 mg/day), calcium (1,000 mg/day), vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU/day), CoQ10 (10–250 mg/day), vitamin E (200–400 IU/day), fish oil (2–4 g of EPA/day), zinc (10–20 mg/day), selenium (20–200 mcg/day), and castor oil applied topically to the joints daily.","Homeopathy: Bryonia for severe pain made worse by movement, the slightest touch, or heat, and relieved by cold applications. Pulsatilla for pain that passes from one part of the body to another and is made worse by warm environments; suitable if fatty food is hard to digest and there's a tendency to feel sad. Causticum for pain accompanied by muscle spasms that tends to improve in wet weather or with warmth. Rhus tox for stiffness that tends to wear off with movement (causing restlessness), worse in cold and damp weather, after rest, or in the morning. Colchicum for stiffness and pain worse in winter and during the nighttime hours. Dulcamara for stiffness and pain worse when overheated, and in cold or damp weather. Mercurius for aches and pains worsened by heat and cold, worse at night, accompanied by offensive sweat. Ruta for pain that especially affects the tendonous insertions of muscles and large joints. Rhododendron for aches and pains worse in cold weather and during electrical storms, located in the muscles and ligaments. Calcarea hypophos for sharp pains in the wrists and hands. Aconite for sudden onset of sharp pain, worse in cold and dry weather, with general restlessness and anxiety.","Acupressure: the Yang Lin Qian point (for inflammation and joint pain); LI4, LV2, LV5, GB41, GB34.","Aromatherapy: ginger essential oil has anti-inflammatory and analgesic effects; other helpful oils include juniper, vetiver, ylang-ylang, turmeric, frankincense, myrrh, and orange essential oils."],"conditions":["Rheumatoid arthritis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_dd_general","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Cardiology — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Almost all cardiac risk factors are related to lifestyle, environment, and inflammation, so treating insulin resistance and lowering inflammation are the cornerstones of preventing and healing heart disease. Add herbs and spices — turmeric, garlic, ginger, cinnamon, curry powder, rosemary, thyme, basil, chili peppers, and coriander — and focus on fiber and healthy fats. Green, black, and oolong tea are great for the heart, as they contain flavonoids and have an anticlotting effect. Increase fiber up to 50 g per day with nuts, seeds, berries, and vegetables. High-potassium foods (avocados, melons, spinach, broccoli, squash) and apple cider vinegar (helps keep the body alkaline) have been shown to improve blood pressure; omega-3 foods like grass-fed beef, wild-caught salmon, chia, and flax are great for the heart. Other foods to focus on: pomegranate juice (8 oz)/seeds, watercress, goji berries, apples, capers, olives, onions, green pepper, and scallions. Limit/eliminate alcohol and caffeine, and eat about an ounce of dark chocolate daily. Detoxify the environment from lead, cadmium, and mercury.","The Four Big S's: stress raises blood pressure — it constricts blood vessels and worsens heart disease — so a daily relaxation technique is important. Moderate exercise lowers insulin resistance, inflammation, and blood pressure (consult a doctor before starting a program, as some conditions need to be addressed first). Chiropractic care can also improve cardiovascular problems."],"conditions":["Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Hyperlipidemia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_atherosclerosis","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Atherosclerosis / coronary heart disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Coronary heart disease is a chronic disease in which plaque (a waxy substance) builds up over many years inside the coronary arteries that supply oxygen-rich blood to the heart. Symptoms: chest pain or pain radiating into the arm, neck, jaw, or back (may feel like indigestion), shortness of breath, sleep issues, and fatigue; pain can be triggered by stress and worsens with activity — any chest pain warrants an immediate ER visit. Once an acute event has passed, preventing another is accomplished with lifestyle changes.","Labs: micronutrient testing, carotid doppler, stress testing, coronary artery calcium scoring, and carotid intima-media thickness testing (especially once past age fifty, or if smoking, high cholesterol, or a family history of vascular disease) — plus Endo-PAT testing for those with known or suspected disease. Advanced lipid testing breaks lipoprotein particles down by size/subclass (small, dense, oxidized particles are the ones that squeeze through vessel-wall cracks and seed plaque) and also flags oxidative stress, endothelial dysfunction, and inflammation markers. A comprehensive nutrition panel (e.g., ION panel) evaluates amino acids, vitamins, minerals, antioxidants, fats, urinary organic acids (how effectively the body turns food into energy), and heavy metals — correcting deficiencies found here, alongside lifestyle change, can meaningfully lower cardiovascular risk. A lipoprotein(a) test helps detect early cardiovascular disease, especially alongside an abnormal calcium score or thickened carotid artery — always look at the whole picture rather than any single value.","Supplements: fish oil (1 tablespoon daily). B vitamins — B1, B3/niacin (500 mg two to three times daily), B6 (50 mg/day), B9, B12. Taurine (3 g twice daily). CoQ10 (100–300 mg daily) — an antioxidant that protects the heart and vessels from free-radical damage and helps stabilize blood sugar. Garlic/Allium sativum (1,200 mg/day) — antioxidant and natural blood-thinning properties; reduces cholesterol and homocysteine. Curcumin (500–1,000 mg/day). Magnesium (500 mg daily) for circulation and blood pressure. Carotenoids. Vitamin C (250–500 mg twice daily) — avoid supplemental doses over 300 mg within 24 hours of lab testing, as it can interfere with results. Vitamins D, E, and K. Carnitine tartrate (500–1,000 mg twice daily, up to 3 g twice daily) to produce ATP and power the heart. Others: quercetin (250–1,000 mg/day), choline (do not exceed 3,500 mg/day; pair with B vitamins, particularly pantothenic acid), grapeseed extract (300 mg/day), L-citrulline (500 mg/day), sytrinol (150 mg/day), ALA (300 mg twice daily), PQQ (20 mg/day), pterostilbene (50 mg/day), ribose (5 g two to three times daily — mitochondrial energy production), NAC (600 mg/day), resveratrol (250 mg/day), alpha-lipoic acid, L-theanine, green tea extract, ashwagandha, berberine (500–1,000 mg/day), selenium, red yeast rice (1,200–2,400 mg daily — lowers LDL and reduces heart-related deaths), and hawthorn (500–900 mg daily — improves circulation to the heart and lowers blood pressure). Glucomannan capsules can help bind toxins. Branched-chain amino acids (leucine, valine, isoleucine in a 4:1:1 ratio; 5,000 mg/day), phosphatidylserine (300–600 mg twice daily).","Plaque stabilization and reversal/reduction: vitamin K2 — menaquinone-7 (100–500 mcg/day) and MK-4 (1,000 mcg/day) — omega-3 (5 g/day), aged garlic extract (600–1,200 mg twice daily), curcumin (1,000–5,000 mg/day), quercetin (500 mg twice daily), vitamin C (1,000 mg twice daily).","Homeopathy: see a licensed homeopath for a constitutional remedy; the following are for temporary/emergency use only. If experiencing heart-attack symptoms, call 911 first — while waiting for the ambulance, a 30C potency can be taken every two to three minutes. Aconitum napellus for numbness and pain in the left arm with intense fear and anxiety. Arnica for a squeezing or bruising sensation in the heart. Cactus for angina, especially with the sensation of a band tightening around the chest.","Acupressure: H3, H4, H7 (stimulates circulation); GV24.5 (produces a sense of calm); CV17 (reduces sadness/anxiety); P3 (eases pain); P6 (improves anxiety-related palpitations); SP4; B15. For chest pain: HP4, HP7, HP6, K27, CV14, CV17.","Aromatherapy: essential oils help inflammation and reduce oxidative stress — ylang ylang, lemon, frankincense, lemongrass, ginger, and helichrysum. For poor circulation, a stimulating massage blend of almond oil with thyme, black pepper, ginger, rosemary, and clove essential oils."],"conditions":["Atherosclerosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_hyperlipidemia","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Hyperlipidemia — the Holistic Rx","body":["One in three Americans has elevated cholesterol. Statins block HMG-CoA reductase, the liver enzyme that drives cholesterol production, thereby lowering blood cholesterol — but among people who take a statin for five years, only about one in eighty-three lives is saved, one in one hundred develops diabetes, one in ten suffers muscular damage, and roughly 18 percent develop undesirable side effects (liver dysfunction, memory loss, increased cancer risk, anemia, sexual dysfunction). Statins also block CoQ10, an antioxidant enzyme central to cellular energy production, heart function, and blood-glucose regulation. Some people do benefit from a statin — always discuss risks versus benefits with your doctor, as alternatives exist depending on your situation.","Supplements: fish oil (1–2 g EPA/DHA per day) to reduce cholesterol and triglycerides. CoQ10 (200–300 mg daily), especially if on lipid-lowering medication. Red yeast rice (start 600 mg twice daily, up to 1,200 mg twice daily at bedtime; take 25–100 mg CoQ10 alongside to prevent deficiency; look for a citrinin-free product). Niacin (1,500–3,000 mg/day if diabetic — high doses can raise blood sugar). B5/pantothenic acid. Chromium. Choline. Phytosterols/plant sterols and stanols (2,000 mg/day). Others: garlic (500 mg/day) lowers cholesterol and raises HDL; berberine (500 mg two to three times daily); guggul (1,500 mg/day standardized to 5% guggulsterones, equivalent to 75 mg guggulsterones — raises HDL, lowers cholesterol); glucomannan (two to four capsules fifteen minutes before meals with a glass of water); artichoke extract (500 mg three times daily); acetyl-L-carnitine (1,000 mg daily) for high triglycerides.","Children: fish oil (EPA/DHA 1–2 g daily), pycnogenol (50–100 mg daily), vitamin B3 (5–20 mg daily), decaf green tea (4–6 cups daily), psyllium (6 g daily), plant sterols (2–2.5 g daily).","Homeopathy: Allium sativum for excessive meat-eaters with belching, burning, and increased appetite. Aurum metallicum for a rapid pulse, depression, or anger, with pain behind the sternum especially at night. Baryta muriaticum for elderly patients with vertigo and heaviness. Nux vomica for those who crave alcohol and fatty foods. Calcarea carbonica for those who are overweight, cold-sensitive, with continuous scalp perspiration and tight, palpitating chest. Lycopodium for a desire for sweets with flatulence and other digestive symptoms.","Acupressure: Liver point, LI11, LI4, and LV3.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can lower cholesterol by decreasing emotional stress and improving circulation, with cardio-supportive and antioxidant properties — lavender, cypress, and rosemary oil support overall heart health."],"conditions":["Hyperlipidemia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_cardiology_hypertension","chapter":"cardiology","title":"Hypertension — the Holistic Rx","body":["Hypertension (high blood pressure) reflects resistance to blood flow in the arteries and the volume of blood the heart pumps. Most people are asymptomatic, though some have headaches, nosebleeds, or shortness of breath with severe hypertension. Diagnosis is made by history and office blood-pressure measurement.","Supplements: if already on BP medication, talk to your doctor first — these can lower pressure further and combining can be dangerous. Omega-3 fatty acids (1,000–2,000 mg daily EPA/DHA) reduce blood pressure with long-term use. Magnesium (500 mg daily) relaxes smooth muscle. CoQ10 (100–300 mg daily) reduces blood pressure and supports heart health — if on warfarin, monitor levels, as it can lower them (GI side effects can occur). Garlic (600 mg aged extract). Others: vitamin C (600 mg daily) induces sodium diuresis; L-arginine (4–8 g/day) or L-citrulline (2–4 g/day); hawthorn/Crataegus oxyacantha (250 mg standardized extract three times daily) dilates artery walls and lowers blood pressure; hibiscus tea (1.25 g steeped in 240 mL water, three times daily). If on diuretics: calcium (750 mg), potassium (100 mg), vitamin B1 (300 mg), P5P/B6 (50 mg), zinc (25 mg), magnesium glycinate (240 mg/day). If on a beta blocker: CoQ10 (start 200–300 mg/day) and melatonin (6 mg at bedtime).","Children: fish oil (EPA/DHA 1–2 g daily), magnesium (200–400 mg daily), arginine (1–2 g daily).","Homeopathy (hypertension and atherosclerosis): Arsenicum album for those who are pale, thin, and cold-sensitive with alternating weakness and vascular lesions; also (15C) for lipid disorders. Arsenicum iodatum to help prevent arterial sclerosis, preventive or curative (five pellets daily to once weekly). Baryta carbonica for elderly patients with high blood pressure, memory disorders, headaches, fatigue, palpitations, and possible aneurysm. Sulphur for hyperphagic, congestive presentations. Thuja occidentalis for truncal obesity. Natrum sulphuricum 15C for heaviness of trunk/shoulders/hips with a tendency to joint pain, bronchitis, and frequent diarrhea. Natrum muriaticum for high blood pressure after emotional upset, salt cravings, intense thirst, sun intolerance, desire to be alone, headaches, palpitations, and insomnia. Phosphorus for labile hypertension with salt cravings, nervousness, and frequent fainting spells. Vanadium for frequent fainting, dizziness, a sense of chest compression, confusion, and mental deterioration. Glonoinum for a pounding arterial sensation with tachycardia, congestion, a tight headache, flushed face, and throbbing carotid pain worse with heat, sun, or alcohol. Arnica to limit hemorrhage/edema and prevent thrombosis via its action on capillaries. Arsenicum metallicum (9C daily) for anxious, depressive patients with hypertensive vascular erethism, palpitations, extrasystoles, and hot flashes that improve with hot air. Aconitum for acute hypertensive attacks with a congested face, tense pulse, severe headache, palpitations, anxiety, and agitation. Argentum nitricum for stress-triggered symptoms with cravings for salt and sweets. Belladonna for a flushed face, pounding headaches, and immediate heat with cold hands/feet and light sensitivity. Lachesis for suspicious, jealous, talkative patients who feel warm and congested, are intolerant of anything touching the neck, bruise easily, and are prone to hypertension around menopause that improves with bleeding (menses, nosebleed). Nux vomica for stress-driven hypertension with irritability, impatience, stimulant/alcohol cravings, overeating, digestive issues, chilliness, and constipation, improving after a short nap. Ignatia for sudden BP spikes with hypersensitivity to worry and frustration. Calcarea fluorica for early-onset atherosclerosis with ligament hyperlaxity. Lycopodium for heart disease with cholesterol, liver, uric-acid, or kidney issues. Plumbum metallicum for renal insufficiency and thin arteries, especially in the elderly. Silicea for thin patients prone to osteoporosis and chronic suppurations. Calcarea carbonica (15C) for familial obesity with a tendency to diabetes, polyps, and kidney/gallbladder stones.","Acupressure: ST36, H7, LI11, P6, LV2 (acute stage), LV3 (during remission); GB20, K3, B38, CV17 (chest tension with stress); GV20, LI4, K1, GB34.","Aromatherapy: essential oils decrease emotional and oxidative stress while dilating arteries — lavender, ylang-ylang, frankincense, geranium, mandarin, marjoram, eucalyptus citriodora, yarrow, and clary sage calm the body and lower blood pressure. A juniper oil massage helps break down fatty deposits in the body and arteries. For atherosclerosis specifically: lemon, ginger, black pepper, basil, linalool, rosemary, cineole, and juniper."],"conditions":["Hypertension"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_general_dd","chapter":"dental","title":"Dental health — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Healing the gut, keeping blood sugar regulated, and creating an alkaline environment is key in healing tooth decay and getting you on the path to remineralization. Four things can contribute to tooth decay: lack of minerals in the diet, like magnesium, calcium, and phosphorus; lack of fat-soluble vitamins like D, A, E, and K; too much sugar consumption; and too much phytic-acid-rich foods. Limit foods high in phytic acid — grains, nuts, seeds, and legumes — especially when starting out the process of remineralization, and incorporate soups with gut-healing bone broth daily.","Eat high amounts of fat-soluble vitamins and healthy fats from fermented cod liver oil, grass-fed organic raw dairy (if tolerated, eat butter), seafood, and coconut oil. Focus on bone broths and organ and gland meats like bone marrow and liver to help boost the endocrine system and balance hormones. Monitor sweet intake, including maple syrup, fruit, and honey. Activator X — important for tooth mineralization and bright, healthy teeth — is found primarily in grass-fed butter and raw milk; aim for 1–1½ tablespoons per day of grass-fed butter if tolerated. Other foods to incorporate for healthy teeth: zucchini, string beans, kale, chard, seaweed, vegetable juice, and fermented foods. Cranberry juice has anti-adhesion properties and can decrease bad bacteria in the mouth.","Add a remineralizing or special probiotic toothpaste and practice oil pulling to support tooth and gum health. To help prevent gum disease, aim the toothbrush at the gum line while brushing. Charcoal can help whiten teeth. To further optimize oral health, use colloidal silver (swish and swallow, one teaspoon, three times per day for sixty seconds) and spray xylitol on the gums three times per day.","The Four Big S's: lowering stress and getting enough sleep have a large impact on oral health — they help stabilize hormones and optimize calcium and other nutrient levels in the body. Practicing gratitude daily will keep you smiling."],"conditions":["Gingivitis","Tooth decay"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_tooth_decay","chapter":"dental","title":"Tooth decay — the Holistic Rx (supplements and homeopathy)","body":["Supplements: fermented cod liver oil (½–1½ tsp/day total), gelatin, B vitamins like B6, B9, and B12, CoQ10, oral probiotics, calcium, magnesium, vitamin C, vitamin D, vitamin K, omega-3, and zinc. For added fat-soluble nutrients, add skate liver oil (⅛–¼ tsp, two to three times per day), or a butter-and-fermented-cod-liver-oil mixture. Children's dosing (butter/cod liver oil mixture): 25 lbs — ¼ tsp/day; 35 lbs — ⅓ tsp/day; 45 lbs — ½ tsp/day; 55 lbs — ⅔ tsp/day.","Homeopathy for tooth decay: Mercurius for decay that is black and loose with bleeding gums and excessive salivation. Silicea for multiple decayed teeth, especially in thin children with a large head. Calcarea for those who are overweight, prone to head sweats, with decay in many teeth. Kreosotum for black tooth decay with foul-smelling breath and constipation. Sulphur for tooth decay with sensitivity to touch; teeth are black and hurt after food or drink."],"conditions":["Tooth decay"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_toothache_homeopathy","chapter":"dental","title":"Toothache — the Holistic Rx (homeopathy)","body":["Plantago for tooth pain that improves with eating and worsens with cold air or pressure; mouth full of saliva, pain feels nervy. Coffea for pain improved with cold and worse with heat. Chamomilla for unbearable pain with a sore head, worsened with food, drink, coffee, at night, and by cold air.","Mercurius for tooth pain that shoots up to the ears, with loose teeth, tender gums that bleed easily, bad breath, and marked thirst. Staphisagria for tearing toothache with red cheeks and a disintegrating black tooth, worse with pressure, food, and cold air. Pulsatilla for tooth pain improved in open air and cold water, worsened with hot food or drink, in a person who isn't thirsty.","Arnica for pain after a filling or an extraction. Belladonna for a developing gum boil that leads to throbbing pain and a dry mouth. Apis for burning, stinging toothache with swollen, tight-feeling gums.","Bryonia for teeth that feel too long, with pain improved by lying on the painful side and by pressure, worse with hot food, drink, and movement. Calcarea for toothache worse with food, cold drinks, or during pregnancy. Magnesia phosphorica for electric-shock-type pain that is soothed by warmth."],"conditions":["Toothache"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_dental_acupressure_aromatherapy","chapter":"dental","title":"Dental health — the Holistic Rx (acupressure and aromatherapy)","body":["Acupressure: LI4, ST6, ST7, ST36, TW13, ST44, K3, SJ2, SJ5, GV14, SI18. Lower jaw: ST6, ST5, TB17. Molars: TB23, GB2. Distal points: LI1, LI4, ST40, B60.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can reduce gum inflammation — myrrh and clove have a numbing effect that helps relieve pain and soreness. Oregano is antibacterial and can reduce bad breath. German chamomile and lemon can also be used for a toothache. Others good for mouth and gum care are cistus, cardamom, fennel, tea tree, peppermint, and nutmeg. Add peppermint oil along with tea tree oil to your toothbrush for natural whitening."],"conditions":["Gingivitis","Tooth decay","Toothache"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_derm_acne","chapter":"dermatology","title":"Acne — the Holistic Rx","body":["Pores are open to allow hair growth and to secrete sebum (oil) from the sebaceous glands. When pores get blocked, excess oil builds up and triggers rapid growth of Propionibacterium acnes — the more bacteria present, the more breakouts occur — and the resulting inflammation forms a pimple. Pores can be blocked by dead skin cells, overactive oil glands, P. acnes, and inflammation, leading to whiteheads, blackheads, and cysts. Elevated blood sugar increases acute inflammation and the inflammatory response behind breakouts, so regulating blood sugar and healing the gut are important steps in managing acne. Diagnosis is made via history and exam.","Supplements: Zinc (50 mg twice daily with meals for three months, then can reduce to 50 mg daily); can take copper (3–5 mg) along with the zinc, since zinc sulfate isn't easily absorbed. Nicotinamide combo (750 mg nicotinamide, zinc 25 mg, copper 1.5 mg, folic acid 500 mcg per day, plus daily vitamin B6) for four to eight weeks can reduce pimples. Tea tree oil (5% solution diluted in water, dabbed onto blemishes twice daily) can act similarly to benzoyl peroxide. Vitex (chasteberry) (160 mg of a standardized Vitex extract) reduces acne formation. Others: vitamin A (2,000–4,000 IU daily) reduces oil and keratin production — going over the upper limit can be a problem, and it can be dangerous for pregnant women; prenatal vitamins should not contain more than 3,000 IU of preformed vitamin A. Burdock root (Arctium lappa) (300–500 mg capsule, or thirty drops of tincture, or 1 cup of tea, three times daily). Topical steam treatment: pour boiling water over strawberry leaves, eucalyptus, thyme, and wintergreen; place a towel over the head and lean over the bowl for ten to fifteen minutes. Natural clay mask: bentonite clay or Fuller's earth mixed with rose water into a paste, left on for 10 minutes. Children: vitamin A (1,000–5,000 IU daily), B3 (4% niacinamide wash applied topically daily), zinc (10 mg daily).","Homeopathy: Antimonium crudum helps acne associated with digestive disorders. Calcarea sulphurica can help chronic or cystic acne presenting with a yellowish discharge. Hepar sulphuris calcareum helps acne filled with pus that is painful to touch and improves with warmth. Ledum palustre helps pustular acne on the nose and cheeks that feels better with cold. Pulsatilla is for acne associated with hormonal changes. Silicea is for chronic white pustules. Selenium is best for acne accompanied by hyperseborrhea and hair loss. Sulphur is for inflamed, sore, red acne worsened by heat, in a patient who is also impatient, irritable, and moody — washing or heat can aggravate symptoms. Nux vomica is indicated for acne aggravated by excessive medications, stimulants, or rich food. Kali bromatum is for teenagers who are easily depressed and anxious, with acne and anxiety shown through nonstop movement. Eugenia jambosa is for acne with a small white suppuration spot at its center.","Acupressure: LI4, LU5, CV9. Others: GB14, SP10, B23, B47, ST2, B10.","Aromatherapy: Lavender oil, geranium, and bergamot (an astringent and antidepressant) can help improve inflammation. Once the skin improves, apply a lotion made with diluted water, lavender, and orange blossom to reduce scarring. Others that can help: clove, geranium, lavender, and tea tree oils. Clary sage, juniper, lemongrass, mandarin, and lemon can help with blackheads."],"conditions":["Acne"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_derm_eczema","chapter":"dermatology","title":"Eczema — the Holistic Rx","body":["Eczema is a chronic skin condition where an itchy, red rash appears over the body. Symptoms include dry, red, inflamed, sensitive skin, intense itching, recurring rash, scaly areas, and rough, leathery patches that can be accompanied by oozing or crusting. Diagnosis is made via history and physical exam.","Supplements: Essential fatty acids (1–2 g EPA/DHA daily, or 2 tbsp of flaxseed oil daily). Evening primrose oil (3 g daily) or borage oil contains GLA and can be used orally or topically. Vitamin E (400 IU) helps the body use fatty acids and promotes wound healing. Burdock root (300 mg capsule or 1 mL of tincture) helps cleanse the skin; vitamin C with bioflavonoids (1–3 g daily) improves the immune system; natural creams with chamomile; licorice root gel 2% (to reduce inflammation and itching); zinc (50 mg three times daily for six weeks); copper (2 mg daily); black cumin seed oil (20–40 mg daily per pound of body weight), taken orally or mixed into lotion for the skin; neem oil; quercetin (500–1,000 mg three to four times daily) as an anti-inflammatory; and turmeric, which is also anti-inflammatory. Improve wound healing with B5, vitamin C, zinc, and fatty acids. Children: fish oil (EPA/DHA 1,000–4,000 mg daily), probiotics, vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU per day), zinc (10–20 mg daily).","Homeopathy by phase: erythematous phase — apis, urticaria urens, belladonna. Vesicular phase — rhus toxicodendron, rhus vernix or rhus venenata, mezereum, cantharis, anagallis arvensis, croton tiglium. Exudative phase — graphites, mezereum, petroleum, antimonium crudum, viola tricolor. Desquamative phase — arsenicum album, arsenicum iodatum, natrum sulphuricum, berberis vulgaris, hydrocotyle asiatica. Fissure phase — nitricum acidum, graphites, antimonium crudum, petroleum, sepia.","Homeopathy for itching and individual presentations: staphysagria (itching on eyelids and genitals), cistus canadensis (red, oozing, scabby lesions on cheeks and backs of hands), manganum aceticum (itching of joint creases with scales or fissures), radium bromatum (burning, itching thick skin), dolichos pruriens (itching with constipation), chamomilla (itching that causes behavioral disturbance). Arsenicum album is for tender, dry, itchy, swollen skin eruptions, worse in winter, with itching intense between midnight and 2:00 a.m.; the patient feels restless. Alumina is for dry, itchy skin in a constipated person. Calendula soothes affected inflamed areas topically. Calcarea carbonica helps eczema that's worse in winter, with clammy hands and feet and cravings for sweets and eggs — a great remedy for cradle cap. Graphites is for eczema mainly affecting the palms and areas behind the ears, with dry, thick, honey-like discharge, and itching intensified by a warm bed. Hepar sulph is for skin that is easily infected and sensitive, worse in the cold. Medorrhinum helps those who've had eczema since very young — they tend to be warm, sweat easily, and crave ice and oranges. Mezereum is for blistering eczema that oozes and crusts, feeling better in open air and cold. Petroleum is for dry, cracked skin on the palms and hands, itching worse at night and with warmth of the bed. Psorinum is for eczema that looks dirty, is irritated, and is prone to infection, scratched until it bleeds, with general chilliness. Rhus toxicodendron is for itchy, blistering eczema that improves with warmth and movement, craving cold milk, itching worse at night or in damp weather. Sulphur helps when skin is red, rough, itchy, and dry, with early-morning diarrhea, aggravated by heat and washing.","Acupressure: LI11 (helps with itching), B40, SP10, B23, B47, ST36, B10, LI4, LU9, B13. Weeping eczema: SP9, B52, CV9. Severe itching: LU7, SP10, LV5, B13.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can help soothe inflammation and itching, including juniper, peppermint, bergamot, carrot, jojoba, lavender, frankincense, thyme, benzoin, sweet marjoram, geranium, and chamomile.","Other Recommendations: biofeedback and climatotherapy (using water and sunlight as therapy) can also be helpful."],"conditions":["Eczema"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_derm_alopecia","chapter":"dermatology","title":"Alopecia (Hair Loss) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Hair loss, or alopecia, can occur at any age and in any gender and can be due to multiple causes, including medication side effects, hormonal issues, and nutritional deficiencies (e.g., ferritin). Diagnosis can be made with a detailed history, examination, and testing, including biopsy.","Supplements: Saw palmetto (320 mg daily) improves hair growth. Pygeum (100 mg twice daily) slows hair loss and improves prostate health. Pumpkin seed oil (8 g, or 1 tbsp, daily). Aloe vera juice (1/2 cup twice daily). B vitamins like biotin (2,000–3,000 mcg daily) and pantothenic acid (100 mg per day). Others: silicon (orthosilicic acid); MSM (1,000–3,000 mg daily), which promotes hair development; bamboo extract (900 mg twice daily). Children (to stimulate hair growth): vitamin C (500–1,000 mg daily), vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU daily), L-carnitine (500–1,000 mg daily), zinc (20 mg daily).","Homeopathy: alopecia areata — eberthinum, paratyphoidinum. Postpartum — eberthinum. Totalis — Thallium metallicum. Seborrheic — Selenium metallicum. Arsenicum album is helpful when hair loss results from stress, with the person very fearful and restless. Aurum muriaticum helps hair loss associated with headaches and boils on the head, symptoms worse at night, with a high likelihood of depression. Fluoric acidum helps brittle hair that falls out in little tufts. Selenium helps painful, sensitive skin with loss of body hair as well. Arnica is for hair loss associated with severe injury. Kali carbonicum helps hair loss associated with dryness of hair and scalp. Baryta carb helps hair loss in an elderly, mentally slow person with poor circulation. Ignatia amara is for hair loss that comes on with acute grief or emotional trauma. Lycopodium clavatum is for hair loss after childbirth and for premature balding and graying. Natrum muriaticum is for hair loss with dandruff and white crusts on the scalp, with depression and salt cravings. Phosphoric acidum is for hair loss after extreme emotion and grief, with a fatigued, indifferent patient. Sepia is for hair loss associated with hormonal changes, like after childbirth or at menopause. Silica (Silicea) is helpful when hair loss accompanies a chronic illness — the hair is brittle and the person tends to be thin, chilly, and easily fatigued. Phosphorus helps with hair that falls out in handfuls.","Acupressure: Baihui point (improves blood circulation), hollow point, ST36.","Aromatherapy: rosemary, lavender, clary sage, and cypress can stimulate hair growth; jasmine, rose, and geranium have relaxing properties. These can be added to homemade shampoos."],"conditions":["Alopecia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_derm_psoriasis","chapter":"dermatology","title":"Psoriasis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Psoriasis is an autoimmune condition of the skin characterized by thick plaques. Diagnosis is via history, examination, and possible biopsy.","Supplements: Digestive enzymes (one to two capsules with each meal) help digest effectively. Hydrochloric acid (one to three capsules with each meal, decreasing the dose if a burning sensation occurs) helps with protein digestion. Optimize vitamin D and B12. Others: milk thistle (70–210 mg three times per day), sarsaparilla (250–500 mg three times per day) to reduce bacterial toxin effects; a good multivitamin with 25,000 IU of vitamin A (a critical nutrient for healthy skin), 400 IU of vitamin E, 800 mcg folic acid, 200 mcg selenium, 200 mcg chromium, 25–50 mg zinc, and other antioxidants; burdock root, cleavers, barberry, calendula, and oil of anise. Topical aloe vera (0.5%) cream has been effective for psoriasis. Creams that help contain avocado oil, B12, and Oregon grape (10%) cream. Others to try: creams with vitamin E, vitamin A, zinc, sarsaparilla, and goldenseal. Psoriatic arthritis: evening primrose, borage, fish oil, curcumin, bromelain, and quercetin. Children: fish oil (1–2 g EPA/DHA per day), vitamin A (1,000–4,000 IU daily), vitamin D (0.005% applied topically three times a day), aloe vera gel (three times daily), Oregon grape root (10% cream applied three times daily).","Homeopathy: Arsenicum iodatum helps dry, scaly, itchy skin that leaves large scales when exfoliated, leaving a raw, exuding surface, with a weak, exhausted patient who sweats profusely. Kali sulphuricum is indicated for scaly dermatosis. Arsenicum album helps restless, anxious patients with dry, itchy, scaly plaques that feel like burning skin, improving with warm applications. Belladonna can treat rectal psoriasis. Calcarea carbonica is for dry, scaly plaques that often open up, in a person who is fatigued, overwhelmed, anxious, overweight, and chilly, craving eggs, dairy, and sweets. Graphites may help psoriasis affecting the skin behind the ears, backs of the hands, scalp/head, and genitals, with honey-colored oozing pus, worse at night. Kali arsenicosum helps extremely scaly lesions aggravated by warmth. Petroleum is for a condition aggravated by cold, worse in winter, with dry, itchy skin worsened by the warmth of the bed. Mercurius solubilis helps those with swollen lymph nodes and greasy-looking skin that becomes easily infected, in introverted people with strong emotions who are sensitive to temperature changes. Mezereum is for fine, white, scaly psoriasis covering large areas, with crusting and itching that feels better with cold. Rhus toxicodendron helps psoriasis that is raw, itchy, red, dry, and chapped, improving with heat or baths, in a person who craves milk and is restless. Sepia is one of the most common remedies for psoriasis, with thickening of the skin, circular eruptions, and dryness, often on the elbow — recommended for women with hormonal imbalance who feel chilly and irritable. Staphysagria helps when psoriasis affects the scalp and erupts after suppressed grief or emotions. Sulphur helps dry, scaly, red, itchy/burning patches that are often moist and oozing, worse after baths and from warmth — used for psoriatic arthritis and when the person often feels too hot.","Acupressure: GB20, LI4, LV3, ST36, and B10.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can bring relief to inflamed skin and support healing, including lavender oil (especially for stress and inflammation), frankincense, tea tree, myrrh, geranium, and carrot seed oil (scalp psoriasis). Coconut oil can be used as the base oil — always check a small test patch first to make sure you aren't reacting to a specific oil. Juniper baths may also be helpful."],"conditions":["Psoriasis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_derm_rosacea","chapter":"dermatology","title":"Rosacea — the Holistic Rx","body":["Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory condition of the skin on the cheeks, chin, eyelids, nose, or forehead that becomes inflamed and red. There are often small red bumps or pustules on the affected areas, small spider-like blood vessels on the face, a red nose, and a tendency to blush or flush easily; over time it can give the nose a swollen, waxy look. Common triggers are emotional stress, hot or cold weather, sunshine, spicy food, MSG, vinegar, cinnamon oil, alcohol, and occasionally some skincare products — even links to Candida have been noted. High doses of B6 and B12 can trigger rosacea. Diagnosis can be made via history and exam.","Supplements: betaine hydrochloride (one to three capsules with each meal, reducing the dose if burning occurs) improves digestion. Licorice helps reduce inflammation and soothe irritation. Feverfew helps reduce redness and dryness — choose a parthenolide-free extract. Nicotinamide combo (750 mg, zinc 25 mg, copper 1.5 mg, folic acid 500 mcg) helps improve symptoms after four to eight weeks. Zinc (60–70 mg per day for no more than three months, then 15–20 mg per day). Others: azelaic acid topical cream (3–10%), gentian root (300 mg, or ten to twenty drops, five to fifteen minutes before meals to improve digestion), burdock root (300 mg or 3 mL three times per day) improves detoxification and hormone balance; natural progesterone has anti-inflammatory benefits and improves skin conditions; B vitamin (50 mg twice daily), especially B12 (400–800 mcg sublingually daily); pancreatin (350–500 mg before meals); and brewer's or nutritional yeast (1½ tsp daily). Turmeric, ginger, aloe vera gel, and raw honey applied to the skin have also been shown to improve symptoms.","Homeopathy: Arsenicum album helps anxious people who get cold easily, with lesions that are hot, dry, and flaky, and who prefer warm drinks. Pulsatilla is for rosacea that feels better in fresh air and worse in warm rooms, with symptoms associated with hormonal changes like puberty or menopause, in individuals who are weepy, sensitive, and crave sweets. Sepia is for rosacea associated with hormonal changes in a woman who is fatigued and irritable, craves salty, sour, and sweet foods, and has a tendency toward depression. Sulphur helps chronic redness and inflammation aggravated by heat, sun, and warmer climates, in a person who prefers coolness. Carbo animalis is for facial lesions of rosacea. Arnica montana is a remedy for papulo-pustules. Sanguinaria canadensis helps rosacea with burning sensations and itching, hot flushes with redness of the cheeks, aggravated by heat. Eugenia jambosa is indicated for rosacea common in alcoholics, who often have cramps in the feet. Calcarea fluorica helps those with severe telangiectasias. Lachesis mutus is prescribed for rhinophyma, especially when symptoms are aggravated by hormonal changes. Thuja occidentalis helps those with rosacea accompanied by varicose veins on the cheeks.","Acupressure: the Yin Tang point, LI20, SI18, ST4, LI4, ST36, SP10, ST3, B10 (helps with anxiety and stress).","Aromatherapy: tea tree oil, eucalyptus, geranium, chamomile, rose, rosemary, thyme, and lavender essential oil can help relieve skin inflammation — try applying with a cool compress."],"conditions":["Rosacea"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ent_general_dd","chapter":"ent","title":"Ear, Nose, and Throat — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Lowering overall inflammation allows the body to heal and improves ear, nose, and throat symptoms. Garlic and onions boost immune function (high in vitamin C, speed recovery, improve circulation); other anti-inflammatory foods include flax, chia, and cold-pressed oils. Apple cider vinegar (1 tsp mixed with water and a little lemon juice) can lower inflammation at the first sign of an allergy attack. Nutritional deficiencies can cause dizziness, so optimize green leafy vegetables for B vitamins; caffeine and alcohol worsen immune function and dizziness.","If you have a tree pollen allergy, consider limiting apples, peaches, pears, kiwi, plums, and nectarines, plus carrots, fennel, green peppers, and celery, along with almonds, walnuts, hazelnuts, and spices like basil, paprika, and coriander. If you have a grass and weed pollen allergy, avoid melons, bananas, cucumbers, echinacea, chamomile, parsley, paprika, dill, oregano, tarragon, and pepper.","Detoxing your environment helps you avoid allergens: a room humidifier with warm mist during dry conditions is beneficial (especially with a forced-hot-air furnace); clean air ducts every two to three years, and change the furnace filter monthly during winter; use negative ions or an air cleaner, especially with moldy air and air-conditioning; clean carpets regularly with a HEPA or water-filter vacuum cleaner and environmentally friendly cleaning agents. Avoid the outdoors when pollen and pollution are high (cold temperatures minimize pollen); check the local pollen count (pollen.com); pollen is higher on sunny, dry, windy days and mornings, lower on windless, cool, moist days. Wear sunglasses, keep windows and doors closed, wash bed linens in hot water weekly, follow a no-shoes-indoors policy, keep dirty clothes in a covered hamper, and trim pet fur. Avoid fans that draw in outside air; use air-conditioning. For year-round allergies, use blinds instead of curtains, dust-mite covers on pillows, and washable area rugs instead of wall-to-wall carpet on hardwood floors. Follow the water/air/living-environment/kitchen/body detox recommendations from chapter 2.","Flushing pollen from the nasal passages eases sinus pressure, congestion, and inflammation: use a neti pot in the morning and before bed with 8 oz of warm water (never tap water) mixed with 1/4 tsp of salt, or premixed saline.","The Four Big S's — special consideration: stress worsens inflammation, so managing stress is key to healing. Release negative emotions with energy-medicine modalities such as Reiki, qigong, craniosacral therapy, and therapeutic touch."],"conditions":["Allergic rhinitis","Sinusitis","Vertigo","Tinnitus"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ent_allergic_rhinitis","chapter":"ent","title":"Allergic rhinitis — the Holistic Rx","body":["In allergic rhinitis (hay fever), the body overreacts to an inhaled substance by making antibodies and releasing IgE and other inflammatory chemicals (like histamines) into the airways. Severity ranges from minor irritation to anaphylaxis; it can be seasonal or perennial (year-round). Symptoms: sneezing, runny nose, itching of the nose/roof of mouth/eyes, swollen eyelids, post-nasal drip, facial pain or pressure, fatigue, dark circles under the eyes, headaches, and loss of hearing, taste, or smell. Diagnosis is by history and physical; RAST blood testing or a scratch test can identify specific allergens, and blood eosinophils can be elevated. Causes are environmental: pollen, mold, smoke, dust mites.","Supplements: raw honey (2 tbsp/day). Stinging nettle (300–500 mg twice daily, herbal or eaten as a green) has antihistamine activity. Butterbur (500 mg/day) reduces mucus. Quercetin (1,000 mg/day). Bromelain (250 mg/day) decreases sinus and nasal swelling. Vitamin C (2,000 mg/day) is an antihistamine and antioxidant. Soil-based probiotics. Others: spirulina (1,000–2,000 mg/day) stops histamine release; Tinospora cordifolia (300 mg three times/day — avoid with autoimmunity, pregnancy, or nursing); Epicor (dried Saccharomyces cerevisiae fermentate, 500 mg/day); astragalus (160 mg twice daily — avoid with autoimmune conditions, lithium, or other immune-suppressing meds); MSM (3,000–5,000 mg/day) reduces allergic/inflammatory responses; eyebright (Euphrasia officinalis, one capsule three times daily, or as a solution — five drops of the tincture in a half-ounce of saline applied to irritated eyes twice daily).","Children's dosing — allergic rhinitis: bromelain (80–320 mg/day), probiotics (2–10 billion CFU/day), mixed carotenoids (1,000–5,000 IU/day), quercetin (200–400 mg divided into two to three doses/day), mixed tocopherols (100–400 IU/day), vitamin C (500–1,000 mg/day), stinging nettle leaf (100–500 mg/day). General allergy: bromelain (200–500 mg/day), flavonoids (200–400 mg divided into two to three daily doses), quercetin (250–500 mg divided into two to three doses/day), vitamin C (500–1,000 mg/day), euphrasia (50–200 mg/day), skullcap (250–500 mg/day), stinging nettle leaf (250–500 mg/day), turmeric (100 mg/day).","Homeopathy: Allium cepa for frequent sneezing, tearing eyes, and irritating burning nasal discharge, with strong thirst and worse from warm food/drink; light hurts the eyes. Nux vomica for dry, ticklish, scraping sensations with watery discharge, stuffiness, and sneezing on waking, in someone impatient and irritable, light-sensitive, itchy ears, obstructed breathing, craving tobacco/sweets/stimulants. Euphrasia for thick, burning, irritating tears with stinging nasal discharge worse lying down and indoors. Arsenicum album for restlessness, fatigue, anxiety, stuffiness, violent sneezing, and burning copious discharge. Sabadilla for violent sneezing, watery eyes, runny nose, sore throat soothed by warm drinks, headache like the head is shrinking, and feeling cold. Arsenicum iodatum for thick honey-colored nasal discharge followed by three to four days of sneezing and burning inside the nose, plus burning throat, dry skin, anxiety, and irritating cough, worse with warmth. Arundo for itchy nose/ears/roof of mouth with sneezing but no discharge, useful early in hay fever season. Psorinum for feeling cold, bland or burning discharge, wanting to lie down, and breathlessness relieved by raising the arms. Gelsemium for apathy, dizziness, shakiness, nonstop watering and sneezing with puffy heavy eyes. Pulsatilla for tearfulness with yellow eye/nose discharge, nighttime congestion better in open air, worse in a warm room. Silicea for feeling cold, a stuffed nose especially on waking, and tender sinuses, with allergies manifesting as upper respiratory infections and tiredness. Dulcamara for feeling cold after exertion with constant sneezing, stuffy nose, and swollen watery eyes worse outdoors and in damp weather. Sanguinaria for nasal polyps, dry congested nasal membranes, and chronic rhinitis (if with asthma, consider Arsenicum iodatum, Iodum, Arsenicum, Kali iodatum, or Sabadilla). Rhinitis worse with exertion or warmth: Silicea or Pulsatilla. Early hay fever: Arundo and/or Wyethia. Stuffy nose with constant, unrelieved blowing: Lachesis, Psorinum, Naja, Sticta, or Kali bichromicum. The homeopathic combination Sabadil can also help.","Acupressure: LI20, ST20, B2, LI4, SP5, B13, GB20. Others: B10 (fatigue, headaches, swollen eyes), ST36, TW5. Nambudripad Allergy Elimination Technique (NAET) can help rebalance the immune system.","Aromatherapy: peppermint oil unclogs sinuses and acts as an expectorant; basil oil supports the adrenals, lowers inflammation, and aids detox; eucalyptus oil improves circulation and acts as an expectorant; lemon oil supports lymphatic drainage; tea tree oil is anti-inflammatory, antiseptic, and destroys pathogens. Eucalyptus steam inhalation mixed with ravensara (decongestant), lavender (decreases stress), and peppermint can open the airways. A chamomile balm traps pollen, is anti-inflammatory, and soothes irritated skin. Frankincense boosts the immune system. Others: bergamot, German chamomile, cinnamon, clove bud, helichrysum, juniper needle, lemon, niaouli, peppermint, ravensara, rosemary cineole, rosewood."],"conditions":["Allergic rhinitis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ent_sinusitis","chapter":"ent","title":"Sinusitis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Sinusitis is inflammation located in the sinuses, causing pain, pressure, nasal congestion, nasal discharge or post-nasal drip, toothache, sore throat, cough, headaches, loss of smell, and fatigue. Causes: infection, obstruction, air-pressure changes, injury, and/or allergies. Diagnosis is by history, physical, and imaging if needed (X-ray, CT, or MRI).","Supplements: a multivitamin twice daily with selenium (100–200 mcg/day), zinc (20–40 mg/day), magnesium (400–600 mg/day), calcium (1,000 mg/day, 1,500 mg/day during menopause), chromium (200 mcg/day), bee propolis (500 mg three times daily), and vitamin E. Grapefruit seed extract (spray four times daily) has antiviral, anti-inflammatory, and anti-aging properties — may slow hepatic metabolism of drugs like statins. Oil of oregano (500 mg four times daily) has antibacterial and antiviral properties. Vitamin C (1,000 mg three times daily) boosts immune function. Garlic (500 mg twice daily) improves immune function but can enhance anticoagulant effects. Echinacea (1,000 mg two to three times daily) fights infection, especially at first sign of symptoms.","Others: nettle for allergies (freeze-dried, 300 mg three times daily); quercetin and bromelain (500 mg/day, between meals); licorice (10–20 drops three times daily); pantothenic acid (500 mg three times daily) with hydrochloric acid (1–2 capsules after protein-based meals) to optimize gut health. N-acetylcysteine (500 mg three times daily) thins mucus to aid sinus drainage. Colloidal silver (spray four times daily for acute sinusitis, twice daily for chronic) and Pelargonium sidoides are helpful. Yin chiao (Chinese herbal formula) three times daily; astaxanthin (4 mg once or twice daily); vitamin B (50 mg twice daily).","For fungal sinusitis and candida overgrowth: garlic or Allimax/Allimed (100% pure allicin), beginning at 720–900 mg three times daily and gradually tapering to 180–450 mg once daily over three months, plus Candex/Candisol (two capsules twice daily on an empty stomach). A combination of antifungal supplements — sodium caprylate, oregano leaf, Pau D'Arco, berberine, grapefruit seed, ginger root, chamomile, biotin, and zinc — can also be used. Children's dosing: bromelain (80–320 mg/day), one multivitamin daily, fish oil (1 g EPA/DHA per day), NAC (100–600 mg/day by mouth), nasal lavage (can add goldenseal, five drops, and NAC, five drops, to the salt solution).","Homeopathy: Belladonna for a hot, red face, above-normal temperature, frontal sinus involvement with right-sided throbbing pain worse lying down or bending the head forward and with the slightest pressure — good at first sign of infection. Pulsatilla for weepiness, pain above the eyes, thick yellow-green discharge with nausea/vomiting worse indoors and better with open cool air, cool compresses, and pressure; right maxillary/frontal sinus involvement with jumpy right-sided facial nerve pain. Bryonia alba for sinus pain worse with any downward head movement. Hepar sulphuris for yellow mucus in later stages, a tender face, sneezing, and pain between the eyes worse with cold dry winds, in someone who feels cold and irritable. Kali bichromicum for fullness and congestion on either side of the nose with stringy thick green/yellow mucus and pain at the roof of the nose. Mercurius solubilis for thick foul-smelling green discharge with a little blood, worse with open air, eating, drinking, and extreme cold or hot, possibly feverish with pain extending to the teeth. Silicea for deep tearing/throbbing pain with an itchy nose tip, worse in the right eye and with light, cold air, movement, noise, and mental concentration. Spigelia for sharp left-sided facial pain relieved by cold compresses or cool water and worse with light, movement, noise, and warmth. Others (30C, three times daily): Aurum muriaticum, Medorrhinum, Natrum muriaticum, Thuja occidentalis, Kali sulphuricum.","Acupressure — to relieve sinus pain and pressure and open cavities for drainage: ST3, LI20, B2, LI4 (sinus pain); B2 (congestion and headaches); SI18, yintang, yu yao point; GB20 (nasal congestion); EX HN8. Frontal sinuses: GB14, B2, EX HN3. Maxillary sinuses: ST2, SI18. Ethmoidal and sphenoid sinuses: GV16, GV20.","Aromatherapy: eucalyptus oil clears blocked sinuses via steam inhalation, bath, or massage; combine with peppermint, rosemary, and thyme. Tea tree oil fights infection to aid sinus relief. Lavender helps sleep and stimulates the immune system. Others: Canadian balsam, helichrysum, hyssop, lavender, sweet and Spanish marjoram, green or orange myrtle, niaouli, pine, cineole and camphor rosemary, cedarwood, ravensara, cajeput."],"conditions":["Sinusitis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ent_tinnitus","chapter":"ent","title":"Tinnitus — the Holistic Rx","body":["Tinnitus (ringing in the ears) is a sensation of hearing whistling, ringing, buzzing, sizzling, or other sounds — intermittent or continuous, and may change, stop and go, or get louder and faster. Causes include infections, blockages in the ear, or inner-ear damage; symptoms can improve once the underlying cause is treated. May be accompanied by difficulty concentrating, brain fog, and confusion. Diagnosis by history and physical.","Supplements: B-complex (50 mg/day) supports healthy nerves. Melatonin (1–3 mg/day). Pycnogenol (100–150 mg/day). Bioflavonoids (200–800 mg/day). CoQ10 (100 mg three times daily) may help those low in CoQ10. Magnesium (250 mg twice daily) supports normal hearing. B12 (1,000 mcg/day) supports healthy nerves — get B12 levels tested. Others: Ginkgo biloba (120 mg twice daily of a 24% extract) improves circulation through the inner ear; zinc (90 mg/day with 2–3 mg copper, under a doctor's supervision) may help those with a zinc deficiency.","Homeopathy: Salicylic acid for roaring in the ears and deafness. Carbo sulph for a blocked feeling in the ears with tingling and roaring. Kali iodatum for longstanding ringing with no additional symptoms. Calcarea carbonica for crackling or pulsing sensations in the ears, in someone easily cold, fatigued, overwhelmed, and anxious. Lycopodium for roaring/humming like an echo, in someone prone to infections with discharge and digestive issues. Chininum sulphuricum for roaring, buzzing, hissing, or ringing that impairs hearing, possibly with vertigo. Graphites for tinnitus with deafness, skin eruptions, poor concentration, and constipation. Others: Carbo vegetabilis, sulphur, Chenopodium anthelminticum, Lachnanthes tinctoria, cimicifuga, Coffea cruda, Kali carbonicum, Natrum salicylicum, Salicylicum acidum.","Acupressure: TW7, TW17, TW21, TW22, SI19, GB2.","Aromatherapy: juniper berry, lavender, cypress, and helichrysum essential oils can help treat tinnitus."],"conditions":["Tinnitus"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ent_vertigo","chapter":"ent","title":"Vertigo — the Holistic Rx","body":["Vertigo is a sensation of dizzy spells in which the environment seems to spin, or you feel like you are spinning. Causes include benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, Meniere's disease, labyrinthitis, or vestibular neuritis (inflammation of the nerve in the inner ear); other causes include injury, medications that damage the ear, stroke, tumors, migraines, or multiple sclerosis. Symptoms: swaying, pulling in one direction, imbalance, and sometimes nausea, headache, sweating, ear fullness, ringing, or hearing loss. Diagnosis by history and physical.","Supplements: Ginkgo biloba (60 mg two or three times daily) increases blood flow to the brain. Cayenne (300 mg twice daily) increases blood flow. CoQ10 (100 mg/day). Turmeric (500 mg twice daily) improves blood flow and lowers inflammation. Ginger root (500 mg twice daily, or 1 cup of tea twice daily). Vinpocetine (15 mg/day) improves inner-ear circulation symptoms. Others: ashwagandha (1,000 mg twice daily) and Panax ginseng (100 mg two to three times daily with 4–7% ginsenosides) improve adrenal function.","For Meniere's disease specifically: magnesium (1,000 mg/day) improves tinnitus and protects the ears from damage; B vitamins (500 mg B1, 250 mg B6, 500 mcg B12) and vitamin C (1,000 mg three times daily) with 250 mg bioflavonoids improve symptoms, as does ginkgo biloba (80–120 mg/day) and fenugreek/chamomile teas. Other herbs: dandelion (decreases fluid in the inner ear; 30 drops of tincture twice daily, or 1 tsp of root, or infuse 2 tsp of leaves in a cup of water and drink twice daily); burdock (boil 1 tbsp of seeds or root in a cup of water and take 30 drops of tincture twice daily — improves circulation and releases excess fluid from the body); licorice (anti-inflammatory — avoid with elevated blood pressure or kidney failure; boil 1 tsp of root in a cup of water and drink twice daily, or use 60 drops of tincture).","Homeopathy: Borax for symptoms worse with downward head movement. Theridion for symptoms triggered by loud noises and Meniere's-type presentations. Calcarea for symptoms worse looking up. Conium for whirling sensations lying down and on head rotation. Gelsemium for dizziness with trembling, possibly viral in origin, with drowsiness — also for post-stage-fright dizziness with anxiety, mental dullness, trembling, and visual disturbances. Kali carb for symptoms worse turning, reading, or walking, better in open air. Nux vomica for symptoms worse with flickering bright lights or crowded spaces, possibly with vomiting, nausea, and a hungover feeling, worse in the morning and improving through the day and with lying down. Tabacum for symptoms that improve with cool air and closed eyes, worse with motion, with fainting, cold clammy feelings, nausea, a tight band around the head, and excess salivation before vomiting. Cocculus for symptoms worse lifting the head, looking out a moving window, traveling by car, sleeping, and in the morning, relieved by sitting, with nausea, vomiting, anxiety, palpitations, and hearing loss. Bryonia for dizziness sitting, with head movement, or bending over, craving cold drinks. Pulsatilla for symptoms worse lying down in a warm congested room, better in open air and/or walking. Aconitum napellus for sudden dizziness with a rapid pulse, shock, and panic. Argentum nitricum for symptoms with headaches and trembling, brought on by phobias, agitation, or being rushed. Lachesis for symptoms triggered by feeling congested/claustrophobic, improving with discharge. Phosphoricum acidum for vertigo with headaches and malaise, or following emotional shock. China for ringing in the ears with anemia and fatigue. Salicylicum acidum for vertigo with ringing in the ears and deafness. Chininum sulphuricum for hearing loss, ringing in the ears, and vertigo together. Chenopodium anthelminticum for sudden Meniere's disease with hearing loss, liver pain, and migraines. Others: Carboneum sulphuratum, Bufo, Magnesia carbonica, Oleander, Staphysagria, Aurum muriaticum, Cyclamen europaeum, Glonoinum, Phosphorus, and/or Sulphur.","Acupressure: TW17, SI19, TW16. Others: P6, GV20, GV19, GV21, GB20, GB21, Tai Yang, LV2, ST36, K1, GV26, B23, B47, ST9, LV3.","Aromatherapy: peppermint oil, black pepper, rosemary, eucalyptus, and cypress oil improve blood circulation and oxygen to the brain; cypress oil can also open breathing passages. Others: basil oil, lavender oil, rose oil, tangerine oil. Ginger oil relieves nausea."],"conditions":["Vertigo"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_endo_general_dd","chapter":"endocrinology","title":"Endocrine Health — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Lowering inflammation and maintaining a balance of glucose and insulin is key to optimizing endocrine function and supporting weight loss. Add the superfoods of coconut, olives, avocados, fatty fish and other proteins, nuts and seeds, and Himalayan or Celtic sea salt. Eating fat or protein with every meal and limiting carbs helps stabilize blood sugar and improve endocrine function. Increase fiber and vinegar in the diet, as these can stabilize blood sugar. Foods high in chromium can naturally balance blood glucose — broccoli, grass-fed beef, and green beans. Other healing foods are cinnamon, ginger, rosemary, green tea, cranberries, blueberries, lemon balm, fenugreek, holy basil, neem, bay leaves, turmeric, gymnema sylvestre, and bitter melon. Grapefruit, romaine lettuce, celery, curry powder, asparagus, aloe vera, and flounder all help with weight loss. Caffeine can be problematic for those with compromised adrenals, as it may prevent sleep.","After an initial weight loss, some experience a plateau. Insulin resistance keeps insulin levels up, leading to weight gain or the inability to lose weight even when eating the right foods; periods of low insulin achieved through intermittent fasting can help break that cycle. It takes time for the body to adjust to fasting, so give it at least a month, and talk to a primary care provider first to confirm fasting is safe. Plateaus can also reflect increasing muscle mass alongside decreasing fat mass, so track fat mass and lean mass separately along with waist and hip circumference — weight may not change even as measurements do. When a plateau persists, check basal metabolic rate, consider genetic testing, review food intolerances and toxin exposures, increase water intake and sleep, and increase sweating (through exercise or sauna); focus on the foundations of good health and lowering inflammation.","The Four Big S's: lowering stress and improving sleep can help restore hormonal balance across every endocrine condition in this chapter."],"conditions":["Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_endo_adrenal_fatigue","chapter":"endocrinology","title":"Adrenal Fatigue — the Holistic Rx","body":["The adrenal gland produces and regulates the stress hormone cortisol, along with sex hormones and neurotransmitters that communicate with every organ. Adrenal fatigue is a term applied to a collection of nonspecific symptoms reflecting adrenal dysfunction and resulting nutrient and hormonal imbalance from chronic stress; though not a formal medical diagnosis, it lies on a spectrum between normal function and Addison's disease (when 90 percent of adrenal function is lost). Symptoms include fatigue, trouble waking up, dizziness when standing, irritability and short temper, frequent infections and colds that take time to clear, low body temperature, palpitations, mood disorders, muscle weakness, increased allergies, insomnia, decreased libido, hair loss, headaches, always feeling cold, sugar cravings, low blood pressure, thyroid problems, high energy at bedtime, inflammation, weight gain, muscle tension, light-headedness on standing, chronic pain, PMS symptoms, and a reliance on coffee. Diagnosis can include a blood pressure test for orthostatic hypotension, a pupil contraction test, checking for unstable body temperature, and hormone testing of adrenal hormones, DHEA, adrenal saliva cortisol, and thyroid function.","Supplements: ashwagandha (500 mg once daily); vitamin C (500–3,000 mg daily); B vitamins — B5 (300–1,000 mg per day), B6 (30–100 mg per day), B1 (up to 600 mg per day) — for fatigue and adrenal support; zinc and selenium. Adaptogenic and GABAergic herbs: rhodiola rosea (100–400 mg daily); eleuthero (2–3 g of dried root in a capsule daily); reishi mushroom (3–6 g of dried mushroom in a capsule daily); maca (75–100 mg per day, avoid in pregnancy); phosphatidylserine (100 mg three times per day, or 100–200 mg per day); passionflower (100–200 mg per day); valerian (100–200 mg per day); lemon balm (100–200 mg per day); l-theanine (100–200 mg per day); holy basil (2–3 mg per day) to help recover adrenal function; Siberian, Panax, or Asian ginseng during periods of high stress (100–200 mg, standardized to 4–5% ginsenosides); licorice (50–100 mg per day) for inflammation, especially arthritis or muscle tenderness; tyrosine (500–1,000 mg daily); adrenal glandular (200–500 mg daily); chromium (200–400 µg per day); DHEA (important especially with autoimmune disease such as lupus or Sjogren's, but only under professional guidance); schizandra (20–30 drops of extract, or 2–4 capsules, per day); shatavari (2–4 mg per day).","Homeopathy: Natrum muriaticum for constipation, dry lips and mouth, salt cravings, symptoms worse in hot sun, and a muddy complexion. Argenticum nitricum for tremors, apprehension, and cravings for salt and sweet things. Silicea for lack of stamina and profusely sweating, foul-smelling feet, worse in cold weather. For stress specifically: phosphoric acidum for stress related to grief or bad news; picric acidum for stress from overwork; ignatia for stress following an emotional upset; nux vomica for stress from bad habits (smoking, overeating, overdrinking) or when irritable and overwhelmed.","Acupressure: P6 (to calm and balance), LI4, GB21, LU1, B10, CV17.","Aromatherapy: mix almond oil with clary sage, frankincense, and geranium oils and massage into the skin. Other stress-relieving oils: litsea, orange, and bergamot. For sleep: ylang-ylang, rose, vetiver, and Roman chamomile. Use in massage, a bath, as lotions, or by other methods."],"conditions":["Adrenal fatigue and HPA dysregulation"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_endo_diabetes_metabolic","chapter":"endocrinology","title":"Insulin Resistance, Prediabetes, Metabolic Syndrome, and Diabetes — the Holistic Rx","body":["Diabetes is a chronic condition marked by abnormally high blood glucose because the body either stops producing insulin or can't use the insulin it produces. Type 1 (juvenile/insulin-dependent) is an autoimmune disease in which the immune system destroys the insulin-producing cells; type 2 occurs when the pancreas can't make enough insulin to keep glucose normal and is made worse by poor food choices. Prediabetes (impaired fasting glucose, impaired glucose tolerance, or insulin resistance) is blood glucose higher than normal but not yet diagnostic of diabetes. Metabolic syndrome (dysmetabolic syndrome X, insulin-resistance syndrome) is a cluster including abdominal obesity, high fasting blood sugar, high blood pressure, low HDL cholesterol, and high triglycerides; about 85 percent of people with diabetes have metabolic syndrome. Signs and symptoms of type 1 include abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, increased urination, increased thirst, fatigue, and absence of menstruation. Type 2 presents as excessive urination and thirst, yeast infections, fatigue, impotence, blurred vision, numbness or burning of the extremities, and poor wound healing. Diagnosis is made by history, physical exam, and blood testing.","Supplements: chromium (600 mcg daily) for insulin sensitivity and blood sugar; cinnamon (2 tsp per day in food, smoothie, or tea); alpha lipoic acid (300–1,200 mg daily) improves insulin sensitivity and reduces/prevents diabetic neuropathy (topical capsaicin up to 4 times per day also helps neuropathy); fiber powder from vegetables and seeds (e.g., PGX, 2–4 capsules before each meal with 8 oz water); berberine (200–300 mg two to four times daily) improves sugar control and retinopathy, avoid in pregnancy; bitter melon; prickly pear; B vitamins (B1, B6, B7, B12); vitamins C, D, K; iodine; zinc; melatonin; choline (reduces fatty liver risk); calcium. For metabolic syndrome/insulin resistance specifically: ginseng; berberine (also restores intestinal barrier function and reduces vascular inflammation — watch for drug interactions); bitter melon; holy basil; spirulina and maca root to raise glutathione and support immune function. Others: gymnema sylvestre (400 mg of a 25% gymnemic acid extract daily) lowers blood sugar; vanadyl sulfate (100–300 mg daily, higher doses under medical supervision) improves glucose tolerance; biotin (9–16 mg daily) for glucose metabolism.","For weight loss: guggul (650 mg twice daily), glucomannan (665 mg before bed), CLA (750 mg twice daily), forskolin (385 mg twice daily), sphaeranthus indicus, garcinia mangostana, green coffee bean extract (400 mg twice daily). Other supplements: berberine for lipid control, diabetes prevention, and weight/memory support; magnesium for insulin resistance; pycnogenol (100 mg once or twice daily); resveratrol (100–250 mg daily); a high-potency multivitamin; an antioxidant supplement (vitamin E, selenium, glutathione or NAC, carotenoids) to reduce inflammation; digestive enzymes; nicotinamide (25 mg per kg body weight) to slow disease progression in newly diagnosed children; folic acid (800 mcg daily) to protect against vascular damage; thiamine (50–200 mg daily) for numbness and tingling; apple pectin (500 mg twice daily); banaba extract (450 mg per day); cordyceps (400 mg per day); bitter melon (500 mg per day); fenugreek (500 mg per day); green coffee bean extract (500 mg per day); garcinia (1,000 mg twice daily); cinnamon (500 mg twice daily); cayenne; saffron (100 mg per day); astaxanthin (4 mg per day); coenzyme Q10 (60–200 mg per day); green tea extract (250–500 mg per day); curcumin (500 mg per day).","Children with type 1 diabetes: magnesium (100–400 mg daily), vitamin E (100–400 IU daily), alpha lipoic acid (100 mg daily), vitamin B3 (2–30 mg daily), chromium (50–200 mcg daily), vitamin C (250–1,000 mg daily), fish oil (500–2,000 mg EPA/DHA per day), vitamin D (600–4,000 IU per day), milk thistle (80% silymarin, 200 mg daily). Children with type 2 diabetes: alpha lipoic acid (100 mg daily), chromium (50–200 mcg daily), magnesium (100–200 mg daily), vitamin D (400–4,000 IU daily), zinc (5–20 mg daily), cassia cinnamon (1–2 g three times daily), gymnema (100 mg twice daily), milk thistle (80% silymarin, 200 mg daily), Panax ginseng root extract (15% ginsenosides, 200 mg daily). Children with obesity: fish oil (1–2 g daily), pycnogenol (50–150 mg daily), psyllium (6 g daily), vitamin B3 (10–20 mg daily), green tea (1–3 cups daily), plant sterols (1.5–2.3 g daily).","Homeopathy: phosphoric acidum for diabetes with symptoms from nervous exhaustion after grief or overwork. Uranium nitricum for diabetes with weakness, bedwetting, and digestive upset. Argenticum nitricum for diabetes with swollen ankles. Codeinum for diabetes with restlessness, depression, and skin irritation. Natrum sulphuricum for diabetes accompanied by gout. Silicea for diabetes with cold, smelly, sweaty feet and loss of stamina. Phosphorus for vascular complications of diabetes. Insulin-dependent diabetes: natrum muriaticum, sulphur. Non-insulin-dependent diabetes: sulphur.","Acupressure: for obesity, ST25, ST36, ST40, SP6, B20, B21, CV12. General/circulation support: ST36, LI4, B40, H7, P6, SP6, K3, GV24.5, GB21.","Aromatherapy: coriander and cinnamon essential oils can help the liver and pancreas balance blood sugar. Grapefruit essential oil can help break down body fat for obesity/metabolic syndrome. Cinnamon oil can help regulate blood glucose; ginger oil reduces sugar cravings and inflammation. Others: cypress, fennel, geranium, juniper, lavender, lemon, rosemary. Relaxing oils: bergamot, jasmine, lavender, rose, sandalwood, ylang-ylang. Use in massage, a bath, as lotions, or by other methods."],"conditions":["Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Obesity"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_general_dd","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Gut healing — the Holistic Rx (diet and daily habits)","body":["Healing the gut is key to nearly every digestive complaint, and eating gut superfoods is essential. Maintain a high-fiber diet, and add fermented veggies and seeds like flax, chia, and hemp to add bulk to stools. Other gut-supportive foods include apple cider vinegar, kombucha, bone broth, healthy fats like coconut oil (a powerful antifungal), wild-caught salmon, almonds, squash, honey, cabbage juice (rich in glutamine), and green leafy and cruciferous veggies (which contain methionine, helpful against H. pylori). Bitter greens — turnip greens, dandelion greens, and mustard greens — help the body produce acid, bile, and enzymes to break down food, and cranberry juice can help prevent H. pylori from adhering to the gut. Eat small meals, filling the stomach only two-thirds of the way, to optimize digestion.","Digestive herbs and herbal teas can aid digestion and decrease bloating and ease acute symptoms: fennel, anise, ginger, peppermint, caraway seeds, cardamom, cloves, lemon balm, spearmint, thyme, celery seed, and basil.","Foods that help eliminate parasites include raw garlic, pumpkin seeds, pomegranates, beets, and carrots. Papaya seeds and papaya extracts (papain) have been helpful in eliminating worms and keeping the intestine in a natural acidic state.","Some patients benefit from a FODMAPs diet, which minimizes poorly absorbed short-chain carbohydrates, including most dairy, corn syrup, wheat products, and vegetables and fruit with a high glucose-to-fructose ratio, such as watermelon and dried fruit.","The four big S's — special considerations: remove the stress from your bowels by creating a bathroom routine and listening to your body instead of ignoring urges. Squatting straightens the anorectal angle and allows for easy passage; using a stool (such as a Squatty Potty) under the feet while seated has the same effect."],"conditions":["Bloating and gas","Constipation","Irritable bowel syndrome","Parasites, intestinal"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_bloating","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Bloating and gas — the Holistic Rx","body":["Bloating is the feeling of gas filling up the stomach or bowels. Digestive disorders, fluid buildup, dehydration, food sensitivities, constipation, hormonal changes, small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, infection, cancer, or even bowel obstruction can lead to bloating.","Supplements: gut-healing supplements are optimal for relieving bloating, including probiotics, digestive enzymes, and even activated charcoal tablets (which help absorb toxins and gases) and chlorophyll, taken with each meal.","Homeopathy: Carbo vegetabilis helps with bloating and belching. Lycopodium is useful for passing gas when constipation is also present, especially when eating onions or garlic. Dioscorea is helpful for gas in the presence of diarrhea and abdominal pain that is better by bending backward. Ignatia is a remedy for bloating of the abdomen after grief. Natrum carbonicum helps those who have trouble digesting foods, associated with heartburn, ulcers, and dairy-induced gas and diarrhea — they crave sweets and potatoes. Thuja is indicated for abdominal bloating, chronic diarrhea, and rumbling of the bowels accompanied by lots of gas. Others that can be used are Ambra grisea, Arnica montana, Aurum muriaticum, folliculinum, Phosphoricum acidum, and sulphur.","Acupressure: ST36, SP6, LV3. Others: ST25, CV6, ST37, ST41, LI4.","Aromatherapy: angelica, cardamom, Roman chamomile, fennel, ginger, lemon, peppermint, and rosemary cineole can help with bloating."],"conditions":["Bloating and gas"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_candidiasis","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Candidiasis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Candida albicans is one of the most common types of yeast infection, found in the vagina, digestive system, mucous membranes, and skin. Candida can overgrow when the immune system isn't working properly; broad-spectrum antibiotics, birth control pills, oral corticosteroids, a high-sugar diet, chronic anxiety and other negative emotions, cancer treatments, and diabetes can trigger candidal overgrowth. Symptoms include fatigue, brain fog, sinus issues, bloating, gas, abdominal and joint pain and other digestive issues, bad breath, sleep and mood disorders, sugar cravings, blood sugar and hormonal imbalances, muscle aches, allergies/sinus issues, loss of sex drive, foggy thinking, recurring infections (especially urinary tract and vaginal), oral thrush, skin and nail fungal infections, food intolerances, and sensitivity to smells, sounds, or light.","Supplements: focus on healing the gut and supporting the liver, with probiotics and milk thistle. Natural antifungals: oil of oregano (300–500 mg three times per day with meals, minimum sixty days), berberine, caprylic acid (1,000 mg three times per day), hemicelluloses. Spleen support: licorice, gentian (250 mg at the beginning of each meal), coptis, gardenia, skullcap, ginseng. Others: selenium (decreases oxidative stress), garlic (500–1,000 mg of aged garlic twice daily, or two cloves daily), grapefruit seed extract (200 mg two to three times daily), vitamin C (1,000 mg twice per day), pau d'arco tea, S. boulardii (5–15 billion CFU two to four times per day for sixty days), and activated charcoal (two to four capsules per day at bedtime for sixty days; may deplete magnesium, so replace it). Start any anti-candidal agent at a low dose and increase very gradually to reduce symptoms of candidal die-off reaction.","Children: probiotics, baking soda (1/4 tsp gargled or swallowed in water or formula four times daily), garlic paste applied topically twice daily, lemon juice (1 tbsp diluted in 8 oz of water, applied topically, gargled, or swallowed twice daily), lemongrass infusion (applied topically or gargled twice daily), a GI-probiotic, black walnut hull tincture (1:1 ratio, one drop per ten pounds of body weight in a glass of water three times daily), caprylic acid (250–500 mg daily), garlic (one to three cloves daily), olive leaf (250–1,000 mg daily). For fungal overgrowth generally: oregano, garlic, caprylic acid, grapeseed extract, probiotics, olive leaf extract.","Homeopathy: Candida albicans (look for 30C). Sepia — yeast infection with yellowish discharge, bearing-down sensation, chronic constipation, itching of the skin, fatigue, but improved by exercise and sunlight; irritable/moody, often chilly but worse in a warm room with limited circulation. Pulsatilla — cries easily, irritable and indifferent; itching or red skin on the chest and abdomen; discharge thick and yellowish-green with vulvar swelling; better in open air, worse lying down, heat, warm rooms. Calcarea carbonica — constipated, overweight, craves sweet and salty; yeast infections thick, creamy yellow, with burning and itching; catches colds easily; coldness of the top of the head and legs with sweating (especially the back of the head) on minimal exertion. Sulphur — burning or redness anywhere on the body (face, ears, rectum, nose, vagina); warm patient, worse from heat, burning soles at night, diarrhea at 5:00 a.m. Graphites — thick, cracked skin at the corners of the mouth or heels. Kreosotum — irritating discharge causing swelling or itching, bad odor, redness, rawness, and pain on urination. Borax — diarrhea with bleeding oral mucosa, rash, discharge sticky and runny like egg whites. Belladonna — bright red, painful inflamed skin, not raw or oozing; irritable. Chamomilla — irritability and diaper rash. Arsenicum album — itchy, burning rashes with anxiety.","Acupressure: SP9, LI4, LV3.","Aromatherapy: essential oils that act as antifungals include oregano, clove, myrrh, melaleuca, and thyme. Lavender oil can help inhibit candida growth. Peppermint oil can relieve intestinal cramping. Others: cedarwood, eucalyptus globulus, geranium, helichrysum, lavandin, lemon, manuka, rosemary verbenone, rosewood, and tea tree."],"conditions":["Candidiasis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_constipation","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Constipation — the Holistic Rx","body":["Constipation is hardened feces with difficulty emptying the bowels; it is important to find the root cause. Causes may relate to lifestyle, dehydration, gut problems, poor bathroom and sleep habits, magnesium deficiency, and medications (antidepressants, antacids, blood pressure medications, painkillers); it can also be associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, hormonal problems and other chronic illnesses, older age, poor sleep, jet lag, and travel. It is diagnosed by history and physical.","Supplements: cod liver oil. Psyllium (5 g psyllium husk twice daily). Flax seed oil or flax seeds (1–2 tbsp daily). Pectin. Aloe vera juice. Others: senna (one to four pills per day), cascara sagrada (250 mg, or 2.5 ml of tincture, two to three times daily for acute constipation), triphala. Children: magnesium (100–250 mg daily), probiotics, castor oil rubbed clockwise on the abdomen, zinc (20–40 mg daily).","Homeopathy: Nux vomica — great urge to defecate but nothing passes, or incomplete defecation; good for those who study hard or are sedentary, elderly, or laxative-dependent; cold, irritable, overstressed. Bryonia — large, dry, hard stools with headaches, congestion, and burning in the rectum after stooling; thirsty and irritable; works well in the elderly. Alumina — no desire to defecate until the rectum is completely full; stools may feel as if they accumulate under the left rib area; even soft stools are hard to pass; indicated in the elderly, who may have memory issues. Natrum muriaticum — hard, crumbly stools every second day or longer; rectum dry and painful; strong thirst for water and salt; possible depression. Sulphur — constipation alternating with diarrhea, stools every two to four days, dark, hard, and large with incomplete defecation, hemorrhoids and anal fissure; burning rectum; strong thirst for ice-cold drinks. Silicea — small, hard stools covered with mucus, sore anus, head sweats, and the feeling of the stool sliding back in; cold and thin patients. Opium — no desire to defecate for days, poor appetite, alert to the slightest noise, wakeful at night and drowsy by day. Graphites — anal fissure or hemorrhoids, no desire to stool for days, then colicky pain and passage of stool covered in white mucus; bowels may ache afterward. Aesculus — fullness, crawling sensation in the anus, rectum hot and dry, feels full of spikes; sensation of a knife opening the bowels. Nitric acidium — splinter-like pain lasting hours after stooling, burning, tearing, itching. Plumbum metallicum — sharp colicky pains with little outcome; stools hard, dry, and black. Lycopodium — relieves constipation and gas when there's no desire to pass a stool and, when passed, it's painful and worse between 4:00 and 8:00 p.m.; craves sweets; improves with warm drinks. Ammonium muriaticum — burning sensation in the anus and rectum; after much straining, crumbly, hard, dry stools. Magnesia muriatica — dry, hard stools that crumble around the anus. Causticum — older individuals prone to ankylosis with ineffectual urge to stool. Hydrastis canadensis — constipated stools covered with mucus, no desire to defecate; very effective in pregnant women, children, and chronic laxative users. Raphanus niger — belly distension and gas at the left upper abdomen, distended and painful, improves with passing gas. Calcarea carbonica — feels overwhelmed, chronically constipated, hands and feet cold and clammy. Sepia — constipation with hormonal issues, heavy sensation in the rectum and lower abdomen.","Acupressure: LV3, LI4, LI11, ST36, CV12.","Aromatherapy: carrot seed, orange, and fennel soothe the digestive system; spearmint soothes digestion. Others: black pepper, marjoram, grapefruit, ginger, mandarin, bergamot, rosemary cineole, jasmine, lavender, rose, pine, sandalwood, and ylang-ylang.","Other recommendations: never repress the urge to defecate — holding back trains the bowels to misbehave and can lead to chronic, even lifelong, constipation. It is possible to retrain the bowels: sit on the toilet at the same time every day, even without an urge — early morning or right after exercise are usually good times. Do not strain (it only creates hemorrhoids or varicose veins); instead breathe deeply using the abdominal muscles and try to relax."],"conditions":["Constipation"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_diarrhea","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Diarrhea — the Holistic Rx","body":["Diarrhea increases the frequency, volume, and wateriness of stool and may be due to a virus or infection; chronic diarrhea (more than four weeks) can signal another bowel issue like inflammatory bowel disease or irritable bowel syndrome. Other causes include reactions to medications, sensitivities to foods or artificial sweeteners, thyroid problems, diabetes, and other chronic illnesses. Other symptoms include bloating, nausea, vomiting, and an urgent need to pass a bowel movement. Diagnosis is made by history and physical.","Supplements: gut-healing supplements — probiotics, digestive enzymes, glutamine, and aloe vera juice (1/2 cup three times daily). Raw sprouted fiber from flax and chia seeds (3 tbsp daily). Others: ginger (500 mg in capsule), goldenseal (300 mg capsule four times per day), oregano oil (500 mg capsule four times per day) as an antimicrobial, and astragalus (500–1,000 mg two to three times per day, not to be taken with a fever). Children: probiotics, zinc (10–20 mg daily), glutamine (0.3 g/kg daily), calcium (200–500 mg daily), fiber (5–15 g three times per day with plenty of water), berberine (400 mg daily), digestive enzymes, colostrum, lauricidin (1/4 tsp three times daily, increasing as needed for normal stool consistency), echinacea, aloe extracts, fatty acids.","Homeopathy: Aconite — sudden diarrhea after cold wind, shock, or summer overheating; distended abdomen, better after passing a stool. Aloe socotrina — morning violent diarrhea with abdominal rumbling, then gushing stools after an anger outburst or food sensitivity exposure; stools yellowish-green and mucus-filled; worse after meals with excessive gas. Arsenicum album — exhausting diarrhea, abdominal pain, vomiting; scant, odorless stool, may have blood and burn the anus; better with sips of hot drinks, worse with cold drinks, foods, and overripe fruit. Podophyllum — offensive diarrhea with a batter-like consistency, color of pea soup; gas with morning urgency. Phosphoric acidium — profuse, painless watery stools with bits of food, after emotional shock or overeating; better after defecation. Sulphur — burning, explosive, foul-smelling diarrhea that forces a person out of bed at 5:00 a.m., hemorrhoids present, anus red and excoriated, craves cold liquids. Veratrum — profuse, painful, rice-water diarrhea with vomiting, cold sweats, craving ice water. China — stools like chopped egg, excessive gas, chronic spasms, exhaustion and weakness following diarrhea; very irritable; worse with summer chills or eating fruit. Colocynth — excessive diarrhea brought on by anger, with gripping pain relieved by doubling up, pressure, and heat; stools yellowish, thin, and frothy. Dulcamara — slimy, green or yellow stool, may have blood, brought on by damp weather or getting cold after exertion. Pulsatilla — stools that don't look alike, worse at night, in a warm room, from cold drinks, onions, and rich fatty foods; better in open air. Argenticum nitricum — painless emotional or nervous diarrhea with bloating and gastric burning, can follow sugar ingestion. Chamomilla — yellow-green diarrhea in teething infants. Ipecacuanha — nausea with diarrhea. Mercurius solubilis — spasmodic, burning, or bloody diarrhea with extreme sweating. Phosphorus — diarrhea in anxious people who crave cold liquids. Antimonium crudum — stools partly solid, partly liquid; thick white-coated tongue; overweight, with impetigo and hyperkeratosis. Natrum sulphuricum — violent, watery diarrhea after meals with a yellow/greenish coated tongue; overweight, may have depression, cold-sensitive. Paratyphoidinum — chronic diarrhea.","Acupressure: LI4, LI11, ST36, SP6, SP4, SP16 (abdominal cramps), CV6 (with gas). Others: SP15, SP4, LV3.","Aromatherapy: essential oils with antispasmodic properties can relieve abdominal cramps — neroli, mandarin, cypress, eucalyptus, fennel, chamomile, peppermint, black pepper, and ginger. Others: lemon balm, lavender, cedarwood, cinnamon, clove (bud), geranium, ginger, marjoram, niaouli, nutmeg, sandalwood, and spearmint."],"conditions":["Diarrhea"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_diverticulosis","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Diverticulosis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Diverticulosis is a condition in which small bulging pouches develop in the digestive tract; it is common in those over forty. It is asymptomatic unless the diverticula become inflamed and infected (diverticulitis), which can cause abdominal pain, nausea, alternating diarrhea and constipation, and fever. Diagnosis is made with lab tests or imaging.","Supplements: gut-healing supplements are important — probiotics, digestive enzymes, slippery elm (500 mg three times per day), aloe vera juice or powder (during an attack take 12–16 oz of aloe juice per day), licorice root (100–300 mg per day), and psyllium fiber to prevent constipation. Others: colloidal silver (1 tsp four times daily) and boswellia (500 mg three times per day).","Homeopathy: Nitricum acidum — beneficial for diverticulosis in the rectosigmoid area. Belladonna — sudden abdominal pain and cramping with constipation, worse with motion, better with firm pressure. Bryonia — left-sided sharp abdominal pain, worse with movement, relieved with heat; accompanied by constipation and vomiting. Colocynthis — cramping, sharp pain that improves with pressure, accompanied by diarrhea and restlessness. Arsenicum album — burning pain, anxious and restless, relieved by a warm environment. Ignatia amara — digestive spasms associated with emotional stress. Magnesia phosphorica — abdominal cramping that worsens with pressure and is relieved by warmth. Nux vomica — cramping pain, especially in a cold-sensitive, irritable person.","Acupressure: CV4, SP4, P6, ST36, CV6 (gas and diarrhea), SP16 (abdominal cramps), LI11 (overall gut health).","Aromatherapy: essential oils with an antispasmodic effect that help relax the abdomen include German chamomile, lavender, geranium, ginger, juniper (berry), Melissa, myrrh, niaouli, bitter orange, peppermint, rosemary, and marjoram."],"conditions":["Diverticulosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_gallstones","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Gallstones — the Holistic Rx","body":["Gallstones are tiny pieces of solid matter in the gallbladder, formed when cholesterol, calcium, and other particles in bile bind together. Symptoms vary depending on where the gallstone is located, but an attack can bring pain, nausea, and upper-right-sided abdominal pain that can radiate under the right shoulder blade or into the back. Diagnosis is made by imaging such as ultrasound or CT, or more specialized imaging.","Supplements: supplements can improve liver health and regulate the production and use of cholesterol. Turmeric, milk thistle (150 mg twice daily), dandelion root (500 mg with meals). Others: activated charcoal helps bind and remove toxins; vitamin C (1–3 g daily) can decrease gallstone formation; vitamin E (200–400 IU per day); lipase enzymes (two capsules with meals to improve fat digestion); bile salts or ox bile (500–1,000 mg with meals) to improve fat breakdown and gallbladder function; phosphatidylcholine (100 mg two or three times per day) can help smooth the bile and decrease stone formation; barberry helps cleanse the gallbladder and liver; L-methionine (1 g per day).","Homeopathy: Berberis 6C. China — for those who are oversensitive and always cold.","Acupressure: for gallbladder health, LV3. Others: GB34, P6, LI4, LV14, CV14, ST36, and Zhigou (TH6).","Aromatherapy: rosemary oil can help reduce inflammation and can be massaged over the gallbladder area twice daily."],"conditions":["Gallstones"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_gerd","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"GERD — the Holistic Rx","body":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and acid reflux symptoms include heartburn, dry mouth, bitter taste in mouth, regurgitation of acid or food, bad breath, gum irritation, belching after meals, bloating one to two hours after meals, feeling full easily, low appetite, difficulty swallowing, chronic constipation, undigested food in stool, rectal itching, anemia, waking up choking, nausea, hiccups, discomfort lying down or bending over, hoarseness, chronic throat irritation, dry and peeling nails, sour taste in mouth, coughing at night, and soreness. As it progresses it can lead to blood in vomit, black stools, and unexpected weight loss. Acid reflux can result from not enough stomach acid, drugs like NSAIDs, birth control pills, muscle relaxers, nitroglycerine, steroids, tight clothing, pregnancy, being overweight or obese, hiatal hernia, stress, large meals, food sensitivities, and excessive exercise.","Supplements: probiotics, digestive enzymes, and HCl with pepsin, L-glutamine, and so on. Others to soothe the gut lining: licorice root (DGL) (one or two 400 mg tablets twenty minutes before each meal). Aloe vera juice (1/2 cup twice a day) helps soothe internal inflammation. D-limonene (1,000 mg per day for ten doses as needed). Slippery elm (300 mg daily, or 1 tsp/3 ml tincture three times per day). Others include chamomile tea, melatonin (6 mg at bedtime), orange peel extract (one capsule of 98.5% D-limonene extract every other day for twenty-one days), vitamin C, peppermint oil, capsaicin, and meadowsweet (1 cup of tea with 1–2 tsp). For Barrett's esophagus: the above can also be used, along with quercetin (500–6,000 mg daily) to protect esophageal tissue, antioxidants like vitamin C (1,000 mg daily), vitamin E, selenium (200–400 mcg per day), NAC (1,000–2,000 mg) and lipoic acid, and other gut-healing herbs like marshmallow, gamma oryzanol, and/or turmeric. Children: apple cider vinegar (1/4 tsp before meals), DGL (300 mg three times daily).","Homeopathy: Carbo vegetabilis — bloating and belching/gas after eating, light burning sensation in the stomach radiating to the back; cold, pale, craves fresh air, feels better with cool things. Nux vomica — heartburn about a half hour after eating with constipation, brought on by excessive food, alcohol, and stress/work; irritable and oversensitive. Pulsatilla — heartburn worse after rich, fatty food, worse in a warm room, better in fresh air; attack begins two hours after eating with pressure under the breastbone, bad taste, nausea, weepiness, desire for comfort, headaches around the eyes. Arsenicum album — heartburn after eating with a stone-weight sensation in the stomach; anxious, restless, vomits or retches until exhausted; better sipping warm water and sitting up, worse in the morning. Bryonia — heartburn after eating, heavy-stone feeling, nausea, faintness, mouth filling with bitter fluid; relieved lying on the back, worse with pressure or slightest movement. China — stomach full of air, sluggish and apathetic, worse at night, not relieved by belching, recent fluid loss (blood, sweat, semen), chopped-egg stool appearance. Anacardium — indigestion one to two hours after meals, foul mouth taste, stomach feels blocked, improves with eating again, worse with cold drinks; forgetful; can be used if peptic ulcer is suspected. Argenticum nitricum — indigestion after sweet foods, alternating constipation and diarrhea, belching, fluttery stomach; suspected peptic ulcer. Sepia — heartburn improved by lying on the right side and by eating; sudden empty stomach feeling, sour mouth taste, bloating, liver tenderness, nausea from smelling food; craves pickles. Lycopodium — gas and bloating, feels full quickly, not improved by belching; anxiety, lack of confidence, constipation, worse in tight clothes, late afternoon/evening; improves with warm fluids; suspected peptic ulcer. Phosphorus — burning chest sensations relieved by ice-cold water but followed by vomiting and nausea; suspected peptic ulcer. Graphites — burning hunger pains relieved by food or hot drinks like milk, nausea after sweets; used for peptic ulcers. Kali bichromium — nausea and vomiting after beer, pain in a particular spot, distended stomach; used for peptic ulcers. Sulphur — diarrhea, burning pain, belching improved by ice-cold drinks. Natrum carbonicum — trouble digesting most foods. Antimonium crudum — pain in the pit of the stomach with overeating, white thick milky tongue, hyperkeratosis and impetigo eruptions. Others: robinia (excess acid, worse at night, frontal and temporal headache), sulphuricum acidum (stomach pain after alcohol and from hiatal hernia, ulcerative stomatitis), asa foetida (aerophagia), nux moschata (extreme flatulence), chelidonium majus (pain in the hepatobiliary region radiating to the inferior angle of the right scapula), hydrastis canadensis (hepatobiliary pain in a thin, tired, constipated person), ignatia (paradoxical cramps and burning worse around 11:00 a.m., improved by entertainment), lachesis (heartburn with PMS, hemorrhoids, hot flashes, and rectal bleeding, after excessive alcohol and during menopause), and thuja occidentalis (gas, colonic distention worse two to three hours after meals, relieved by heat; overweight, cold-sensitive, phobia-prone).","Acupressure: HP6, ST36, CV12, SP4. For gastritis: ST21, ST36, SP4, B21, CV12.","Aromatherapy: a peppermint and ginger massage — combining sunflower oil carrier with peppermint essential oil, ginger essential oil, and dill oil — can relieve reflux symptoms. Antispasmodic, calming, warming oils like cardamom, coriander, and mandarin can relieve indigestion. Others: cedarwood, Roman chamomile, lemon, sandalwood, anise, basil linalool, bergamot, fennel, lavender, mandarin, marjoram, rosemary cineole, spearmint, and thyme linalool. Use aromatherapy at least two hours apart from eating and exercise.","Other recommendations: drink more water, but not with meals; stay upright after meals; stay upright after large meals and elevate the head of the bed by six inches; avoid tight-fitting clothing around the middle. Heel drops can help reset a hiatal hernia — standing, take a gulp of water and come up on your tiptoes, then, as you swallow, drop your heels to the ground."],"conditions":["GERD"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_hemorrhoids","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Hemorrhoids — the Holistic Rx","body":["Hemorrhoids are rectal or anal veins that become painful and swollen. There are two types: internal (inside the anus or lower rectum) and external (outside the anal opening). Symptoms include anal pain and itching, painful bowel movements, bright red blood on stool/toilet paper/in the bowel, and harder, tender lumps near the anus. Diagnosis is by history and physical exam; a doctor may use an anoscope or sigmoidoscope to look inside the anal canal.","Supplements: fiber, like psyllium husk. Pycnogenol (100 mg twice daily) for acute hemorrhoids. Horse chestnut (100 mg daily) improves circulation and vascular tone. Witch hazel reduces inflammation and pain. Others: butcher's broom treats chronic venous insufficiency; diosmin (1,000–3,000 mg per day in divided doses for one week for acute issues, then 1,000 mg per day in divided doses for at least two to three months) reduces symptoms of chronic or recurrent hemorrhoids; collinsonia (500 mg three times a day) reduces swelling; bioflavonoid complex (1,000 mg two or three times daily) reduces swelling and prevents bleeding; bilberry; vitamin E (400–800 IU of alpha and mixed tocopherol daily). For temporary relief of pain and itching, use as a topical: cocoa butter, zinc oxide, olive oil, or calendula gel. Salves: vitamin E oil, comfrey, calendula ointment, or goldenseal salve gently applied to the anus with fingers; witch hazel is also soothing.","Homeopathy: Aesculus — large, painful internal hemorrhoids, dry spiky rectal sensation, lumpy stools stabbing and tearing on passage, pain extending to the back, anus dry, hot, and itchy. Aloe — large, painful hemorrhoids like a small bluish cluster of grapes, diarrhea, rectal fullness and burning or needle-like pain, better with cold applications; dry, excoriated mucous membranes with sphincter problems. Collinsonia — relieves bleeding, constipation, itching, and the desire to defecate. Hamamelis — bleeding hemorrhoids with bruised, sore feeling, aggravated by heat and the slightest touch. Nitric acid — anal fissure with cutting, burning pain throughout defecation. Nux vomica — large, painful hemorrhoids that burn and sting with incomplete emptying and frequent urge, improved by cold baths; best for sedentary patients; from constipation and excessive straining; irritable, tend to overuse drugs, meds, or alcohol. Pulsatilla — hemorrhoids that bleed easily (dark blood), worse lying down, in heat and stuffy rooms, better in cool air; associated with constipation, aversion to fats, and weakness. Sulphur — large, itching, burning hemorrhoids, sore inflamed red anal skin, worse with warmth and at night, foul-smelling flatulence. Ammonium carbonicum — extremely painful hemorrhoids, worse with menses and lying down. Capsicum — burning, protruding piles. Causticum — sore hemorrhoids, inability to pass stool even when straining. Calcarea fluorica — bleeding and itching hemorrhoids, with possible constipation, back pain, and flatulence. Ignatia amara — rectal spasms, stabbing pain worse from emotional upset. Ratahia — cutting pain after a bowel movement with fissures and itching, relieved by cold or very hot water, may accompany burning diarrhea. Arnica montana — bruising sensations worse with touch. Carduus marianus — bloody hemorrhoids with constipation, liver problems, and/or varicosities. Graphites — painful, itchy fissures with no tenesmus. Kali carbonicum — protruding, painful, bleeding hemorrhoids improved by cold or sitting on a hard surface, with weakness, paleness, respiratory disorders, and flatulent indigestion. Nitricum acidum — clean, sharp fissures that bleed easily, radiating around the anus, pain worse with hard or soft stools and lasting after defecation. Sepia — hemorrhoids that ooze, with prolapsed anorectal tissue. Chronic remedies (usually low potency, daily): Collinsonia canadensis (bloody hemorrhoids with constipation, best in pregnant women), paeonia (burning, copiously oozing hemorrhoids, pain before and after stooling, thick honey-yellow oozing with fissures), Hora brasiliensis (anal or perianal eruptions, constipation alternating with diarrhea, sensation of anal constriction), Fluoricum acidum (hemorrhoids with itching aggravated by heat, improved by cold).","Acupressure: GV1, GV20, B30, SP6, LU3, CV4, B31, K7, B32, B57. Others: CV6, B60, SP8, LU9, LI12.","Aromatherapy: cypress oil stops excess blood flow and reduces anxiety — apply topically with three to four drops on a cotton ball. Helichrysum, lavender, and turmeric oils can be used topically as anti-inflammatory agents. Lemon and geranium oils detoxify. Juniper has an astringent action that shrinks varicose veins and hemorrhoids. Others: frankincense, cajuput, cedarwood, Roman chamomile, clary sage, cypress, niaouli, patchouli, sandalwood, spikenard, and tea tree oil."],"conditions":["Hemorrhoids"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_ibd","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Inflammatory bowel disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Inflammatory bowel disease is chronic inflammation of all or part of the digestive tract; types include Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis. Symptoms include pain, fatigue, diarrhea, weight loss, fever, nausea and vomiting, floating stools, anemia, rectal bleeding, and joint pain. Ulcerative colitis causes open sores or ulcers in the innermost layer of the large intestine and rectum; Crohn's disease can occur anywhere in the digestive tract, often in patches surrounded by healthy tissue. Diagnosis is made by blood tests, colonoscopy and sigmoidoscopy with possible biopsy, barium enema, and other imaging.","Supplements: focus on gut-healing supplements plus calcium and a multivitamin with antioxidants, zinc, vitamin C (500 mg), vitamin E (400 IU of mixed tocopherols and tocotrienols), curcumin, bromelain (2,000–3,000 mg per day), and slippery elm. Others: aloe vera, DGL (300 mg before meals), enzymes (one to two capsules with each meal), glutamine (1–3 g three times daily on an empty stomach), phosphatidylcholine and IgA supplements, marshmallow, cat's claw (250 mg per day), and boswellia (can help in Crohn's, but should not be taken more than eight weeks straight).","Children — Crohn's disease: fish oil (EPA/DHA 3 g daily), probiotics, vitamin D (1,000–5,000 IU daily), NAC (3–6 g daily), glutamine (5,000 mg daily), bromelain (250–1,000 mg daily), butyrate (4 g daily), fiber, L-carnitine (500–2,000 mg daily), turmeric standardized extract (400–600 mg daily). Ulcerative colitis: probiotics, fish oil (3 g EPA/DHA daily), vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU daily), bromelain (250–1,000 mg daily), butyrate (500–1,000 mg daily), vitamin C (500–2,000 mg daily), curcumin (500–1,000 mg daily).","Homeopathy: can be taken every two hours for up to ten doses during an acute attack; look for a constitutional remedy. Mercurius — abdominal pain unrelieved by passing stool, offensive-smelling stool with blood and mucus, extremely painful passage. Arsenicum — burning abdominal pain worse after midnight with vomiting, anxiety, restlessness; feels cold, prefers warm drinks. Phosphorus — pain improves after passing bloody stools but the anus feels wide open afterward. Arsenicum album — burning abdominal pain better in a warm environment or with warm drinks, anxious and restless. China — for suspected obstruction. Belladonna — abdominal pain that stops and starts abruptly, tender abdomen very sensitive to light vibration or jolts, worse with motion; red hot face, throbbing/burning fever. Bryonia — stomachache that feels like the abdomen is about to burst, worse with pressure, so severe the person can't talk or move. Chamomilla — cutting pain making the person double up and cry, often after anger, bloated abdomen. Colocynth — sharp, colicky, twisting pain just below the navel, improved by passing gas, pressure, or bending forward. Magnesia phosphorica — cramping pain that makes the person cry out, improved by pressure, warmth, and friction. Nux vomica — cramping digestive pain, irritable and always cold. Ignatia — for emotional stress that leads to spasms of the digestive tract. Pulsatilla — diarrhea from fatty foods, worse at night. Sulphur — burning pain improved by cold drinks, explosive and urgent diarrhea that can wake you at night, craves alcohol and spicy foods. Cambogia — chronic colitis. Podophyllum — painless, explosive diarrhea worse after eating or drinking, followed by exhaustion, painful leg and foot cramps. Veratrum album — bloating, cramping, exhaustion, chills, and vomiting with profuse watery diarrhea that worsens after fruit; craves cold liquids. For anal fistula: Calcarea sulph (painful abscess), Silicea (post-cleaning discharge), Lachesis (pain traveling up the rectum, worse from coughing or sneezing).","Acupressure: SP6, ST25, B25, SP4, ST37, ST36, ST39, CV4, LI11, SP16 (for painful cramps). Others: SP15, CV12, CV9, CV6.","Aromatherapy: frankincense improves circulation and digestive health. Chamomile, lavender, and lemongrass oils relieve pain. Ginger, peppermint, and fennel relieve inflammation."],"conditions":["Inflammatory bowel disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_ibs","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Irritable bowel syndrome — the Holistic Rx","body":["Irritable bowel syndrome leads to diarrhea, constipation, and pain after bowel movements, mucus in the stool, an immediate need to go to the bathroom on waking and during or after meals, and a feeling of incomplete emptying due to faster or slower intestinal contractions.","Supplements: focus on gut-healing supplements — probiotics, digestive enzymes, L-glutamine, fish oil (EPA/DHA 1,000 mg daily), aloe vera (half a cup three times per day), fiber (10–15 g per day), zinc. Others: slippery elm, ginger (500 mg per day), and licorice root. Diamine oxidase (DAO) (one to two capsules within fifteen minutes of eating) can help reduce histamine levels; vitamin C, magnesium, and gentian root (300 mg before meals); peppermint oil (450–1,100 mg per day before meals for all types of IBS). Children: digestive enzymes, probiotics, fiber, melatonin (3 mg daily), tryptophan (1–5 g daily), peppermint oil (one enteric-coated capsule three times daily), artichoke leaf extract with 13–18% caffeoylquinic acid (200–500 mg daily).","Homeopathy: Argentum nitricum — bloating pain in the upper-left abdomen, nervousness, constipation alternating with diarrhea after eating sweets, tense stomach feeling. Cantharis — nausea, vomiting, cystitis, burning abdominal pains. Colchicum — nausea and watery stools made worse by food smells, with tearing pains. Colocynth — violent, spasmodic, cutting abdominal pain relieved by doubling over or pressure on the abdomen, occurring after fruit, during diarrhea, or with anger. Arsenicum album — feels cold, prefers warm drinks; diarrhea, restlessness, anxiety, pains worse midnight to 2:00 a.m. Lycopodium — abdominal pain and gurgling noises, bloating, constipation, hemorrhoids, low confidence and irritability, worse 4:00–8:00 p.m., better with warm drinks. Magnesia phosphorica — spasms and cramping, better with warmth, no nervous trigger. Nux vomica — bowel problems from poor diet and stress; craves spicy food, alcohol, stimulants; constipation, spasms, tenesmus, heartburn, feels never fully emptied; worse with anger or excitement, better after a short nap. Ignatia — spasms, constipation with right iliac pain, sensation of a ball in the throat, spasmodic hiccough worse with worry, better with entertainment; anxious. Natrum carbonicum — multiple food allergies, indigestion and heartburn; craves milk, potatoes, sweets but gets irritable bowels from them; desires to be left alone. Sulphur — awakened by diarrhea, possible constipation, irritated rectum, foul rotten-egg gas; craves sweets, alcohol, and spicy foods. Iris tenax — colicky pain in the appendicular area. Thuja occidentalis — abdominal pain with rumbling/flatulence, spasmodic diarrhea or constipation, obsessions and/or phobias.","Acupressure: CV12 (prevents indigestion, use before meals), CV6 (soothes abdominal pain, constipation, and gas), P6 (calming effect), ST37, ST25, B25, B20, B21, SP6 (with menses). Abdominal pain: LI4, LI11, LV3.","Aromatherapy: peppermint, fennel, and ginger improve IBS symptoms. Others: chamomile, lavender, neroli, catnip, marjoram, and sage. Black pepper can help relieve constipation."],"conditions":["Irritable bowel syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_parasites","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Intestinal parasites — the Holistic Rx","body":["A parasite is an organism that lives on or in a host and causes disease and illness. Parasites can enter the system from contaminated food or water; those with leaky gut or a weakened immune system are more prone to infection. Symptoms come in waves but never fully go away: night sweats, fatigue, allergies, digestive issues, anal itching, unexpectedly high white blood cell count, skin rashes (eczema, psoriasis, rosacea, hives, or unexplained rash), trouble falling asleep, waking multiple times at night, teeth grinding, muscle aches, never feeling satisfied after eating, and iron-deficiency anemia. Suspect parasites when symptoms worsen after camping or travel where contaminated water may have been consumed. A comprehensive stool test can help detect parasites, but a negative test isn't always definitive.","Supplements: before starting a parasite cleanse, make sure you have daily regular bowel movements. Black walnut (250 mg three times daily). Wormwood (200 mg three times daily). Oregano oil (500 mg four times daily) has antiparasitic effects. Grapefruit seed extract (as directed). Clove oil (500 mg four times daily). Ginger root (500 mg four times daily). Others: coptis (gold thread) (200 mg three times daily), a Chinese antiparasitic herb, and goldenseal (300 mg four times daily) to fight gut and digestive tract infections. Other helpful herbs: aloe, anise, barberry, cashew, curled mint, Oregon grape, papaya, pomegranate, pumpkin, sweet basil, thyme, and turmeric.","Homeopathy: Cina — pinworms or threadworms; irritable, doesn't want to be touched, grinds teeth at night; child patient picks at the nose, jerking movements of feet and hands, may be pale with dark circles around the eyes. Caladium — worms in the vagina that can excite masturbation. Teucrium — pinworms, roundworms, threadworms; crawling sensation in the rectum and nose with itching, nervousness, irritability. Spigelia — worms with colicky pain around the navel and an itchy rectum, bad breath, pale face, blue rings around the eyes, nausea. Indigo — ascarides or threadworms with convulsions and intense umbilical pain. Sabadilla — tapeworms or pinworms with vomiting, colic, nausea, and itching of the nose or ears alternating with rectal itching. Stannum — worms with pale face, dark under-eye circles, fatigue, and passive fever; prefers lying on the stomach. Natrum phosphoricum — helps all types of worms; creamy/yellow tongue coating with heartburn and belching. Filix mas — tapeworms; irritable and anxious, dark circles around the eyes, itchy nose with swollen gland. Cuprum nigrum — removes all kinds of worms, trichinosis, and tapeworm; use small doses (1X); can alternate with Nux vomica; for tapeworms, use four to six weeks, four to five times per day. Others: calcarea.","Acupressure: ST36, SP6, LU7, LI11, LU5, K27.","Aromatherapy: essential oils that help eliminate parasites include oregano, thyme, fennel, tea tree, bergamot, peppermint, lavender, Roman chamomile, and clove."],"conditions":["Parasites, intestinal"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_peptic_ulcer","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Peptic ulcer disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Peptic ulcers are sores on the lining of the esophagus, stomach, or small intestine. Symptoms include pain that can be felt anywhere, worse when the stomach is empty and flaring up at night; the pain can be temporarily relieved by food or acid-blocking medication. Diagnosis is made via lab and imaging tests. About 70 percent of ulcers are due to H. pylori overgrowth; others are due to NSAIDs that damage the digestive lining.","Supplements (used alongside pharmaceutical therapy): continue to focus on gut-healing supplements, like probiotics and L-glutamine. Chamomile (1 cup four times daily) soothes the nerves and heals ulcers. Vitamin E (400 IU of mixed tocopherols daily) protects against and helps destroy H. pylori. Grapefruit seed extract (ten drops three times daily) has strong antimicrobial action. Zinc (75 mg twice daily) heals the lining and fights bacteria. Additional help for H. pylori: vitamin C (500 mg twice daily) helps eradicate infection and heal tissue; licorice root (500 mg before meals) may help stop H. pylori and heal the gut lining; aloe vera (500 mg two to three times daily) helps fight H. pylori; mastic gum (500 mg two to three times per day for sixty days); S. boulardii (5–15 billion CFU two to four times per day for sixty days). Supportive treatment: cabbage juice (4 oz daily for twenty-eight days). Others: citrus seed extract, goldenseal, activated charcoal, and gamma oryzanol. Sano-Gastril, comfrey, and calendula have also been shown to help heal ulcers. Children: juiced cabbage daily (may interact with cancer therapies), glutamine (500 mg twice daily).","Homeopathy: Nux vomica — heartburn about a half hour after eating, with constipation; attacks from excessive food, alcohol, stress/work; irritable, oversensitive. Pulsatilla — heartburn worse after rich, fatty food, in a warm room, better with fresh air; pressure under the breastbone, bad taste, nausea, weepiness, headaches around the eyes. Arsenicum album — heartburn with a stone-weight stomach sensation, anxious, restless, vomits/retches until exhausted, better sipping warm water and sitting up, worse mornings. Anacardium — indigestion one to two hours after meals, foul mouth taste, stomach feels blocked, improves with eating again, worse with cold drinks; forgetful; if peptic ulcer is suspected. Argenticum nitricum — indigestion after sweet foods, alternating constipation and diarrhea, belching, fluttery stomach; suspected peptic ulcer. Lycopodium — gas and bloating, full quickly, not improved by belching, anxiety, lack of confidence, constipation, worse in tight clothes and late afternoon/evening, better with warm fluids; suspected peptic ulcer. Phosphorus — burning chest sensations better with ice-cold water, then vomiting and nausea soon after drinking; likely peptic ulcer with 'coffee ground' or bright red vomit. Graphites — burning hunger pains relieved by food or hot drinks like milk, nausea after sweets; used for peptic ulcers. Kali bichromium — nausea and vomiting after beer, pain in a particular spot, distended stomach with cessation of digestion; used for peptic ulcers. Sulphur — diarrhea, burning pain, belching improved by ice-cold drinks. Others for ulcers: Nitricum acidum (fibroscopy shows a crack or fissure, itching/burning/cramping pain worse with fatty food and better with heat, fissures where mucous membranes meet skin) and Iris versicolor (dumping syndrome, intense burning pain with vomiting, oily diarrhea, and migraines). If nervous symptoms accompany the ulcer: Argentum nitricum and Arsenicum album.","Acupressure: the points listed for GERD are also beneficial for ulcers, specifically ST36, SP6, SP4. Others: B21 and CV21.","Aromatherapy: peppermint and frankincense can help with ulcers and can be added directly to water twice daily. Others that help an upset stomach: ginger, clary sage, and lavender. Relaxing oils: ylang-ylang, neroli, orange, lavender, rose, vetiver, and sandalwood."],"conditions":["Peptic ulcer disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gastro_sibo","chapter":"gastroenterology","title":"Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth — the Holistic Rx","body":["Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) is a condition where bacteria grow in the wrong place — normally the colon has far more bacteria than the small intestine. When bacteria overgrow in the small intestine, it can promote intestinal permeability. Symptoms include difficulty digesting meals, gas, bloating and abdominal distension (especially after meals or most of the time), odorous loose stools, abdominal pain, signs of leaky gut or chronic disease, or a diagnosed B12 and iron deficiency. Causes include antibiotic use, acid-suppressing medication, food poisoning, and low stomach acid, among others. SIBO can be confirmed with a lactulose hydrogen and methane breath test that detects excessive gas generated by overgrowing bacteria fermenting dietary sugars and starches; retest with another breath test within two weeks of finishing SIBO treatment.","Supplements: wormwood. Berberine-containing botanicals (like Berberis vulgaris). Caprylic acid. Grapefruit and other citrus seed extracts. Black walnut. Oil of oregano (150 mg, two capsules, three times per day for sixty days). For methane-producing bacteria, add garlic extract (two capsules, three times per day for sixty days). Others: goldenseal. Supportive treatment: peppermint tea (two to three cups per day). If regrowth occurs: prophylactic use of betaine with pepsin, low-dose naltrexone, low-dose erythromycin; Bifidobacterium lactis and Lactobacillus rhamnosus can help prevent recurrence.","Homeopathy, acupressure, and aromatherapy (oregano, thyme, and sage) are similar to the remedies used for bloating and other specific symptoms."],"conditions":["Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_dysmenorrhea","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Dysmenorrhea (painful periods) and endometriosis — the Holistic Rx","body":["A woman's reproductive system is often the first system to show signs the body is out of balance — irregular periods and pelvic pain are frequent early signals. Managing glucose effectively, healing the gut, and taming toxins lowers gynecologic problems and helps balance hormone levels. With heavy or irregular bleeding, focus on high-iron foods along with vitamin C, which increases iron absorption and triggers ovulation; foods rich in vitamin E, folate, and zinc also optimize reproductive health. The Four Big S's — staying away from stress, getting good sleep, having healthy social relationships, and cultivating a spiritual practice — all help lower cortisol and balance hormones.","Painful periods can occur due to numerous issues, including hormonal imbalances and endometriosis. Diagnosis is via history and examination, along with imaging like ultrasound or more invasive techniques. Endometriosis is when uterine tissue lands on pelvic tissue, leading to dysmenorrhea, painful stools, and painful sexual intercourse.","Supplements: vitex (chasteberry) (400 mg twice daily) balances hormones — don't take with oral contraceptive pills. Fish oil (1,000 mg daily); gamma-linoleic acid from evening primrose oil or borage oil (300 mg) can also help. B complex (50 mg daily). Natural progesterone cream (1/4 tsp days 6 through 26 of the cycle, stopping after the third month of pregnancy or the week of your menses) balances out estrogen. Milk thistle (150 mg twice daily). Others: indole-3-carbinol (300 mg daily) helps the liver detoxify estrogen; dandelion root (300–500 mg with each meal) helps liver detoxification; vitamin E (400 IU twice daily) helps with inflammation and estrogen metabolism; D-glucarate (500 mg daily) assists the liver in estrogen breakdown. Vitamin C (6–10 g per day), beta-carotene (50,000 IU per day), selenium (200–400 mcg per day), and lipotropics (1,000 mg choline and 1,000 mg methionine or cysteine three times per day) can also be tried.","Homeopathy: Arnica montana — deep, bruising pain. Belladonna — pain worse before the period, dragging or aching sensation, skin hot and flushed, blood bright red, symptoms worse lying down. Borax — pain with nausea and a muffled buzzing in the ears, pain extending into the thighs. Caulophyllum — pain with cervical spasms. Chamomilla — severe pelvic cramping, angry and restless. Calcarea phosphorica — tall women with scant, heavy periods and pelvic pain extending to the lumbar region. Cimicifuga racemosa — strong labor-like uterine contractions that worsen as flow increases, radiating across the pelvis into the thighs, with headaches leading up to the period. Colocynthis — cramping better lying with knees up and with pressure on the abdomen. Dioscorea villosa — uterine pain forcing the woman to arch her back, worse lying down, better standing. Gelsemium — scanty, late periods, heavy uterus with sharp pains soothed by heat. Ignatia — anxious, stressed, full of worry and sadness. Lachesis — anger, jealousy, suspicion; menstrual cramps with large purple clots and frequent PMS. Lilium tigrinum — hypersensitivity of the vulva. Magnesia phosphorica — spasmodic pelvic pain, cramping, and bloating soothed by heat, pressure, and movement; worse in cold air; membranes found in the flow. Murex — painful, heavy periods with clots. Nux vomica — prolonged, early periods with constipation, irritability, chilliness, and exhaustion; pain is spasmodic. Pulsatilla — menstrual pain with nausea, vomiting, and weeping, better when comforted, strong craving for sweets. Sepia — hormone imbalance; irritable, craves sweets, salt, and sour foods; pain with intercourse and pelvic heaviness. Staphysagria — painful periods that worsen with repressed anger or indignation. Viburnum — late, scanty periods with spasmodic pain into the front of the thighs, loins, and pubis, with faintness; nervous and agitated. For hormonal dysfunction: Folliculinum (30C on days 8 and 20 of the cycle), Cimicifuga (9C), and Lac caninum (9C, five pellets once to twice a day).","Acupressure: CV4, LV3, SP6, LI4 (pain anywhere). Others: SP10, GB31, ST36.","Aromatherapy: hot flashes — blend of bergamot, Roman chamomile (calms and soothes), rose, and geranium (increases well-being). Mood swings — clary sage, lavender, ylang-ylang. Dysmenorrhea — clary sage helps balance hormones and has been shown to reduce symptoms of endometriosis; angelica, chamomile, fennel, ginger, lavender, frankincense, and sandalwood can help reduce inflammation and support healing. Increased bleeding — cypress, frankincense, and geranium. Scant bleeding — basil, clary sage, and juniper berry."],"conditions":["Dysmenorrhea","Endometriosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_fibroids","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Uterine fibroids — the Holistic Rx","body":["Fibroids are uterine growths of connective tissue that can lead to heavy, prolonged periods and pelvic pain. Assessment is via history and examination/pelvic exam and imaging (ultrasound, MRI, hysterosalpingography, and/or hysteroscopy). Healing the underlying root cause and balancing hormones are key to improving symptoms.","Supplements: vitex (chasteberry) (400 mg twice daily) balances the estrogen/progesterone ratio — do not use with a birth control pill. Others: natural progesterone cream (1/4 tsp, days 6–26 of the cycle; if pregnant, stop after the third month) helps balance low progesterone; antioxidants, N-acetylcysteine, and other detoxification supplements (1,200 mg/day), like indole-3-carbinol (300 mg daily), dandelion root (Taraxacum officinale) (300–500 mg with meals, three times a day), and D-glucarate (500 mg daily). Vitamin E (400 IU twice daily) helps with estrogen metabolism and inflammation. Other herbal therapies: Scudder's, Fraxinus ceanothus, Turska's, and specific herbal formulas for specific symptoms (menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea, etc.). Warm castor oil packs placed on the abdomen can be effective to shrink fibroids and relieve pain, along with ginger compresses. Heavy bleeding can be controlled with uterine-toning herbs like black cohosh, red raspberry, and false unicorn root, or astringent herbs like cinnamon, yarrow, beth root, and lady's mantle, or oxytocic herbs like shepherd's purse — often found in combinations. The tonic and astringent herbs can be used twice a day for two weeks before the period, then shepherd's purse is added once bleeding begins; a combination of all three herbs can be taken every thirty minutes for up to six doses. Pain and cramping can be helped with herbs like black cohosh, boswellia, ginger, turmeric, cayenne, cramp bark, and mitchella repens, and enzymes like neprinol, bromelain, and wobenzym. B complex (50 mg daily, as B vitamins are involved in estrogen metabolism) and milk thistle (150 mg twice daily) help balance hormones and detox the liver.","Homeopathy: homeopathy has little effect on sclerotic fibroids, and large fibroids causing compression problems or bleeding should be managed conventionally. Aurum muriaticum natronatum (6C) — painful spasmodic vaginal contractions, uterus feels swollen. Calcarea carbonica — craves eggs and sweets, easily overwhelmed, fatigued, anxious; uterine hemorrhage present. Fraxinus americanus — a specific remedy for uterine fibroids in women with watery brown vaginal discharge, painful period cramps, swollen uterus with a bearing-down feeling. Phosphorus — bright red blood with clotting, craves ice-cold drinks. Lachesis — anger, suspicion of others, surges of heat aggravated by warmth; short, scanty periods as menopause approaches, abdominal/uterine pain improving once flow starts; abdomen sensitive to tight clothing. Sabina — lower back and sacral pain reaching to the pubic bones, with heavy uterine bleeding and clots. Silicea — bleeding between periods, flow heavier than usual, constantly cold. Sepia — irritable, wants to be left alone, craves salty, sour, and sweet foods; uterus feels like it will fall out with a bearing-down sensation. Sulphur — fibroids in women who overheat easily, desire a cool environment, strong craving for ice-cold drinks. Kali iodatum — uterus feels squeezed during periods. Calcarea iodatum — small fibroids with yellow, profuse vaginal discharge. Thlaspi — continuous bleeding.","Acupressure: SP10, GB31, ST36, SP6, LI4 (fibroid-associated pain).","Aromatherapy: frankincense, thyme, and clary sage can help. Two drops of frankincense oil, twice daily, on the roof of the mouth, and two drops of the other oils rubbed over the lower abdomen a couple of times a day, can help balance hormones naturally. Black pepper, rosemary, and marjoram can improve pain and increase blood flow."],"conditions":["Uterine fibroids"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_infertility","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Infertility (female and male) — the Holistic Rx","body":["If you have been having unprotected sex for at least one year and can't get pregnant, you or your partner may have primary infertility, or secondary infertility (inability to become pregnant or carry a pregnancy to term following the birth of one or more biological children). Causes span a broad range, from poor nutrition and environmental toxin exposure to hormonal issues or other medical conditions.","Female infertility supplements: vitex agnus-castus (chasteberry) (160–240 mg daily) helps stimulate the ovaries and balance estrogen and progesterone ratios. Vitamin B complex (50 mg daily) is important in estrogen metabolism. Vitamin E (400 IU daily). Evening primrose oil (1,500 mg once or twice daily from day 1 to day 14 of the menstrual cycle) supports fertility efforts. Natural progesterone cream (20 mg to the skin daily, started after ovulation and used under a doctor's guidance). Others: black cohosh; shatavari (600 mg of the root three times a day), an Ayurvedic remedy that helps promote reproductive health; tribulus (should contain at least 100 mg of the key active compound; can be taken three times daily on days 5 through 14 of the cycle); rhodiola (4.5 mg rosavins and 1.5 mg salidroside per dose, about two to three tablets per day) for anxiety and mental stress; ginger (ten to twenty drops in a warm cup of tea); and dong quai (tablet form, 1 g root equivalent, twice per day).","Male infertility supplements that improve sperm count, motility, and quality: L-arginine (2,000 mg twice daily on an empty stomach). Panax ginseng (300 mg), zinc (30 mg twice daily) along with copper (3–5 mg). Vitamin C (500 mg twice daily). Multivitamin. CoQ10 (200–300 mg per day). Vitamin C (500–1,000 mg per day). NAC (600 mg per day). L-carnitine (2,000–3,000 mg per day).","Homeopathy: Sepia (30C) — irritable, weepy, often cold, aversion to sex; irregular periods, womb feels as if about to drop out of the vagina. Sabina (30C) — previous miscarriages before twelve weeks. Conium (30C) — desire for sex suppressed for some reason, breast tenderness with areas of hard swelling. Lycopodium (30C) — dry vagina, tenderness in the lower abdomen over the right ovary. Agnus (6C) — best when a pituitary problem is diagnosed; take up to three times per day for three weeks for regular periods, stopping when the period starts.","Acupressure: ST29, SP6, CV4, SP8 (improve circulation and implantation). B44, LV14 (improve sex drive and libido). CV14 (improves secretion of cervical mucus). K16 (autoimmune-related infertility). CV6 (boosts libido, improves energy, relieves stress and menses pain, helps sleep). B15 (corrects cycle and hormonal imbalances). B23 (controls pain, improves discharge). B47, GV4. Others: ST36 (stimulates absorption), LU1 (relieves tension), Face Epang II.","Aromatherapy: essential oils that may improve fertility are ylang-ylang, thyme, and Roman chamomile. Rose oil is said to improve sperm motility."],"conditions":["Fertility and preconception"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_irregular_periods","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Irregular periods and abnormal uterine bleeding — the Holistic Rx","body":["A normally functioning ovary releases an egg every twenty-five to twenty-eight days; the uterus builds tissue in anticipation of implantation, and when that doesn't occur, it sheds the lining as menstruation. Regular, moderately pain-free periods each month are a sign that hormones are balanced. Irregular periods, missed periods, or very painful and intense PMS symptoms signal that one or more hormones are imbalanced. Diagnosis is made after a detailed history, exam, labs, and diagnostic tests. Causes of irregular periods include: high stress levels; a poor diet low in antioxidants and probiotic foods and high in sugar, hydrogenated fats, pesticides, or artificial additives/preservatives; extreme weight loss or gain; overexercising; thyroid disorders; PCOS; stopping the birth control pill; and food sensitivities or allergies. Balancing hormones is key to balancing irregular periods. Other signs of hormonal imbalance are infertility, weight gain or loss, depression and anxiety, sleep issues, low libido, fatigue, digestive issues, and hair loss/thinning. Specific problems associated with hormonal imbalance include estrogen dominance, PCOS, low estrogen, thyroid issues, diabetes, and adrenal fatigue.","Supplements for excessive menstrual bleeding: vitamin C (500–1,000 mg three times per day). Fish oil. Chasteberry extract. Bioflavonoids (1,000 mg per day). Others: chlorophyll (25 mg per day), iron (30 mg twice a day with meals), and chasteberry. For adolescents: first two weeks of the cycle, 1 tbsp flax and 1 tbsp pumpkin seeds; second two weeks, 1 tbsp sesame seeds, 1 tbsp sunflower seeds, fish oil (2–4 g EPA/DHA per day), pycnogenol (50 mg daily), and chasteberry (5% vitexin, 20–40 mg daily).","Homeopathy for amenorrhea (temporary or permanent absence of periods) — can be taken every twelve hours for up to fourteen days: Aconite — periods stopped due to exposure to dry cold, or stopped suddenly from an emotional event. Calcarea — fatigue, legs feel heavy, swollen and painful breasts, anxiety. Dulcamara — periods stop after extraneous exercise that leaves the woman cold. Natrum muriaticum — constipation, irritability, headaches, thinning hair, recent emotional shock. Ignatia — periods stopped because of loss or grief. Sepia — weak, irritable, weepy, with abnormal vaginal discharge. Lycopodium — lower abdominal pain, headaches, sadness, dry vagina; slight emotional upsets disrupt the cycle. Ferrum — weak, tired, pale face, occasional hot flashes, wants to sit down all the time.","Homeopathy for abnormal bleeding: China — intermittent bleeding with abdominal cramps, faintness, pale face, giddiness, dark clots. Sabina — dark blood, bad labor-like back pain, difficulty controlling weight, with or without clots, emotional upsets, increased blood loss. Belladonna — regular periods with bright red blood, throbbing headache, hot and flushed face. Ipecac — profuse bright red bleeding with nausea. Calcarea — cramping abdominal pain, back pain, head congestion, weight problems, general paleness. Ferrum — dark, watery blood with a pale face, improved with walking. Crocus — sensation of movement inside the uterus, feels sick, worried, and weak, expels blackish clots. Kali carbonica — exhaustion, cold extremities, thin watery bleeding without clots, feeling of dying, worse with warmth. Borax — profuse bleeding with nausea and pelvic/abdominal cramps, especially the first day or two of the period, worse at night.","Acupressure: LI4 (pain and inflammation), SP10, SP6.","Aromatherapy: basil can improve delayed menstruation and scanty periods. Cypress can relieve stagnation associated with heavy bleeding or midcycle bleeding. Geranium has an overall balancing effect on female hormones."],"conditions":["Irregular periods and abnormal uterine bleeding"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_menopause","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Menopause — the Holistic Rx","body":["Menopause is a time in a woman's life when sex hormones start to decline due to the degeneration of the ovaries. Women can experience a wide variety of symptoms, from irregular periods, hot flashes, and night sweats to mood swings, irritability, depression, anxiety, vaginal dryness, increased abdominal fat, and thinning hair. With these hormonal changes, there is increasing loss of ovarian follicles and a decreased amount of estrogen produced; this begins about six to twelve months before menopause, in the late thirties and forties, and continues throughout the menopause process.","Supplements: black cohosh (80 mg one or two times daily) improves hormonal imbalances. Natural progesterone cream (about 20 mg applied to the skin of the wrists and forearms two to three times per day from days 14 to 25; if perimenopausal, 20 mg three to four weeks of the month; postmenopausal, 10 mg three weeks of the month) helps with fibroids and vaginal dryness. Vitex (160–240 mg) regulates hormones — don't take with oral contraceptive pills. American ginseng (600–1,200 mg daily). Red clover lowers risk of heart complications and loss of bone density, and improves hot flashes, weight issues, and sleep troubles. Others: hops (250 mg two to three times per day) for anxiety with mild hormone-balancing properties; the Chinese herb rehmannia (25–100 mg daily) reduces heart palpitations, hot flashes, and night sweats; evening primrose, licorice root, red raspberry leaves, wild yams, sarsaparilla, and chaste tree; DIM (diindolylmethane) (75–300 mg per day), omega-3, calcium D-glucarate, probiotics, plant phytoestrogens, resveratrol, black cohosh, melatonin, and SAMe. Chondroitin sulfate can be used for incontinence. Hot flashes: sage (three cups of tea per day), black cohosh (20 mg for twelve weeks), omega-3, flaxseed, vitamin E (400 mg per day). Heavy periods: yarrow (two droppers of tincture every thirty minutes until bleeding slows). Fatigue: ashwagandha (500 mg twice daily). Vaginal dryness: kudzu. Low sex drive: Peruvian maca root (500–1,000 mg per day), ArginMax for women (ginseng, ginkgo, multivitamin, minerals), L-arginine (improves circulation). Mood issues: St. John's wort. Adaptogenic herbs: maca root (1,000–2,000 mg daily) to improve stress, decrease cortisol, reduce hot flashes, and improve energy and libido; also ashwagandha, medicinal mushrooms, holy basil, and rhodiola.","Homeopathy: Amylium nitrosum — sudden onset of hot flashes followed by a sensation of coolness. Belladonna — throbbing symptoms in the head, sudden flushing, profuse sweating, restlessness, heat, palpitations, headaches on the right side. Bryonia — constipation with blackish or burned-looking stools, vagina dry and thin. Calcarea carbonica — overwhelmed, anxious, fatigued; night sweats/hot flashes, cold, leg cramps, heavy menses, weight gain during the transition. Kali carbonica — loss of appetite, hot flashes, palpitations, nervousness, backaches worse around 3:00 a.m. Graphites — hot flashes especially on the face, cutting abdominal pains, scanty periods, weight gain, nosebleeds. Lachesis — hot flashes, anxiety, headaches on waking, dizziness, flooding during periods, sleep issues, memory/concentration problems, palpitations worse lying on the left side; talkative, suspicious, jealous, angry; constricted around the abdomen, must avoid tight clothing at the neck. Natrum muriaticum — hot flashes and vaginal dryness, cries easily, depressed, craves salt and cold drinks, migraines and backaches, worse in the sun. Oophorinum — a specific remedy for hot flashes in women who have had their ovaries removed. Pulsatilla — hot flashes, hemorrhoids, varicosities; fair-haired, often cold, cries easily, prefers open air, worse in a warm room, craves chocolate and sweets. Sanguinaria canadensis — hot flashes that redden the cheeks and burn the ears, radiating to the palms and soles. Sepia — heavy menstrual periods, backache, sinking feeling in the pit of the stomach, anxiety, pain during intercourse; can help with prolapsed uterus, incontinence, and vaginal dryness; exhausted, irritable, tearful, craves sweets/chocolate or sour foods. Sulphur — hot flashes worse after exercise and at night, easily sweats, craves ice-cold drinks. Lachesis, Sulphur, and Sepia, already mentioned as constitutional remedies, also cover hot flushes in their pathogeneses. Bone and joint disorders associated with menopause: Kali carbonicum and Natrum sulphuricum. Osteoporosis: Silicea and Calcarea carbonica. Nervous disorders: Lachesis, Sepia, Sulphur, Thuja, Ignatia, Nux vomica, Aurum muriaticum. Vaginal dryness: Sepia and Lycopodium depending on symptoms.","Acupressure: LI4, GV24.5 (improve headaches and hot flashes). GV20 (headaches, clearing the mind). CV17 (sleep, hot flashes). P6, B60, GB20 (irritability, dizziness, stress, hot flashes). LI11 (hot flashes). H6 (night sweats, anxiety). LV3 (headaches, insomnia, mood changes). K3 (night sweats, mood disorders). SP6 (balance). CV6, CV17 (hot flashes, mood disorders).","Aromatherapy: rub three drops of the chosen oil on the tops of the feet and back of the neck one to three times per day. Hot flashes: clary sage, peppermint, geranium, cypress, lime; thyme oil can help balance hormones. Depression: bergamot, clary sage, cypress, geranium, grapefruit, jasmine, lavender, marjoram, neroli, niaouli, sweet orange, petitgrain, rose, or ylang-ylang. Mood swings and anxiety: bergamot, cedarwood, Roman chamomile, clary sage, frankincense, geranium, jasmine, lavender, mandarin, marjoram, sandalwood, or ylang-ylang. Stress: Roman chamomile."],"conditions":["Menopause"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_pcos","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) — the Holistic Rx","body":["PCOS occurs when a woman's hormones are out of balance, leading to symptoms like weight gain and trouble losing weight, acne, irregular periods, thinning of scalp hair, extra hair on the face and body, depression, and fertility issues. Diagnosis occurs via history and examination, blood tests demonstrating insulin resistance and elevated testosterone, and imaging like ultrasound demonstrating ovarian cysts. Left untreated, PCOS increases the risk of developing diabetes, high blood pressure, high cholesterol, and heart disease.","Additional integrative modalities: the underlying insulin resistance driving PCOS is managed with the diet, supplement, and lifestyle protocol described in the Insulin Resistance, Prediabetes, Metabolic Syndrome, and Diabetes entry (Endocrinology chapter) — glucose control, gut healing, and lowering toxin exposure are the core levers for rebalancing hormones in PCOS."],"conditions":["Polycystic ovary syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_pms","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Premenstrual syndrome (PMS) — the Holistic Rx","body":["PMS is common in about 75 percent of menstruating women. Symptoms include breast tenderness, mood instability and emotional changes, bloating, fatigue, cramping, and skin changes. Symptoms can start seven to ten days before the menstrual flow and end shortly after. Diagnosis is via history and physical.","Supplements: vitex (chasteberry) (240 mg daily) to balance hormone levels — don't take with oral contraceptive pills. Calcium carbonate (500 mg twice daily) for PMS prevention. Vitamin B6 (50–100 mg daily). Zinc (15–20 mg per day). Vitamin E (200–400 IU per day). Magnesium (250 mg twice daily). Natural progesterone cream (1/4 tsp or 10 mg applied twice daily to thin skin like the wrists, from the beginning of ovulation until the day before your period). Indole-3-carbinol (300 mg daily) aids estrogen metabolism. Dong quai (Angelica sinensis) (300–500 mg twice daily, on the last seven days of the cycle) helps with breast tenderness and cramps. Others: vitamin A (5,000 IU per day); B complex (with 50 mg of B6); evening primrose oil (500 mg twice daily); 5-HTP (50–100 mg twice daily) to boost serotonin and lower anxiety; green tea or L-tyrosine (500 mg two to three times daily); ginkgo biloba (80–160 mg twice daily); hypericum perforatum/St. John's wort (900–1,800 mg per day). Preteen/teen: calcium (800–2,000 mg daily), vitamin B1 (100 mg daily), vitamin B6 (20–40 mg daily), fish oil (EPA/DHA 2–4 g daily), magnesium (100–200 mg daily), vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU per day), fennel extract liquid (thirty drops twice daily), ginkgo biloba leaf extract (50:1 ratio, 24% ginkgo heterosides, 6% terpene lactone, 80 mg daily), saffron (60 mg daily), valerian root (0.8% valerenic acid, 250 mg daily), and chasteberry (5% vitexin, 20–40 mg daily).","Homeopathy (take every twelve hours for up to three days, starting twenty-four hours before premenstrual symptoms): Calcarea carbonica — fatigue, cold sweats, anxiety, painful and swollen breasts; craves eggs and sweets, clammy chilly hands and feet. Matricaria chamomilla — unbearable, painful menses; irritable, angry, very sensitive to pain; better with motion, worse with warm applications. Cimicifuga — painful menses worsening as flow increases, cramping and shooting pains across the legs/thighs, headache, stiffness in neck and back. Causticum — irritable, pessimistic, frequent urination, symptoms of cystitis, colicky lower abdominal pain. Kali carbonicum — exhausted and tense, worse around 3:00 a.m., works best in overweight women. Lachesis — a major PMS remedy; symptoms worse in the morning with emotional issues (rage, jealousy, suspicion), painful breasts and abdomen improving once menses starts, irritated by anything touching the throat; headaches before periods aggravated by heat, sunshine, and alcohol, plus hot flushes; dosing schedule: Lachesis 9C on day 19 of the cycle, 15C on day 20, 30C on day 21. Lilium tigrinum — great irritability, crosses her legs for relief from a feeling that internal organs will prolapse, fresh air brings relief. Lycopodium — depressed, lacks self-confidence, irritable, digestive problems, craves sweets. Nux vomica — constipated, frequent urination, chilly, irritable, angry, craves coffee, alcohol, spicy or fatty foods; improves with warmth and rest. Pulsatilla — sudden bursts of tears, irritability, mood swings, painful breasts, irregular periods; improves with consolation and attention; craves sweets, especially chocolate; worse in warm rooms. Sepia — irritable, weepy, emotionally flat, feels uterus will fall out, breast tenderness, turned off by sex, craves salty and sweet foods; improves with exercise. Natrum muriaticum — fluid retention, swollen breasts, irritable and sad, lower back pain and migraines, craves salt, dislikes sun. Sulphur — for when the major PMS symptom is a craving for sweets. Specific symptoms: nervous symptoms — ignatia, Nux vomica, moschus, Murex purpurea, and cyclamen. Mastodynia — Lac caninum, phytolacca, Conium maculatum. Headaches — sanguinaria, Lac caninum. Venous disorders — Hamamelis, Vipera, Aesculus hippocastanum. ENT symptoms — Magnesia carbonica, Lac caninum. Skin issues/herpes — Rhus toxicodendron, borax, croton. Acne — Kali bromatum, Eugenia jambosa, Natrum muriaticum, sepia, sulphur, Calcarea carbonica. Water retention — Natrum muriaticum, Thuja occidentalis, Natrum sulphuricum. Hormone balance — Folliculinum (30C on the seventh or eighth day of the cycle).","Acupressure: LV3, SP4, SP6, GB34, ST29, B47, CV6 (improve pain), HP6. To relieve digestive complaints like diarrhea and constipation: CV6. For lower back pain: B25, B31, and B40. To ease tension and stress: LV3.","Aromatherapy: for abdominal cramps — black pepper and rosemary. To balance female hormones — geranium and rose oils. To reduce stress — geranium, rose oil, lavender, bergamot, or jasmine. To increase sexual desire — patchouli and ylang-ylang. Fluid retention — juniper compress."],"conditions":["Premenstrual syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_gyn_yeast_infection","chapter":"gynecology","title":"Vaginal yeast infection — the Holistic Rx","body":["Yeast infection is a candidal infection of the vagina. Yeast infections occur in areas where yeast and fungus can thrive, in moist conditions like the mouth, throat, and genitals. When yeast overpopulates in the vagina, symptoms can include vaginal discharge — usually thick, white, clumpy (cottage-cheese-like), and odorless — along with irritated skin around the vaginal opening, painful intercourse, and pain when urinating or during menses. Diagnosis is confirmed via examination and culture collection.","Additional integrative modalities: use the full antifungal diet, supplement, and topical protocol described under Candidiasis (Gastroenterology chapter) — the same overgrowth-lowering approach applies to vaginal yeast infections."],"conditions":["Candidiasis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_hemeonc_anemia","chapter":"hemeonc","title":"Anemia, iron deficiency — the Holistic Rx","body":["Anemia occurs when the blood doesn't have enough red blood cells to carry oxygen to your organs, so the body isn't getting enough oxygen. Types include anemia from a vitamin deficiency (B12 or folic acid), anemia of chronic disease (cancer, Crohn's disease, kidney failure), aplastic anemia (bone marrow can't make all three blood cell types), hemolytic anemia (red blood cells destroyed faster than the marrow can replace them), and sickle cell anemia (red blood cells form a crescent/sickle shape that can block blood flow, causing pain). Symptoms: fatigue, pale skin, shortness of breath, feeling cold, irritability, chest pain, dizziness/lightheadedness, palpitations or rapid heartbeat, and headache. Severe anemia can damage organs. Iron deficiency anemia is the most common type. Diagnosis is by history, physical exam, blood tests, and occasionally biopsy.","Diet and detox: heal the gut first, since appropriate digestion and absorption of B12, zinc, and magnesium depends on it. Nourish the spleen (responsible for red blood cell production) with squash, green leafy vegetables, and bitters like romaine and arugula. Eat iron-rich foods — beef/chicken liver, organic grass-fed meats, leafy greens, and beets — and remove processed and junk foods, balancing insulin levels. Foods rich in vitamin C help the body absorb and retain iron. Also helpful: blackstrap molasses, leeks, figs, eggs, cherries, strawberries, cashews, and brewer's yeast (rich in iron, folic acid, and B12). Cook in cast-iron pots or pans, since food absorbs minerals from the cookware. Juicing can sometimes make foods easier to digest and absorb.","Stress and worry can deplete the spleen and liver, so managing stress is part of the protocol.","Supplements: iron (50–100 mg of a well-absorbed form like ferrous fumarate, gluconate, or citrate), taken with vitamin C to help absorption. Vitamin B12 (1,000–2,000 mcg daily, methylcobalamin form, sublingual) and folic acid (800–1,200 mcg daily, sublingual) if your doctor has diagnosed a deficiency. Vitamin C (250–500 mg with each dose of iron). Green superfood powder — spirulina (2,000 mg daily, or one heaping teaspoon daily) — stimulates RBC production; choose one that contains spirulina, vitamin B12, dandelion, and folic acid. Other herbs — alfalfa, dandelion, burdock, and yellow dock (one capsule or twenty drops of tincture), plus gentian — improve iron absorption, help clean the blood, and have been used to treat anemia by stimulating the digestive system to more easily absorb iron and other nutrients; don't use with chronic pain or frequent urination, and be aware they may lower blood pressure.","Homeopathy: Ferrum phosphoricum for iron deficiency in someone with a pale face that flushes easily, a generally robust appearance, and oversensitivity. Calcarea phosphorica for anemia during growth spurts (the first two years of life or adolescence) or chronic anemia in adults, accompanied by poor digestion and irritability. China officinalis for anemia from blood loss or illness, in a patient who is usually cold, overly sensitive, and exhausted. Natrum muriaticum for anemia with a dull or muddy complexion, dry mouth and lips, headaches, and a tendency to cold sores and constipation. Picric acidum for anemia coupled with mental overload. Arsenicum for those with B12 deficiency.","Acupressure: SP6, ST36 (support circulation); LI4 (headache); CV4, SP10, LI6 (stress, headaches, or neck/facial pain).","Aromatherapy: ginger oil and citrus oils may improve iron absorption and anemia symptoms. Cinnamon oil supports blood sugar balance; ginger, eucalyptus, and black pepper can improve poor circulation. Ledum can be tried for liver cleansing or support. Others that help: lemon, thyme, chamomile, peppermint, and spikenard."],"conditions":["Anemia, iron deficiency"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_hemeonc_cancer","chapter":"hemeonc","title":"Cancer (general oncology support) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Cancer refers to diseases characterized by abnormal cells that grow and divide without control and can invade and destroy normal tissue. Tumors form when these abnormal cells form masses, but some cancers, like leukemia, don't form solid tumors. The immune system plays an extremely important role in defending against and removing cancer. Lowering inflammation gives the body a fighting chance and helps build a stronger, healthier you — one able to combat symptoms and side effects of cancer treatment and lower the risk of relapse, recurrence, or a second cancer.","Diet and detox: maintain an acid/alkaline balance by drinking alkaline water (add lemon or lime juice and/or hydrogen peroxide), increase raw vegetables, and remove grains and dairy to optimize detoxification. Increase omega-3 fatty acids, calcium, vitamin D (e.g. cod liver oil), blue-green algae, conjugated linoleic acid, mushrooms, folic acid (effective against breast, pancreatic, and colorectal cancer), melatonin, vitamin B6 (pyridoxine), and vitamin C. Low-carbohydrate diets with intermittent fasting help stabilize insulin, which improves estrogen dominance — tumor cells overexpress insulin receptors, letting more glucose in to feed the tumor, so managing insulin resistance matters. Fiber lowers circulating estrogen and estrogen metabolites that stimulate breast tissue, and lowers insulin and glucose levels that feed tumor cells. Anticancer foods: cruciferous vegetables, pomegranate, turmeric, garlic, green tea, oolong tea, olive oil, bean sprouts, strawberries, figs, olives, selenium, tomatoes, dietary fiber, ginger, onions, and quercetin (found in capers, cocoa, and hot chili peppers). Postmenopausal women should prioritize calcium and selenium. Keep BMI below 25. Intermittent fasting may lower cancer risk, but always discuss it with your doctor, since it should be avoided during active treatment or when weak. Men should avoid beta-carotene and take as little vitamin E and calcium as possible. Also avoid vitamin E, alcohol, grilling or cooking in a wok, and blood in meat.","Stress is a carcinogen, similar to environmental toxins — reducing it improves quality of life and has an inhibiting effect on tumor growth. Releasing suppressed emotions and replacing them with positive ones supports emotional health, since caging in suppressed emotions can contribute to illness. Strong social support, connecting to your inner intuition, and following your sense of calling and purpose all matter.","Supplements shown to help reduce cancer risk: probiotics (optimize the immune system), vitamin D (preventive effect on recurrence of malignancies, supports healthy bones), vitamin C (antioxidant activity), calcium/magnesium (bone health), omega-3 fatty acids (studies suggest biochemical and clinical benefits in breast, prostate, and non-small-cell lung cancer), mushroom supplements, turmeric (antitumor activity), and CoQ10 (antioxidant, may help in malignancies, especially in those receiving cardiotoxic chemotherapy).","Supplements that can be taken during and after cancer treatment, by symptom: stress, fatigue, and depression — levocarnitine, ginseng, royal jelly, brewer's yeast, maca powder, wheat germ, camu camu, zinc, and magnesium. Hair growth — brewer's yeast, B1, and B6. Weight loss — green tea, caffeine, common ash, and meadowsweet. Erectile dysfunction — vitamin D, L-arginine (2–5 g daily), pycnogenol (40 mg two to three times daily). Always check with your doctor about supplement–drug interactions before use: botanicals can decrease the concentration and efficacy of cancer treatment (e.g. echinacea, grapeseed, kava, St. John's wort, and possibly garlic), and ginkgo can increase toxicity of chemotherapy.","Homeopathy: cancer represents a profound breakdown at every level, so home prescribing generally isn't appropriate — for acute symptoms, a 30C potency four times daily may be used under guidance. Cadmium sulphuratum for vomiting, hair loss, and fatigue. Nux vomica for GI symptoms like indigestion, nausea, or constipation. Ipecacuanha for persistent vomiting and nausea, especially when vomiting doesn't relieve symptoms. Arsenicum for restlessness, burning pains, anxiety, and nausea. Radium bromatum specifically for radiation poisoning, especially when followed by arthritic complaints. Arnica montana for bruised or restless sensations. Eupatorium for aching bone pain that's worse with motion, often with stiffness and chills.","Acupressure: P6, LI4 (reduces pain); extra 1 acupoint (anxiety); LV4 (abdominal distention or pain). To support the immune system and liver: B23, B47, CV6, K27, and ST36.","Aromatherapy: combine bergamot, chamomile, and lavender for immune support, in a warm bath or for massage. By symptom — fatigue: rose, jasmine, or geranium. Anxiety or stress: lavender and Melissa. Pain: black pepper, rosemary, and ginger. Antibacterial/antifungal/antiviral: tea tree oil. Digestive issues and nausea: peppermint or ginger oil. Juniper can help break down toxins; others that help are cedarwood, frankincense, mandarin, sweet orange, and rose.","Nutritional protocols: supplemental nutritional programs discussed include the Gerson Therapy and juicing, the Budwig protocol, proteolytic enzyme therapy, vitamin C chelation, frankincense essential oil therapy, and oxygen therapy."],"conditions":["Cancer (general oncology support)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_nephro_ckd","chapter":"nephrology","title":"Chronic kidney disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Optimize kidney health by lowering inflammation, healing the gut, and optimizing glucose control. Drinking enough water will keep your kidneys working effectively and lower the risk of getting kidney stones. Eating a nutrient-dense diet can help balance the body's pH; these foods include fresh fruits and vegetables and vitamin E–rich foods (avocados, olive oil, berries, almonds, and butternut squash), which can help balance oxalate levels and prevent mucous membrane damage. You can also balance pH with lemon or apple cider vinegar. Consuming foods with magnesium and potassium can help balance calcium levels in the body (leafy green veggies, bananas, avocado, and melons). For kidney disease, follow your doctor's recommendations depending on your specific condition.","The Four Big S's — special considerations: regular stress management will lower inflammation. Exercise is great, whereas being sedentary can cause bones to release more calcium into the blood, which raises the burden on the kidneys."],"conditions":["Chronic kidney disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_nephro_kidney_stones","chapter":"nephrology","title":"Kidney stones — the Holistic Rx","body":["Kidney stones are stones present in the urinary tract. They grow slowly over several months or years and are made of minerals like oxalate, uric acid, and calcium. Symptoms include abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, fever, chills, excruciating pain in the buttock area with constant movement to try to relieve it, and pain in the genital area as the stone moves. Your doctor may confirm the diagnosis with a CT scan, ultrasound, and/or X-rays.","For stone prevention: avoid regular unsprouted grains and foods high in oxalic acid (spinach, tomatoes, collards, eggplant, beets, celery, sweet potatoes, almonds, blueberries, blackberries, strawberries, parsley, and cocoa) — these can increase oxalate in people prone to kidney stones. Elevated zinc can increase the odds of stones in those prone to it. Avoid too much vitamin C (more than 4 g per day), as it may worsen kidney stones. Caffeine and alcohol can make stones worse, since both are dehydrating. For uric acid stones, don't overindulge on meat and limit dairy. For cystine stones, avoid methionine-rich foods like soy, wheat, and dairy.","Supplements for prevention of calcium-oxalate stones: B vitamins like B6 (50 mg daily) help reduce calcium and oxalate levels. Magnesium (250 mg once or twice daily with meals) prevents calcium-oxalate crystals. Calcium 300–1,000 mg per day. Vitamin E (400 IU daily) can be taken in an antioxidant or multivitamin. Aloe vera juice/gel (1/4 cup daily) decreases urinary crystals. Inositol hexaphosphate (120 mg daily). Cranberry extract (400 mg twice daily of a standardized extract, or as directed on the container) helps prevent UTIs. Turmeric and ginger. Vitamin A (5,000 IU daily) prevents kidney stone formation. Others: Rumex crispus tincture. For uric acid stones: folic acid (5 mg per day). For magnesium-ammonium-phosphate stones: acidify with ammonium chloride (100–200 mg three times per day). For brushite and struvite stones: acidify the urine with cranberry juice.","Homeopathy: these should be given as often as necessary for up to ten doses while waiting for help — a higher potency is selected when the characteristics of the sensitive type are present. Calcarea carbonica is effective both as a remedy in renal colic and as a remedy of the chronic reactional mode. Belladonna is used for right-sided, sudden, excruciating pain worse with any jarring and accompanied by a flushed face and high fever. Berberis vulgaris is for sharp, stitching pain felt between the hip bone and lower ribs that radiates to the ureters, bladder, thighs, and testes; pain is worse with the slightest movement and relieved by lying on the painful side. Cantharis is for pain that feels like knives stabbing in all directions, leading to a burning sensation in the bladder, with thirst, food that seems nauseating, and an intolerable urge to urinate. Dioscorea helps when pain forces you to stretch or arch the back backwards for relief. Lachesis is for left-sided kidney pain that may cause hemorrhaging. Lycopodium is for right-sided pain that stops at the bladder, with back pain improved by urinating, clear urine with red sediment, and symptoms worse between 4:00 and 8:00 p.m. Nux vomica is for right-sided pain that stabs toward the genitals and down the right leg, with nausea and vomiting, or right-sided pain that shoots to the rectum with bladder urgency, in someone very irritable and cold-sensitive. Tabacum is for renal stones causing pain that radiates and darts down the ureter, with cold sweat and nausea. Calcarea phosphorica is for phosphate stones, with shortness of breath and fatigue. Pareira brava helps urogenital pain and helps prevent recurrence. Sepia is for those with enuresis and a tendency toward superinfections. Lithium carbonicum is best for uric acid stones and can be paired with Lycopodium and Calcarea carbonica. Solidago virga aurea helps pain in the lumbo-costal angles; Sarsaparilla helps those with stones and skin disorders. Benzoicum acidum is indicated in uric acid stones for patients with a tendency toward recurring UTIs whose urine smells strongly of ammonia.","Acupressure: B23, GB34, K3, LI4.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can help detoxify — beneficial ones are lime, lemon, orange, grapefruit, and helichrysum. To relieve abdominal pain: chamomile, lavender, and marjoram. These can be combined with a carrier oil and massaged into the lower abdomen twice daily, or added to a bath."],"conditions":["Kidney stones"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_general_brain_health","chapter":"neurology","title":"Brain health and neuroinflammation — the Holistic Rx (general)","body":["The gut and brain are intimately connected: lowering inflammation in the brain requires lowering inflammation in the gut and controlling insulin resistance. Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) allows the growth of new neurons; low BDNF is linked to a higher risk of Alzheimer's disease. Raise BDNF with turmeric, DHA omega-3, coffee, and coffee fruit extract. Other foods that support the brain include grass-fed butter (high in butyrate and BDNF-raising), other healthy fats, and probiotic and prebiotic foods like garlic, leeks, onions, and acacia gum.","The Four Big S's apply strongly to brain health: emotional stress and anxiety can trigger inflammation and the release of brain chemicals that provoke vascular changes and symptoms like migraines, so relaxation strategies and regular aerobic exercise are essential for optimal brain function."],"conditions":["Brain health and neuroinflammation (general)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_dementia","chapter":"neurology","title":"Dementia and Alzheimer's disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Dementia, including Alzheimer's disease (now referred to by some as \"diabetes type 3\"), causes problems with memory, behavior, and thinking that worsen over time and interfere with day-to-day living. Diagnosis is based on a detailed history, exam, and laboratory tests.","Supplements: resveratrol (100 mg twice daily), turmeric (350 mg twice daily, or a standardized extract at 1,500 mg daily), alpha lipoic acid (600 mg per day), and coconut oil (1–2 tbsp daily). Others: vitamin E (2,000 IU per day), CoQ10 (200 mg daily), ginkgo biloba (120 mg daily), phosphatidylserine (300 mg daily, shown to improve brain cell communication and memory and to help early-stage Alzheimer's disease), astaxanthin (2–4 g twice daily), huperzine extract (0.2 mg of huperzine A daily), bacopa monnieri (150–450 mg per day), acetyl-L-carnitine (1,000 mg three times daily, improves brain cell communication and memory), vitamin B12 (800–1,600 mcg daily), DMAE, L-glutamine (500 mg three times daily), magnesium, NAC (500 mg three times per day), NADH or coenzyme Q10, potassium (500 mg per day), SAMe (400–600 mg), vitamin C (500–1,000 mg in three divided doses), melatonin, increased B-vitamin doses, and jellyfish extract (10–20 mg daily of a standardized extract).","Homeopathy: Alumina for sudden worsening of confusion with memory impairment and constipation (30C daily for three or four days). Lycopodium for trouble recalling words with fearfulness (30C twice daily for two weeks). Calcarea for those who can't remember most words and show childish behavior and wandering attention. Plumbum for colicky, anemic patients with a nervous complaint and difficulty finding words. Anacardium for memory problems related to names, absent-mindedness, and a sense of two conflicting personalities. Sulphur for difficulty remembering names and words.","Acupressure: K1, H7 (improve memory), L7, P6, LU1, GV24.5, and ST36.","Aromatherapy: rosemary and frankincense support brain function. Lavender, ylang-ylang, orange, sandalwood, and rose provide a calming effect for dementia symptoms. For concentration and focus, use peppermint, basil, thyme, and cardamom."],"conditions":["Dementia and Alzheimer's disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_headaches","chapter":"neurology","title":"Headaches (tension, cluster, and migraine) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Headache pain can occur anywhere in the head or neck region. Types include tension headaches, cluster headaches, and migraines, with throbbing, pulsating, or pounding pain that may begin on one side and spread to both, lasting anywhere from four to seventy-two hours; nausea, vomiting, irritability, loss of appetite, fatigue, numbness, and sensitivity to light, noise, or movement may accompany the pain. Diagnosis is by detailed history and physical exam, plus imaging if needed.","Supplements: magnesium (200–600 mg per day), butterbur (50 mg two to three times daily with a meal), a B-complex combination of B2 (400 mg daily), folic acid (2 mg), B6 (25–75 mg), and B12 (400 mcg per day), plus additional B12, 5-HTP (50–100 mg three times daily), coenzyme Q10 (100 mg three times daily), and alpha lipoic acid. Others: feverfew (250–500 mcg of parthenolides daily), ginger root (fresh, about 10 g per day, or dried 500 mg four times daily, extract standardized to 20% gingerol) and shogaol (100–200 mg three times daily for prevention; 200 mg every two hours up to six times daily for an acute migraine), medicinal mushrooms, capsaicin cream, kudzu, and melatonin. For children — tension headaches: magnesium (200–400 mg daily), melatonin (3 mg daily); migraines: CoQ10 (100–300 mg daily), fish oil (EPA/DHA 2–4 g daily), vitamin B2 (40 mg daily), butterbur extract (15% petasins, 50–150 mg daily), feverfew (20–100 mg daily), and magnesium (400 mg per day).","Homeopathy: Aconite for sudden-onset headaches that feel like a tight band around the head, with apprehension, worse in cold or drafty surroundings. Arnica for sharp, bruised, achy headache pain aggravated by stooping. Apis for stabbing, burning, or stinging headache with a tender, bruised body sensation, worse in hot, stuffy environments. Belladonna for right-sided throbbing, congestive headaches beginning in the back of the head and extending to the right eye or forehead, with dilated pupils, light sensitivity, hot skin, and a flushed face, aggravated by hot sun, noise, and jolts, improved lying down in a dark quiet room. Bryonia for left-sided pain in the eye or forehead extending over the whole head, with constipation, nausea, irritability, wanting to be alone, and thirst; worse with movement, better with rest and pressure. Calcarea phosphorica for irritable school-age children with digestive aches and chronic headaches, intense pain in the back of the head, and cravings for smoked meats. Cimicifuga for headache with neck stiffness tied to hormonal changes during menopause or the menstrual cycle. Gelsemium for tight-band, mostly occipital headaches with neck pain, bruised-feeling eyes, a purple congested face, weak shaky limbs, fatigue, dizziness, blurred vision, and dilated pupils, improved by urinating and raising the head, sometimes preceded by visual disturbances. Glonoinum for violent, congestive, pounding headaches worse with head movement, stooping, and heat, improved by cold applications. Hypericum for a bursting or aching headache with a hypersensitive scalp, aggravated by foggy, damp weather. Ignatia for a tight band or nail-like pain on the side of the head with neck/back spasms, following trauma or emotional grief, worse from the slightest emotion or effort, better with distraction. Iris for constricted migraine headaches with nausea, bilious vomiting, and blurred vision, improved with movement, occurring on days off or at the start of vacations, with burning throughout the digestive tract. Lachesis for left-sided pulsating, burning headaches present on waking, with a flushed face, better in open air, worse from heat and touch. Lycopodium for right-sided temple or forehead headaches worse between 4:00 and 8:00 p.m., from hunger, overheating, or concentrating, with dizziness. Magnesia phosphorica to relax tight muscles and tension headaches. Natrum muriaticum for blinding, throbbing headaches triggered by sunlight, grief, or stress, improved lying down in a dark, cool room, sometimes with lip, tongue, or nose tingling. Natrum sulphuricum for headaches triggered by head injury. Nux vomica for bruising headaches triggered by food sensitivities, constipation, overwork, or stress, worse first thing in the morning and with noise/light, better on rising or with cold applications, with irritability, stomachache, nausea, and dizziness. Pulsatilla for a bursting headache worse in the evening or around menses, aggravated by rich fatty food, with easy tears. Ruta for a pressing headache aggravated by reading, improved by rest, with fatigue. Sanguinaria canadensis for congestive throbbing/burning headaches starting in the morning, peaking midday, improving in the afternoon, with cheek redness, pain moving from the back to the top of the head and settling over the right eye, improved by sleeping, releasing gas, or lying down, worse from motion, light, noise, or odors. Silicea for headaches beginning at the back of the head and located above the eye, with head sweats, improved by keeping the head warm. Spigelia for sharp, darting pain over the left eye, worse from stooping or sudden movement. Thuja for left-sided, nail-like piercing headaches.","Homeopathy for headaches with digestive symptoms: Lac defloratum for headaches with constipation, pain starting in the front and moving to the back of the head. Lac caninum for headaches that change sides. Kali bichromicum for supraorbital migraines with pain in one spot on the right side, yellow discharge, nausea, vomiting, gastritis, and visual problems, improved with pressure, worse from beer. Venus mercenaria for headaches with constipation, gas, and a coated tongue. Iris also applies here.","Homeopathy for headaches tied to emotion or overwork: Kali phosphoricum for headaches from being overworked and fatigued, with increased sexual desire. Phosphoricum acidum for a headache with heaviness and pressure, worse from motion and noise, better from rest and lying down, with an inability to concentrate, fatigue, and weakened memory. Zincum metallicum for headaches worse from intellectual effort and nervous fatigue, in pale individuals intolerant of wine or alcohol with decreased vision. Anacardium orientale for headaches with irritability and a sense of split personality in overworked individuals, improved by eating. Cyclamen for headaches related to menstruation, preceded by visual disturbances or dizziness, worse from coffee or open air. Actaea racemosa (black cohosh) for headaches proportional to menstrual flow with painful periods.","Acupressure: cluster headache — GB14, GB20, hold B2 with B10, fan fingers over GB6–11, end with LI4. Migraine — B10, TW16, GB12, GB6–11, GB14, LI4. Tension — GB21, GB20, GB12, GB6–11, B3–9, LI4. Sinus — GB20, GB17, SI8, LI20, ST1, B1, B2, GB14, LI4.","Aromatherapy: peppermint, lavender, eucalyptus, frankincense, rosemary, rosewood, grapefruit, coriander, lemongrass, bay laurel, lemon balm, marjoram, ginger, sandalwood, wintergreen, anise, basil, linalool, and Roman chamomile may all be beneficial."],"conditions":["Headaches (tension, cluster, and migraine)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_nerve_pain","chapter":"neurology","title":"Peripheral neuropathy (nerve pain) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Nerve pain causes pins and needles, electric shocks, and tingling in the affected extremity due to nerve damage. Causes include diabetes or prediabetes, other chronic illness, trauma, medication, toxins, autoimmunity, cancer, deficiencies (like B12), or infections such as Lyme disease or herpes zoster. Diagnosis is made via detailed history, physical exam, lab testing, imaging, and nerve conduction studies.","Supplements: alpha lipoic acid (300 mg twice daily) protects against nerve damage and lowers insulin resistance. Capsaicin cream (0.075%) applied up to four times daily. Evening primrose oil (360 mg daily) lowers pain sensations. Chromium (600 mcg daily) improves insulin sensitivity. Cinnamon (1–2 tsp with a daily meal). Vitamin B12 (500–5,000 mcg per day depending on lab values) and B6 (30 mg per day). Others: an energy revitalizing multinutrient powder, acetyl-L-carnitine (500–1,500 mg twice daily) to generate energy for healing nerve cells, topical Neuro-Eze rubbed into the area two to three times daily (most effective in early-phase small-fiber neuropathy), and magnesium.","Homeopathy: Mezereum for nerve pain worse with warmth and at night, best for ciliary neuralgia and pain tied to tooth decay. Hypericum perforatum for shooting pain with numbness, tingling, and burning. Lachesis for pain worse after falling asleep. Aconite for a congested, numb feeling in the affected part after cold exposure. Arsenicum for searing, hot-needle-like burning pain triggered by cold, with exhaustion, restlessness, and chilliness. Colocynth for lacerating pain improved by heat, triggered by cold or damp, especially facial neuralgia. Magnesia phosphorica for intermittent, sudden darting pain improved by pressure or heat and worse from cold and bending forward, especially of the head and neck. Ranunculus for pain above the eye or over the rib cage. Spigelia for pain above the left eye aggravated by any motion. Pulsatilla for acute, rheumatic facial neuralgia. Verbascum for pressure/crushing pain in the zygomatic bones, aggravated by talking, sneezing, chewing, cold air, or pressure. Platinum for constrictive, cramping pain with numbness and tingling, worse at night and at rest, with profuse, exhausting menses. Belladonna for infraorbital neuralgic pain radiating to the temples, ears, and back of the neck, worse around midnight, from noise, motion, chewing, or cold air, relieved by rest. Chamomilla for violent pain with numbness, improved by heat. Kalmia latifolia for pain that follows the course of a nerve, worse with movement and during the first part of the night. Aranea diadema for nerve pain after cold, damp weather exposure, with arm numbness that can wake the patient, a swollen feeling of the limb, worse at night. Others that can help: Allium sativum, Arsenicum album, Capsicum annuum, Carbolicum acidum, Dioscorea villosa, Dolichos pruriens, Gnaphalium polycephalum, Iris versicolor, Kreosotum, Mercurius solubilis, Phytolacca decandra, Sanguinaria canadensis, Selenium metallicum, Stannum metallicum, Staphysagria, Thallium metallicum, and Thuja occidentalis; for peripheral neuropathy specifically, Coffea cruda.","Acupressure: SI18, LI4. Lower extremity — K6, B62, GB30, GB34, GB39, SP6. Hands — LU10, LI4, SJ5, LI11, LI15, and the baxie points. Trigeminal neuralgia — LI4, ST25, ST44, TB17, GB20.","Aromatherapy: lavender, peppermint, and frankincense lower inflammation and dull pain. Others that help nerve pain: cajuput, chamomile, citronella, clove, geranium, marjoram, nutmeg, peppermint, rosemary cineole, sandalwood, yarrow, and St. John's wort–infused oil."],"conditions":["Peripheral neuropathy (nerve pain)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_parkinsons","chapter":"neurology","title":"Parkinson's disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["Parkinson's disease is a chronic degenerative condition of the nervous system in which the dopamine-producing nerve cells in the brain break down and die; because dopamine is a neurotransmitter that signals the parts of the brain controlling movement, this leads to movement impairment. Symptoms include tremor, loss of balance, stiffness and rigidity, poor posture, slowed blinking and movement, memory loss, mood disorders, dementia, and digestive and speech issues. Diagnosis is made via history, physical exam, and imaging.","Supplements: coenzyme Q10 (1,200 mg daily), vitamin C (750 mg four times daily), vitamin E (400 IU per day), and a green superfood formula with spirulina, chlorella, and wheatgrass. Others: inosine monophosphate (500–3,000 mg/day depending on uric acid levels), carnitine monohydrate (20 g for five days, then 4–10 g per day), caffeine pills (50–200 mg twice daily max, or one to two cups of coffee), NADH (5 mg twice daily), NAC (500 mg three times daily), melatonin (300 mcg–1 mg per night), proanthocyanidins (increase from 80 mg to 380 mg daily in three divided doses; don't exceed 380 mg daily), and vitamin B6 (increase from 75 mg to 150 mg daily; don't exceed 150 mg daily — contraindicated with levodopa), plus zinc (30 mg per day; large doses can interfere with mineral absorption, impair blood cell function, and depress immune function). Iron and manganese should be avoided.","Homeopathy: Argenticum nitricum for uncoordinated movement, imbalance, and hand tremor. Causticum for hand tremor worse with writing, with slow paralysis mostly on the right side. Helleborus for brain fog and slow speech. Mercurius for trembling hands and stammered speech with excessive sweating, patchy memory and concentration, loss of willpower, excess saliva, and a sweet metallic taste, worse from perspiration, sensitive to heat and cold. Natrum muriaticum for hand tremor, constant head nodding, and dropping things. Plumbum for progressive paralysis, cramping, and muscle wasting. Hyoscyamus for obscene behavior, jealousy, suspicion, restlessness, and muscle twitching. Agaricus for extreme spinal sensitivity with jerking, trembling stiffness of the extremities. Rhus tox for stiffness or cramping improved by movement and worse from dampness or immobility, with difficulty on first movement and little tremor. Gelsemium for staggering gait, tremor, and weakness affecting the eyes, tongue, and swallowing. Manganum can be used when no other remedy fits.","Acupressure: B18, B23, GB20, GB34, GV14. Others: GV14, K3, LV3, SP6, LV2, GV20, GV21, CV23, ST36.","Aromatherapy: frankincense oil reduces inflammation and vetiver oil can reduce tremors. Essential oils for muscle aches and pains — myrrh (promotes mental balance), lavender, cardamom, tea tree, clove, nutmeg, fennel, and peppermint — can also help."],"conditions":["Parkinson's disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_rls","chapter":"neurology","title":"Restless leg syndrome — the Holistic Rx","body":["Restless leg syndrome is a chronic neurological disorder causing unpleasant sensations in the legs and an uncontrollable urge to move them, typically occurring in the evening and disturbing sleep, with tingling, cramping, burning, throbbing, or creeping sensations. Diagnosis is by detailed history, physical exam, and lab tests. Primary RLS can have no apparent cause; secondary RLS can be associated with conditions like Parkinson's disease, peripheral neuropathy, diabetes, thyroid disease, fibromyalgia, anemia or iron deficiency, ADHD, nutrient deficiency (magnesium or folate), varicose veins, other chronic medical conditions, and pregnancy.","Supplements: iron (30–60 mg) and calcium (100 mg) on an empty stomach to bring ferritin above 60 ng/mL. Magnesium, vitamin D (400 mg), folic acid, and 5-HTP. Amino acid L-tryptophan. Sufficient iron, B vitamins, omega-3 fatty acids, magnesium, calcium, and trace minerals. To boost the brain's ability to make GABA, use taurine along with NAC (500 mg–2 g) and alpha lipoic acid (600 mg).","Homeopathy: Arsenicum album for chilliness, restlessness, anxiety, and a constant urge to move the legs. Phosphorus for those who take catnaps that steady the limbs, with twitching worse lying on the right side, especially in elderly patients. Zinc for twitching, restless, trembling lower extremities even while asleep. Agaricus for legs that twitch, tingle, tremble, and itch, waking the person, worse in cold weather or early sleep. Belladonna for legs that spasm and feel hot despite feeling cold, worse when trying to fall asleep. Ignatia for jerky legs after emotional trauma, with insomnia and despair of ever sleeping again. Kali carbonicum for jerking movements occurring mostly between 2:00 and 4:00 a.m. Sepia for restless legs worse during the day, improved with exercise. Tarentula for the irresistible urge to move the legs constantly, with twitching, jerking, and yawning.","Acupressure: CV6, ST38, GV20, GV26, B20, LV3, LV5, CV17, ST36.","Aromatherapy: cypress, rosemary, black pepper, ginger, lavender, marjoram, nutmeg, and cedarwood have antispasmodic qualities that can help. Others that can help: frankincense and chamomile."],"conditions":["Restless leg syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_neuro_seizure","chapter":"neurology","title":"Seizure disorder — the Holistic Rx","body":["A seizure happens when there is abnormal electrical activity in the brain that may come on suddenly, varying in duration and severity; recurrent seizures are known as epilepsy. Types include generalized (tonic-clonic and absence in adults; febrile and infantile spasms in children) and partial seizures. Symptoms range from muscle twitching to brief loss of consciousness. Causes can include stroke, brain tumor or injury, electrolyte imbalance, cancer, medications, or infection. Diagnosis is made via history, neurological exam, and testing including EEG.","Supplements: B6 (50 mg three times per day), folic acid (0.4–4 mg per day), and zinc (25 mg per day). Bacopa leaf (200–400 mg per day in two to three divided doses of an extract standardized to 20% bacosides). Others: sedative herbs such as chamomile, kava, valerian, and passionflower; manganese (10 mg three times per day), selenium (100 mcg per day), taurine (500 mg three times per day), and ubiquinone (100 mg per day). Avoid ginkgo biloba, Panax ginseng, St. John's wort (interacts with seizure medications), and white willow. For children: fish oil (2–4 g of EPA/DHA per day), taurine (500 mg three times daily), GABA (750–1,500 mg daily), B6 and B12, and melatonin (3 mg per day).","Homeopathy: Aconite for seizures brought on by fever or fright. Aethusa for episodes with teeth clenching, thumbs clenched inside the fists, drowsiness after vomiting or passing stool, dilated pupils, and a seizure that follows digestive symptoms or gastroenteritis. Belladonna for wide, staring eyes, a red, hot face, worse from excessive movement or jolting, useful for febrile seizures. Chamomilla for thumbs clenched inside the palms, one cheek red and one pale, green-yellow watery diarrhea, episodes triggered by anger or teething. Cuprum metallicum for a violent attack triggered by vomiting or menstruation, with a blue face, clenched lips and fists, and mental dullness. Glonoinum for a seizure triggered by heat or sun exposure, with fingers and toes spread wide and a hot, congested head. Ignatia for facial twitching with pallor, triggered by emotional trauma or upset. Zinc for irritability before an attack, head rolling side to side with fidgety lower-limb movements, and a pre-rash stage of infectious illness. Causticum for seizures triggered by emotional shock, with hopelessness and fearfulness. Cicuta for status post-seizure occurring after a head injury.","Acupressure: GV20, GV26, CV14, SI3, SP4, B15, LV1, B60, K6, LV3, ST40.","Aromatherapy: calming, relaxing oils can help relieve stress-related triggers — jasmine, ylang-ylang, chamomile, lavender, and clary sage. Frankincense oil is helpful for decreasing seizures. Avoid rosemary, fennel, sage, eucalyptus, hyssop, pennyroyal, tansy, thuja, wormwood, and spike lavender."],"conditions":["Seizure disorder"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ophtho_general_dd","chapter":"ophthalmology","title":"Eye health — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Your eyes are a window to your outer and internal world — the inflammation that may otherwise be silent in your body can be seen literally from deep within the eye, giving your doctor a glimpse inside. Optimize your health by lowering inflammation for a clearer window to the world.","Some of the best foods for your eyes are dark greens, carrots, green beans, red peppers, peas, nuts/seeds, berries, egg yolks, and wild-caught fish, as these contain antioxidants; eye vitamins like A, E, and C; and zinc with carotenoids like lutein and zeaxanthin that protect the eyes by lowering free radicals and relieving inflammation. Blueberries and cherries contain anthocyanin, which helps fight the free radicals that can damage your eyes and keeps the collagen around the eye flexible. Chromium (present in brewer's yeast) and magnesium both have beneficial effects on the eye.","The Four Big S's: relieving stress, improving sleep, and optimizing social and spiritual health are important to healing the overall inflammation behind chronic eye conditions. Avoid tanning beds, and protect your eyes from heat when taking a sauna. Use eye exercises and acupressure to keep them feeling comfortable, and massage the temporomandibular joint (TMJ), temples, and neck three times a day. Wear sunglasses and a hat for UV light protection."],"conditions":["Cataracts","Diabetic retinopathy","Dry eye","Glaucoma","Macular degeneration"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ophtho_cataracts","chapter":"ophthalmology","title":"Cataracts — the Holistic Rx","body":["Cataracts are clumps of protein that collect on the lens of the eye and are the most common cause of vision loss in people over forty; most develop slowly over time. Symptoms depend on the maturity and location of the cataract (cortical, posterior subcapsular, or nuclear) and can include blurred or double vision, halos around lights, fading or yellowing of colors, frequent changes in eyeglass or contact lens prescription, brown spots in the visual field, and increasing difficulty with night vision. Diagnosis is made via history and a detailed eye examination; some professionals believe cataracts can be prevented and delayed.","Supplements: a multivitamin (one or two daily) with B-complex (50–100 mg daily) — B2 and B3 are protective; vitamin C (1,000 mg three times daily, if tolerated); and vitamin E (400–800 IU daily with a meal), which helps with healing after laser eye surgery. Vitamin A (10,000 IU daily with a meal), an antioxidant. Others: riboflavin (3–5 mg per day), selenium, and bilberry (an anthocyanoside; 80 mg two to three times daily) to decrease free radicals.","Homeopathy: for temporary treatment, choose the appropriate remedy and take 6C three times a day for one week, then twice daily for up to one month; if vision continues to improve, take a two-day break, then resume twice daily for another month. Ideally, work with a homeopath to find a constitutional remedy. Calcarea can be taken as soon as circular lines are visible on the lens and a doctor confirms early-stage cataracts. Once symptoms begin, switch to a different remedy: Phosphorus for a sensation of mist or a veil before the eyes, or of something pulled tightly over them. Silicea for later stages that begin to interfere severely with sight. Ruta graveolens for red, hot eyes with dimmed vision. Digitalis purpurea for dim and double vision, with lid margins red, swollen, and agglutinated in the morning. Secale cornutum for hard or soft cataracts accompanied by headaches, roaring in the ears, and vertigo. Naphthalinum for a cortical cataract.","Acupressure: UB1 and UB2 at early stages; Yuyao, SJ23, GB1, Qiuhou, ST1, LI3 to improve circulation; LI4.","Aromatherapy: essential oils should never be put in the eye or on the eyelids. Clove oil, taken internally, is a powerful antioxidant that can help prevent and slow cataracts; lavender can also be helpful."],"conditions":["Cataracts"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ophtho_diabetic_retinopathy","chapter":"ophthalmology","title":"Diabetic retinopathy — the Holistic Rx","body":["Diabetic retinopathy is a complication of diabetes caused by damage to the blood vessels of the light-sensitive tissues in the retina. It initially causes no symptoms but can progress to blindness. Diagnosis is made via a dilated eye exam.","Supplements: a multivitamin with B-complex (50–100 mg daily), B12 (1–2 mg daily, if tolerated), vitamin C (1,000 mg three times per day), and vitamin D. Vitamin E (400–800 IU daily at mealtime). Vitamin A (5,000 IU per day). Magnesium (500–1,000 mg at bedtime). Zinc (30 mg daily for several months). Essential fatty acids (500 mg twice daily). Alpha-lipoic acid (200 mg twice daily). Lutein (6–10 mg daily). Zeaxanthin (0.5–1.0 mg daily). Chromium (50–200 mcg daily). Copper (2 mg daily while taking zinc). Bilberry (100 mg twice daily). Garlic (one odorless pill daily). Others: quercetin (1,000 mg daily), vanadyl sulfate (20 mg daily for two weeks, then 2 mg daily), phosphatidylinositol (PI), Gymnema sylvestre (50 mg per day), and fenugreek (15 mg per day).","Homeopathy: Arnica as soon as floaters in the eye are seen. Conium if Arnica doesn't stabilize the condition within twelve hours. Lachesis to help prevent further leakage of blood vessels in the retina once the condition has been stabilized (can be taken every four hours for up to six doses).","Acupressure: similar to general eye-health acupressure points combined with diabetic acupressure points.","Aromatherapy: cinnamon oil and coriander oil can help relieve diabetes by supporting the pancreas, improving weight loss, and aiding general diabetes management and prevention; coriander oil enhances the secretion of insulin from the pancreas and exhibits insulin-like activity at the cellular level."],"conditions":["Diabetic retinopathy","Diabetes mellitus, type 2"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ophtho_dry_eye","chapter":"ophthalmology","title":"Dry eye — the Holistic Rx","body":["Dry eye occurs when a person doesn't have adequate tears to lubricate and nourish the eyes, from an imbalance of tear production and drainage. Causes include hormonal changes, medications, and medical conditions like rheumatoid arthritis, Sjögren's, diabetes, thyroid disease, and inflammation of the eyelids; environmental factors like smoke, dry or windy climates, and even LASIK surgery can also contribute. Diagnosis is made via an eye exam and complete history, along with measuring tear volume and production using the Schirmer's test.","Supplements: fatty acids (1,000 mg DHA and 500 mg EPA once or twice a day) and fat-soluble vitamins like A (20,000 IU daily for the first month, then 10,000 IU daily) and E (400–800 IU daily with a meal). Magnesium (500 mg at bedtime). B-complex (50–100 mg daily) with B12. Vitamin C (1,000 mg three times daily, if tolerated). Calcium (750 mg daily, at a different time from magnesium). Zinc (30 mg daily for several months). Copper (2 mg daily while taking zinc). Chondroitin and glucosamine sulfate (100–500 mg daily).","Homeopathy: Alumina for dry eye when objects look yellow and the eyes feel cold. Belladonna for a very hot, red, or swollen eye with dilated pupils. Bryonia alba for dryness associated with eye pain. Nux moschata for dry eye when objects look larger, look very distant, or vanish, and the patient is unable to open the eyes.","Acupressure: UB1, ST1, GB1.","Aromatherapy: castor oil and flaxseed oil can help with dry eye."],"conditions":["Dry eye"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ophtho_glaucoma","chapter":"ophthalmology","title":"Glaucoma — the Holistic Rx","body":["Glaucoma is a slow-progressing chronic disease causing a build-up of fluid in the eye that can lead to optic nerve, retinal, and lens damage; it is the second leading cause of blindness. There are no early symptoms, but as the disease progresses side vision is lost, followed by central vision loss; other symptoms include blurry vision, rainbow-colored halos around lights, red eye, headache, nausea, vomiting, and pain. Diagnosis is made by an eye care professional with visual acuity, visual field, pupil dilation, and tonometry tests.","Supplements: B-complex (50–100 mg daily) with B12. Fish oil (600 mg EPA and 400 mg DHA daily) helps relieve intraocular pressure. Magnesium (500–1,000 mg daily) helps relax the blood vessel wall and improve blood flow. Vitamins A and D (unless already in a multivitamin). Vitamin C (1,000 mg three times daily). Vitamin E (400–800 IU daily, taken with a meal). Zinc (30 mg daily for several months). Copper (2 mg daily while taking zinc). Alpha-lipoic acid (200 mg daily), an antioxidant shown to improve vision for some people with this disease. Lutein (6–10 mg daily). Garlic (one odorless pill daily). Others: bilberry (160 mg twice daily); astaxanthin (2 mg per day); CoQ10 (300 mg daily) to help prevent free radical damage; Ginkgo biloba extract to improve circulation (fifteen drops twice daily); Coleus forskohlii; Trifola.","Homeopathy: Physostigma venenosum for open-angle glaucoma with muscle spasms and neurological problems. Phosphorus for glaucoma with vertigo. Spigelia for sharp pains. Belladonna (30C) for glaucoma with blurred vision and pain in one eye that is made worse by bright light.","Acupressure — especially helpful for people who use their eyes a great deal and suffer from eyestrain: ST3 relieves pressure on the eyes; UB10 soothes tired eyes and headaches; LI3, LI4, Cuan Zhu, and B1.","Aromatherapy: frankincense and helichrysum oil can improve eyesight; cypress oil improves circulation, which can relieve glaucoma; lavender, rose, sandalwood or ylang-ylang, bergamot, and jasmine can help reduce anxiety and tension.","Other recommendations: avoid eyestrain for long periods of time, don't watch TV in the dark, and avoid drugs that jeopardize the eye."],"conditions":["Glaucoma"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_ophtho_macular_degeneration","chapter":"ophthalmology","title":"Macular degeneration — the Holistic Rx","body":["Macular degeneration is an age-related chronic illness and the leading cause of vision loss in people over sixty in the United States. It affects the macula, the part of the retina responsible for central vision, leading to blurred vision, a blind spot in the center of vision, and difficulty with reading and everyday tasks in one or both eyes; often the first symptom is needing more light to see up close. Dry AMD (age-related macular degeneration, the more common type) is gradual degeneration of the macula causing gradual loss of central vision; wet AMD involves blood vessels growing under the retina that leak fluid and blood, causing a sudden loss of central vision. Symptoms of dry AMD include blurring of print when reading, haziness of vision, and a blurred spot in the central visual field that may grow larger and darker; symptoms of wet AMD include sudden loss of central vision, a sudden blind spot, objects appearing farther away or smaller than usual, and straight lines appearing wavy. Diagnosis is via a visual field test and an Amsler grid (covering one eye and staring at the black dot in the center of a checkerboard-like grid — wavy or missing lines can point to macular degeneration), plus fluorescein angiography and optical coherence tomography as needed.","Supplements: a multivitamin (one or two capsules as directed with a meal) with vitamin A (10,000 IU per day) and D, magnesium (500 mg daily), fish oil DHA (500 mg twice daily), selenium (50–200 mcg daily), taurine (200 mg daily), B-complex (50–100 mg per day), B12 (1–2 mg per day), chromium (200 mcg daily), zinc (30 mg or less daily), and vitamin E (400–800 IU daily with meals). Digestive enzymes as required. Vitamin C (1,000 mg, one to three times daily, if tolerated). Garlic (one odorless 1,000 mg pill daily). CoQ10 (60–120 mg daily). Ginkgo biloba extract (fifteen drops once or twice daily). Beta-carotene (5,000–10,000 IU daily). Lutein (6–20 mg daily with a meal), an antioxidant that protects the eyes and skin and prevents oxidative damage of the macula. Zeaxanthin (0.5–1.0 mg daily with a meal) protects the eye tissues (lens and macula) for clear vision, prevents oxidative damage, and prevents glare and light sensitivity. Bilberry (100 mg twice daily), an antioxidant. Copper (2 mg daily while using zinc). Others: astaxanthin (2 mg daily) to help prevent retinal damage.","Homeopathy: a constitutional remedy can be helpful for macular degeneration, matched to the person's symptoms, so seeing a licensed homeopath is important.","Acupressure: ST36 improves digestion and nutrition absorption; GB37 improves vision and relieves watery eyes; GB39, LI3, LI4, UB1, GB20, Tai yang, P6, LV2, SP6.","Aromatherapy: frankincense oil has been shown to improve eyesight, cypress oil can improve circulation, and helichrysum can improve vision and support nerve tissue function — three drops can be applied twice daily to the lateral area of the eye, but never directly into the eyes.","Other recommendations: test yourself regularly with the Amsler grid. Eliminate unnecessary medication, as some medications may deplete essential nutrients, vitamins, and minerals and accelerate macular degeneration. Optical devices that enlarge visual images and other low-vision aids are available as needed."],"conditions":["Macular degeneration"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_general_dd","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Pediatric foundations and gut health (general) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Diet and detox special considerations: educate children early about food and the \"good bugs\" living in their belly, and teach them to prioritize vegetables, clean protein, and healthy fat at every meal. Breast milk is the most perfect food for babies, supporting a healthy gut microbiome; if a baby doesn't seem to tolerate breast milk, look for food sensitivities behind colic, gas, or reflux. If breastfeeding isn't possible, use a natural organic formula and supplement with fish oil and probiotics, following a proper weaning schedule. Limit juice, which displaces nutrient-rich foods and lowers immunity, and limit screen time in favor of outdoor play — the microbes that live in soil increase biodiversity for optimal health and happiness.","Watch for subtle signs of imbalance — colic, gas, spitting up, indigestion, congestion, rash or hives, cough, difficulty breathing, constipation or other digestive issues, chronic ear infections, cradle cap, projectile vomiting, tics, sinus issues, or adenoids — as risk factors for bigger problems down the road like autism, autoimmune disease, or mood disorders. Remove one or two problematic foods at a time, starting with the food the child eats the most, following the Gut Rx; breastfeeding mothers can eliminate the same foods and watch for symptoms in their baby. For serious issues like colitis, autoimmunity, seizures, or autism, a gut-healing diet such as GAPS (Gut and Psychology Syndrome Diet), combined with avoiding food sensitivities and the foundational good-health principles, is a good starting point. Add supplements slowly and gradually — some children with autism may regress on certain supplements like B12 or GABA/glutamate — and children with folate deficiency should avoid all dairy, including camel's milk.","Clean up the child's surroundings: pesticides and other environmental toxins can cause neurodevelopmental damage and disrupt the endocrine system. Heavy metal detoxification is important for improving autism by helping the body maximize glutathione, and optimizing gut health while limiting daily toxin exposure helps protect against adverse vaccine effects; homeopathics can also be used to help prevent or limit vaccine side effects.","The Four Big S's — special considerations: managing stress is essential to any healing and disease-prevention program, since a child's thoughts, feelings, and emotions directly affect physical health, and parenting itself shapes a child's brain chemistry. Loving, respectful bonds activate a sensory nerve that releases oxytocin and natural opioids, lowering stress-response systems and supporting brain development — get down and cuddle, play, make eye contact, laugh, read together, and offer imaginative, exploratory activities. Establish family rules and clear boundaries to lower everyone's stress and help children feel secure; keep communication open, avoid judging or negating feelings, and discipline in ways that preserve the child's dignity. Older children can reduce stress with abdominal breathing or mindfulness practices such as meditation, prayer, yoga, and tai chi. Keep proper bedtimes so the adrenal glands don't go into overtime, and build gratitude, humor, optimism, and resilience through daily practice — be honest about your own stress and emotions, since children are like sponges who sense even subtle changes in a parent's mood or behavior.","Supplements and botanical safety: most children should be taking fish oil, vitamin D, or cod liver oil, and probiotics to optimize health and prevent illness, especially if these aren't coming from the diet already. Botanical medicines NOT to be used in infants and toddlers: aloe vera (under ten years of age), buckthorn bark, cajeput oil, camphor, eucalyptus, fennel oil, horseradish, mint and peppermint oils (external application), nasturtium, rhubarb root, senna, and watercress.","Homeopathy is an excellent, effective choice for children, using the same remedy types and dosages as for adults. Acupressure: adult points also work for children and can be given while massaging, bathing, dressing, or nursing — infants respond quickly, so use only ten- to fifteen-minute sessions with a small circular motion and light pressure held for ten to thirty seconds at a time; stretching isn't required for infants. Colic points: LI2, B47, K3, HP3, LV3. Teeth pain: TW5, LU9, LI4, LI11, ST3, SI18. Sleep: GB20, SP4, SP6, H5, HP4. Pain: LI4, LV3. Digestion and nausea: P6, ST36. Anxiety: GV20.","Aromatherapy is very beneficial for children — essential oils are potent, so only a small amount is needed, and they should be well diluted in a carrier oil since a child's skin is thinner. Teething: clove and grapefruit oils. Temper tantrums: ylang-ylang, rose. Colic: lavender, roman chamomile, fennel, marjoram, bergamot, ylang-ylang. Age-appropriate essential oils — three days to three months: Roman chamomile, lavender, mandarin. Three months to five years: the above plus bergamot, cedarwood, eucalyptus smithii, frankincense, geranium, ginger, lemon, sweet marjoram, sweet orange, rose, rosemary, sandalwood, tea tree, and thyme linalool. Five years to puberty: all oils considered safe for adults are safe for children, but used in smaller amounts.","Bodywork: massage and chiropractic/osteopathic manipulation are very beneficial for children, stimulating hormone release that supports development, deepens bonding, and improves circulation and healing. Hydrotherapy is also very healing for a child."],"conditions":["Pediatric foundations and gut health (general)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_autism","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Autism spectrum disorder — the Holistic Rx","body":["Autism is a neurobehavioral disorder that presents early in childhood in varying degrees, with symptoms that may include social challenges, communication difficulties, repetitive behaviors, sensory processing problems, sleep problems, seizure disorders, and other associated medical issues. Diagnosis is via detailed history and professional assessment by a developmental pediatrician or other qualified professional, but getting to the root causes of the symptoms requires additional testing.","Testing: start with the basic workup (see chapter 4, Adjusting the Holistic Rx for Your Condition). Common deficiencies seen in children with autism include calcium, selenium, zinc, magnesium, iron, cysteine, sulfate, taurine, B12, B6, lysine, methionine, essential fatty acids, and vitamins D, E, and A. Consider: red blood cell or whole-blood minerals (zinc, copper), urine organic acids, amino acids, essential fatty acids, fat-soluble vitamins, reduced glutathione, lipid peroxides, plasma cysteine, plasma sulfate, CSDA, food allergy testing, immune testing (IgA, IgM, IgG, IgG subclasses, lymphocyte subsets, natural killer cell activity, vaccine titers, viral titers), thyroid antibodies, anti-myelin basic protein (MBP) antibodies, folate receptor antibody test, PANDAS profile (ASO titer, anti-DNase B titer), provoked urine testing for heavy metals, urine porphyrins and genetic polymorphisms, mold testing, and testing for chronic viral/bacterial infection (see the Autoimmune Disease chapter) — start with viral IgG/IgM titers to HHV1, HHV2, HHV6, CMV, and Epstein-Barr, and consider a Lyme panel, ESR, hsCRP, platelets, streptococcus markers (ASO titers and anti-DNase antibodies), stool cultures, urine cultures, and urine organic acid testing.","Supplements: fish oil (1,000–3,000 mg EPA/DHA combined); probiotic; cod liver oil with vitamin D (2,000–5,000 IU daily with meals); digestive enzyme (one to two capsules with each meal); B complex with B12 (1,000 mcg daily) and B6 (0.6 mg/kg); multivitamin with zinc (10–20 mg daily), selenium (50–100 mcg daily depending on weight), calcium and magnesium (6 mg/kg), vitamin E (100–400 IU daily) with an essential amino acid combination, and vitamin C.","Detoxification: antioxidants such as vitamin C (1,000 mg/day), vitamin A (5,000 IU/day minimum), NAC (600 mg daily), selenium (100 mcg daily), transdermal glutathione (100–200 mg twice daily), and melatonin (1 mg/day). To support methylation: methyl-B12 injections (check with an integrative practitioner first), raising glutathione (IV, transdermal, or nebulized), folic acid, TMG/trimethylglycine (start with 250 mg twice daily — side effects include hyperactivity and more emotional behavior) or DMG/dimethylglycine, and NAC (oral NAC can cause yeast overgrowth in some children, so give it transdermally at 100–200 mg twice daily, up to 2,000 mg/day if needed).","To reduce behaviors commonly seen in autism such as sensory defensiveness, sleeplessness, eye aversion, stimming, and scripting: tyrosine, TMG, GABA, NADH, tryptophan (100 mg daily), essential amino acid combination (500–2,000 mg daily), GABA (500–1,000 mg three times daily). Treat infections with oregano oil, olive leaf extract, garlic, lauricidin, and caprylic acid. Others to discuss with your physician: taurine, coenzyme Q10, iron (especially if deficient, 10–80 mg elemental iron/day), chromium (50–100 mcg daily), vitamin K, L-carnitine (250–500 mg daily), DMAE (100–500 mg/day), silymarin, 5-HTP, activated charcoal, pantothenic acid (B5), phosphatidylcholine, oral gamma globulin, pycnogenol, quercetin, curcumin, creatine (750 mg to several grams/day), carnosine (400 mg twice daily or as directed — improves neurological function), and SAMe. BH4/tetrahydrobiopterin (1–2 mg/kg body weight) can be tried for treatment. Melatonin (1–3 mg/day, increasing weekly by 1–2 mg if not effective) helps with sleep issues.","To limit die-off, use Epsom salt baths for detoxification support; start one new supplement at a time, giving three to four days before adding another, and start at half the target dose for one to two days to check tolerance. Keep a diary and watch for patterns of behavior change. Treat hyperactivity or inactivity per the Psychiatry chapter, poor sleep as for insomnia, and digestive issues per the Gastroenterology chapter.","Homeopathy: finding a constitutional remedy is key — work with a licensed homeopathic practitioner for a personalized plan. Chamomilla suits children who repeatedly bang their heads on the wall, get upset with noise, lash out when approached, and are restless and irritable. Silicea suits those with night head sweats, who count things over and over on the floor, and who have sweaty, smelly feet. Hyoscyamus suits children who go into fits of laughter, mutter, play with their genitals, and are suspicious of unfamiliar objects. Agaricus suits those who are fearless, mutter and sing, talk but don't answer, move their head constantly, and have double vision. Cuprum metallicum suits tense, aggressive, obsessive, inflexible, irritable children with tics and hand-banging behavior.","Acupressure: traditional Chinese medicine points can help with behavioral problems in autism by addressing specific energy points and pathways — see a licensed practitioner for a personalized plan. Aromatherapy: lavender oil for calmness; frankincense oil to support the brain; vetiver essential oil to help balance brain waves; sandalwood and cedarwood can also help.","Other therapies: hyperbaric oxygen therapy (begin the supplement protocol and detox first; weigh the risks — fatigue, lightheadedness, ear pain, or even serious lung complications, mostly at high duration or pressure — against the benefits); infrared sauna; chelation detox; allergy desensitization."],"conditions":["Autism spectrum disorder"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_otitis_media","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Otitis media (ear infections) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Ear infections (otitis media) occur when fluid becomes trapped in the middle ear, leading to earache and thick drainage from the ear; other symptoms include fever, loss of appetite, digestive issues, irritability, or trouble sleeping. Infections can be viral or bacterial, usually arising from swelling of the eustachian tubes after an upper respiratory infection or an allergy that traps fluid, which then becomes infected. Diagnosis is by detailed history and exam.","Supplements: fish oil; garlic oil, or a garlic/mullein or probiotic mixture in olive oil, a few drops in each ear; zinc (10 mg twice daily for children older than two); vitamin C (500 mg twice daily for children six to twelve); echinacea (2 mL four times daily for children); vitamin D3 (400–2,000 IU daily for children two to twelve). Others: vitamin A (2,000–5,000 IU daily for up to five days, for children up to six years old); goldenseal (2 mL four times daily for children); or larix (one to two teaspoons for infants, four times a day for older children).","Homeopathy: read through the remedies below and match the one that best fits the presenting symptoms; give 6C, 15C, or 30C once or twice daily, or 30C hourly up to six doses for an acute earache. Children may continue the remedy as long as symptoms persist and stop once they improve. Aconitum napellus for the first six hours of an ear infection, with bright red ears and violent pain with increased thirst, often after cold, dry-wind exposure. Belladonna for sudden-onset earache with staring eyes, a hot flushed face, fever, cold feet, unusual sensitivity to touch, and excited, incoherent behavior — the right ear more affected than the left. Chamomilla for the very irritable, angry, screaming/crying child who wants to be carried, with the ear infection often tied to teething and one cheek red while the other is pale. Hepar sulphuris when pus or drainage is present, the child is irritable, the ears are sensitive, and sharp pain improves with warmth. Pulsatilla for the weepy, feverish child with pushing-out pressure pain and yellow-green nasal discharge, worse in warmth and better with cold applications or open air, who wants to be held and comforted. Lachesis when the left ear is infected or the infection moves from left to right, improved at night and with warmth. Lycopodium for right-sided ear infections in an irritable, gassy child with digestive upset, improved with warmth and worse between 4:00 and 8:00 p.m. Mercurius solubilis (or vivus) for earache with smelly drainage, increased sweat, bad breath, and increased salivation, worse at night with a thick coating on the tongue. Silicea for chronic ear infections, or for acute infections in thin, cold-sensitive children (especially sensitive to wind); also useful for eardrum perforation. Ferrum phosphoricum for those who have fever but don't act sick.","Acupuncture and acupressure: TW21, SI19, GB2, TB17, K3 help ease pain. Aromatherapy: tea tree oil has powerful antiseptic properties; others that are beneficial are basil, cajeput, roman chamomile, eucalyptus radiata, lavender, and rosemary, massaged onto the outer ear."],"conditions":["Otitis media"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_pandas","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"PANDAS — the Holistic Rx","body":["PANDAS (pediatric autoimmune neuropsychiatric disorders associated with streptococcus) is diagnosed when motor and/or vocal tics, obsessions and/or compulsions, moody or irritable behavior, separation anxiety, and anxiety attacks appear suddenly or worsen following a streptococcal infection. It usually affects children from about age three to puberty. Diagnosis is via history, physical exam, and laboratory testing (see the autism testing list above, including a PANDAS profile with ASO titer and anti-DNase B titer).","Supplements: vitamin D3, B12, calcium, magnesium, zinc, iodine, and omega-3s, along with detoxification and gut-healing support.","Homeopathy, acupressure, and aromatherapy follow the same approaches used for autism and for anxiety (see the Psychiatry chapter)."],"conditions":["PANDAS (pediatric autoimmune neuropsychiatric disorders associated with streptococcus)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_colic","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Colic — the Holistic Rx","body":["Colic responds to probiotics, plus fennel tea (1/4 teaspoon three times daily) or chamomile tea (1/4 cup three times daily). Because food sensitivities transmitted through breast milk can drive colic, gas, and reflux, a breastfeeding mother can follow an elimination approach similar to the Gut Rx, removing the most-eaten problematic food first and watching the baby's symptoms.","Acupressure: colic points LI2, B47, K3, HP3, LV3, applied for ten to fifteen minutes in a small circular motion with light pressure held for ten to thirty seconds at a time.","Aromatherapy: lavender, roman chamomile, fennel, marjoram, bergamot, and ylang-ylang oils, well diluted in a carrier oil, since a baby's skin is thinner than an adult's."],"conditions":["Colic"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_failure_to_thrive","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Failure to thrive — the Holistic Rx","body":["Failure to thrive responds to glutamine (250 mg/kg) and probiotics to support gut healing and nutrient absorption. Address underlying food sensitivities and build the diet around the foundational, nutrient-dense pediatric approach, since inadequate absorption or ongoing gut inflammation is often the real driver of poor growth."],"conditions":["Failure to thrive"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_picky_eating","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Picky eating — the Holistic Rx","body":["Picky eating often reflects incomplete nutrition rather than pure preference. If dairy is eliminated, replace the lost calcium with 900–1,000 mg daily, split into two doses, and introduce one new food every two weeks. Add zinc (15 mg daily) once the child is older than three, along with a multivitamin, vitamin C (100 mg), vitamin D (800–1,000 IU per day), vitamin A (2,500 IU or less), selenium, and chromium.","Tips for picky eaters: stock the home only with appropriate, healthy foods, so that's what's available; set a good example, since children imitate their guardians; make family mealtime the standard, with everyone eating the same meal; involve children in picking out vegetables, fruit, protein, and fat sources at the store and in food preparation; make it fun by cutting vegetables into shapes and arranging them in rainbow colors; and stand your ground and stay consistent — don't give in to unhealthy substitutes. One good bite of food is better than no good bite, but no bite of food is better than a bite of poison; children may occasionally need to go to bed hungry, but they won't let themselves starve.","Educate children about the relationship between food and the body: explain what happens when they eat good food versus bad food, and about the good bugs/\"pets\" living in their belly that need to be fed well so the bad guys can go away and the good guys can win. Swap for healthy: exchange favorite foods for healthier versions (such as almond-flour brownies) as a treat after they finish their vegetables, protein, and fat."],"conditions":["Picky eating"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_peds_dyspraxia","chapter":"pediatrics","title":"Dyspraxia — the Holistic Rx","body":["Dyspraxia responds to fish oil, a vitamin D complex, and phosphatidylcholine to support neurological development and motor coordination."],"conditions":["Dyspraxia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_adhd","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Attention deficit disorder — the Holistic Rx","body":["Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is characterized by difficulty concentrating and being easily distracted, irritable, disorganized, unable to sit still, and impulsivity. According to Dr. Daniel Amen there are seven types: Type 1, classic ADD/ADHD, with inattentiveness, hyperactivity, impulsivity, restlessness, distractibility, and disorganization. Type 2, inattentive ADD, not hyperactive but inattentive, disorganized, and easily distracted. Type 3, overfocused ADD, with loops of negative behavior, oppositional and argumentative behavior, excessive worrying, inattentiveness, obsessiveness, and inflexibility. Type 4, temporal lobe ADD, with a short fuse, dark thoughts, irritability, distractibility, disorganization, mood instability, and possible learning disabilities. Type 5, limbic ADD, with inattentiveness, distractibility, disorganization, and pessimistic/depressive tendencies. Type 6, ring-of-fire ADD, with cyclic moodiness that may be oppositional and overly sensitive/irritable. Type 7, anxious ADD, with the classic symptoms plus nervousness, predicting the worst, and anxiety that may present with physical symptoms. Types 3 through 7 may or may not present with hypersensitivity. Testing can be performed at the child's school or by a licensed mental health professional.","Diet and the four big S's: heal the gut, balance insulin, and avoid toxins — critical for neurotransmitter function and overall mental health. Follow a gut-healing protocol like GAPS and avoid food sensitivities; start the day with a high-protein breakfast to optimize brain function. Eat foods high in tryptophan (turkey, chicken, tuna), complex carbs (butternut squash, sweet potatoes), raw milk if tolerated (goat or sheep), magnesium (green leafy vegetables, sesame and sunflower seeds), and B vitamins (organic meat, brewer's yeast, liver, green leafy vegetables). Managing stress and sleep lowers inflammation and optimizes mental health; combine aerobic conditioning, weight training, burst training, and stretching (exercising before work aids concentration); connect with nature, play daily, get optimal sleep, and get thirty to sixty minutes of sunlight or 10,000-lux light therapy in the morning.","Supplements: fish oil (1,000 mg per day of EPA/DHA), B complex (50 mg daily), a multimineral supplement (500 mg calcium, 100–300 mg magnesium — especially with tics, over-focus, or sleep/anxiety issues) and 5–20 mg zinc twice daily (especially with learning or memory issues), and a probiotic.","Specifics: GABA (250 mg twice daily); rhodiola rosea to help with focus; inositol powder (2–6 g twice daily) if anxious, sleep-troubled, or overfocused; pycnogenol (50–200 mg) for hyperactivity or impulsivity; phosphatidylserine (200 mg) for learning or memory problems; bacopa, gotu kola, and American ginseng for hyperactive/underfocused kids; SAM-e for depression and fatigue; L-theanine (200–400 mg, or 100–300 mg twice daily for kids) if anxious; ginkgo biloba (60–120 mg daily); DMAE (100–400 mg daily) for learning and attention; chromium (400–800 mcg per day) for blood sugar issues; and L-carnitine (1–2 g daily).","Dr. Amen's type-specific recommendations: Type 1 — rhodiola, green tea, ginseng, L-tyrosine, zinc, grapeseed, or pine bark (stimulating supplements). Type 2 — stimulating supplements. Type 3 — 5-HTP plus stimulating supplements with a lower-protein, higher smart-carb diet. Type 4 — GABA, ginkgo, vinpocetine, huperzine A, ALC, PS, NAC, and ALA plus stimulating supplements with a higher-protein, lower-carb ketogenic diet. Type 5 — SAM-e plus stimulating supplements. Type 6 — GABA and 5-HTP plus stimulating supplements. Type 7 — L-theanine, Relora, magnesium, and holy basil plus stimulating supplements.","Homeopathy: Hyoscyamus niger for children who are impulsive and violent, sexually precocious, and talkative. Stramonium for multiple fears, anger, rage, a tendency to destroy things, and night terrors. Sulphur for those who are stubborn, curious, always thirsty for cold drinks, craving spicy foods, sweating easily, and hyperactive. Tarentula hispanica for impulsive, mischievous, restless, hurried children who love dance and music. Coffea tosta for extreme moodiness with a constant flow of ideas and anxiety with insomnia. Kali bromatum for inability to sit still, constantly moving hands, and frequent night terrors. Luesinum when nervousness prevents concentration; appears wrinkled, nervous, and unstable, improving in the mountains. Hepar sulphuris for excitable, irritable children who are hypersensitive to pain with recurring suppurations. China for irritable, touchy individuals with frequent insomnia who may have worms. Staphysagria for irritable, touchy individuals who rarely explode with anger and often masturbate. Moschus for agitation and anxiety with sexual problems and paradoxical symptoms. Medorrhinum for poor memory, irritability, major temper tantrums, violence toward other kids, inattentiveness, hands and feet always moving, never finishing what they start, symptoms improving at the seaside, and cravings for oranges and ice. Chamomilla for the irritable, anxious, agitated child who dislikes being talked to and is hypersensitive to pain. Lachesis mutus for those who are very talkative, jealous, oversensitive, and occasionally depressed. Nitricum acidum for fatigue with indifference and occasional depression.","Homeopathy for attention and memory issues: Baryta carbonica for the timid, mentally fatigued, backward slow learner. Calcarea phosphorica for anxiety with mental and physical fatigue. Natrum muriaticum for the quickly discouraged with frequent headaches, salt cravings, intense thirst, thinness with a good appetite, and indifference. Zincum metallicum for fatigue, excessive worry, restless feet, and poor memory. Silicea for anxious, fatigued, easily discouraged, nervous, irritable children prone to respiratory infections, sensitive to cold, with sweaty feet and difficulty concentrating. Agaricus muscarius for slow children with tics. Anacardium orientale for fatigue after the slightest effort, sensitivity, poor memory, indifference, symptoms improving with eating, cruelty to animals and people, low self-esteem, and antisocial behavior. For inattention with fatigue: tuberculinum (stubborn, demanding, impatient, recurring viral infections like rhinopharyngitis or bronchitis, boredom, violent tempers, cravings for milk and smoked meats), psorinum (fatigue with anxiety, fear of the future, inferiority complex), sepia (mental fatigue with irritability and poor memory), aurum muriaticum (lack of motivation, extreme irritability, anxiety, and depression), phosphoricum acidum (indifference, frequent headaches, poor memory), and kali phosphoricum (frequent headaches, sleep problems, hypersensitivity, irritability, nervous exhaustion).","Acupressure: CV12, LI11, P6, H7, LU1, and SP6.","Aromatherapy: vetiver, cedarwood, cardamom, peppermint, basil, and thyme can improve focus and alertness. Calming oils include ylang-ylang, neroli, orange, lavender, rose, sandalwood, chamomile, and vetiver."],"conditions":["Attention deficit disorder"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_anxiety","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Anxiety — the Holistic Rx","body":["Anxiety is a normal feeling of nervousness, but constant anxiety, regardless of the circumstance, can lead to other chronic ailments. Types include panic disorder, social anxiety disorder, generalized anxiety disorder, and specific phobias. Symptoms include muscle tension, restlessness, a fast heartbeat, stomach upset, fatigue, irritability, and/or trouble falling asleep. Diagnosis is made through a detailed history, physical exam, and basic labs.","Supplements: vitamin C (1,000 mg in the morning); magnesium and calcium (500 mg calcium and 250 mg magnesium twice daily); B-complex (50 mg with 1 mg folate); 5-HTP (50–200 mg twice daily); kava (200–250 mg three times daily); GABA (500 mg three times daily); adaptogenic herbs like ashwagandha and tulsi; inositol (3–5 g two to three times daily); EPA (1–2 g); and St. John's wort (900 mg per day of a quality product, divided twice daily).","For trauma-related anxiety and PTSD support: inositol (3–6 g three times daily), magnesium glycinate (125–250 mg twice daily, morning and bedtime), and melatonin (0.5–1 mg) for sleep issues. For children — anxiety: inositol, L-theanine, kava (if comfortable with the risk), and herbals like California poppy, lemon balm, and valerian.","Homeopathy for panic and generalized anxiety: Aconitum napellus for acute panic attacks with a sense of doom, worse around midnight. Arsenicum album for anxiety, agitation, and insecurity with a fast heart rate and disturbed appetite, obsessed about cleaning and organization. Calcarea carbonica for anxiety with feelings of being overwhelmed, coldness, irritability, sadness, and fatigue. Gelsemium for acute anxiety from crowds, also with diarrhea and trembling. Kali phosphoricum for poor memory, general anxiety, and fatigue. Pulsatilla for anxiety about being alone. Chamomilla for anxiety with anger, oversensitivity to pain, and irritability. Ignatia for those very sensitive to contradiction, moody, with a tendency to spasm (a ball-in-throat sensation and abdominal pain), improved by entertainment; also for anxiety following an emotional trauma like a death or breakup. Magnesia carbonica for nervousness and spasmodic pain, prone to diarrhea with sour-smelling sweat. Cyclamen for exhausting perfectionism and feelings of abandonment, worse during menses, with migraines and vertigo. Magnesia muriatica for anxiety with cramp-like pain, constipation, nervousness, and agitation with paresthesia. Nux vomica for irritability, anger, intolerance to contradiction, aggressiveness, and digestive symptoms, with insomnia around 3:00–4:00 a.m. Nux moschata for anxious, emotionally sensitive individuals with abdominal distention and dry mucous membranes. Stramonium for anxiety with tics, agitation, and laryngeal or esophageal spasms. Lycopodium for anxiety about a new situation or stage anxiety, with sweet cravings, worse in stuffy rooms. Arsenicum for restlessness, excessive tidiness, coldness, and fatigue. Phosphorus for nervousness with extreme sensitivity to others, a love of reassurance, and symptoms worse during thunderstorms. Calcarea for a person who is easily forgetful, feels cold, and becomes overweight easily. Natrum muriaticum for those who hate sympathy and dwell on morbid topics. Tarentula for anxiety from a tendency to overwork, with difficulty relaxing night and day.","Homeopathy for hysteria (converting mental stress into physical symptoms — overdramatic or attention-seeking behavior): Ignatia for a lump-in-throat sensation and other symptoms from emotional upset, weepiness, and moodiness. Valeriana for sensations of floating, losing identity, and sleepiness. Platinum for numbness, coldness, haughtiness, and contemptuousness. Moschus for palpitations, difficulty breathing, overdramatic and manipulative behavior, hysterical emotional behavior, changes in sexual excitement, and hyperesthesia. Asafoetida for asthma, hysterical cough, and nausea/vomiting with retching, as if the body is about to explode.","Homeopathy for specific phobias — fear of heights: Argenticum nitricum for fear with an impulse to jump; borax for fear with a sensation of falling; sulphur for fear of heights with extreme giddiness. Fear of the dark: stramonium for a person who fears the dark, talking and praying continuously; phosphorus for serious anxiety, fear of the future and the dark, hypersensitivity, fatigue, and agitation, improved with reassurance. Fear of performing in public (social anxiety): lycopodium for anxiety about performing, low confidence about eating in public, grouchiness on waking, feeling full after a small amount of food, social inadequacy, sweet cravings, and digestive upset; gelsemium for feeling weak at the knees with test anxiety and trembling; anacardium for anxiety leading to stomach upsets, specifically for performers; argenticum nitricum for those in a rush with time-organizing issues and fear that can lead to diarrhea and gas, or for loss of emotional control.","Other Recommendations: cranial electrotherapy stimulation.","Acupressure: HP7, HP6, TW5, H7, H5, LU1, TW15, B10, P3, P6 (for nervous tension), GV24.5, CV17, LV3, GB13, K27, SP6, B23. TFT (thought field therapy) uses acupressure points to help break up energy patterns that can lead to symptoms.","Aromatherapy: essential oils that provide a relaxing effect include lavender, rose, vetiver, ylang-ylang, bergamot, chamomile, frankincense, neroli, sandalwood, and orange. Other calming and soothing oils are cypress, palmarosa, helichrysum, patchouli, clary sage, jasmine, lemon balm, and lime. During a panic attack, frankincense can help improve deep breathing. Bergamot helps restore appetite, while peppermint, melissa, lavender, and chamomile can help improve digestive complaints like diarrhea or cramps."],"conditions":["Anxiety"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_ocd","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) — the Holistic Rx","body":["OCD is a mental health disorder characterized by a pattern of unreasonable fears (obsessions) leading to repetitive behaviors (compulsions) that interfere with daily routine. Diagnosis is made through detailed history and physical exam.","Supplements: inositol (18 g/day, improvements may take up to twelve weeks), NAC (600–1,200 mg twice daily), glycine, and milk thistle (600 mg/day). For children — OCD: inositol, L-theanine, and/or herbals to reduce ambient anxiety, with NAC also considered.","Homeopathy for obsessions and compulsions: Aurum for thoughts of dying and feelings of worthlessness. Anacardium for feeling as if the body and mind are separate, or as if being controlled by someone else. Silicea for unshakable feelings of not being good enough, with a need to sit on the floor and count small objects. Thuja for a conviction that live animals are wriggling in the stomach, brittle limbs that may break, and warts.","Other Recommendations: the four-step behavioral technique — Relabel (call the obsessive thought or urge what it really is), Reattribute (answer the question of \"why\"; attribute these feelings not to yourself but to your OCD), Refocus (learn to work around your thoughts as they occur), and Revalue (give less value to your anxiety and more value to your life).","Acupressure: see the Anxiety protocol for relevant points depending on predominant symptom.","Aromatherapy: see the Anxiety protocol for relevant oils depending on predominant symptom."],"conditions":["Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_ptsd","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Post-traumatic stress disorder — the Holistic Rx","body":["Trauma is an emotional reaction to an emotionally painful and traumatizing event that can leave someone feeling powerless and overwhelmed. When someone fails to recover from that event, it is called post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD).","Supplements: inositol (3–6 g three times daily), magnesium glycinate (125–250 mg twice daily, in the morning and at bedtime), and melatonin (0.5–1 mg) if sleep issues are present. For children — PTSD: inositol and L-theanine, and/or calming herbals."],"conditions":["Post-traumatic stress disorder"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_bipolar","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Bipolar disorder — the Holistic Rx","body":["Bipolar disorder (manic depression) is a mood disorder defined by depressive lows and manic high-energy states. Diagnosis is made via history and physical exam.","Supplements: fish oil; adaptogenic herbs like ginseng, holy basil, ashwagandha, and rhodiola; EFAs/EPA (0.5 g for ages four to eight, 1 g for ages nine to eleven, and 2 g for ages twelve and above, per day); magnesium citrate or aspartate (150 mg up to age six, 300 mg for ages seven to eleven, 40–500 mg for ages twelve and above); vitamin C (250 mg/day for ages three to five, 500 mg/day for ages six to eleven, 1,000 mg/day for ages twelve and above); EMPowerplus (five capsules three times daily as a loading dose, then four to five capsules or powder twice daily as maintenance, before beginning medications); and inositol (3–6 g three times daily). Begin diet and supplements first, allowing a two-month trial before moving on to medications. Never combine SAM-e, St. John's wort, 5-HTP, ginseng, or ginkgo with bipolar depression, even with a family history of bipolar disorder.","Children with bipolar disorder: fish oil (500–1,000 mg EPA), inositol (3–6 g two to three times daily); if emotionally dysregulated, consider DBT; avoid St. John's wort, SAM-e, ginkgo, ginseng, stimulants, and antidepressants; consider EMPowerplus as a central biochemical intervention. If there are no current psychiatric or contraindicated medications, the dose can be increased to five capsules three times daily after food over a few days; if already on medication, work with a practitioner to cross-taper. If mania persists, consider choline bitartrate (300–500 mg two to three times daily) and NAC.","Homeopathy: Apis for those who feel as if they are going to die, who are very jealous, and not thirsty. Hyoscyamus for hallucinations, obscene remarks, and fear of being poisoned. Belladonna for high fevers, a red face, and wild staring eyes. Cannabis indica for exalted ideas, a feeling that time is slow, and uncontrollable laughter. Phosphorus for rapid oscillating moods and a temper, with a calming effect.","Acupressure and Aromatherapy: see the relevant section (Anxiety, Depression, etc.) depending on the predominant symptom at the time."],"conditions":["Bipolar disorder"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_depression","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Depression — the Holistic Rx","body":["Depression involves feelings of sadness, loss, frustration, low sex drive, fatigue, and helplessness, among others, that can cause problems in day-to-day living. There are multiple types of depression, like major depression, dysthymia (mild depression that is long-lasting), atypical depression, adjustment disorders, postpartum depression, premenstrual dysphoric disorder (the week before a period), seasonal affective disorder, and the depressive phase of bipolar disorder. Diagnosis can be made with a detailed history, physical exam, and basic labs.","Supplements: adaptogen herbs rhodiola and ashwagandha (1,000 mg twice daily). Vitamin B complex (50 mg daily); make sure it contains folate as 5-methyltetrahydrofolate or folinic acid and B12 as methylcobalamin, hydroxocobalamin, or adenosylcobalamin. A multivitamin with magnesium (400 mg per day), zinc (15–30 mg daily) with copper (1–3 mg daily), and iodine (200 mcg to 3 mg) — Atlantic kelp and selenium (100–200 mcg per day) are ideal for anxiousness and depression and for those with low thyroid function or low T3 levels. St. John's wort (300–500 mg daily). Evening primrose (start with 500 mg twice daily). Cod liver oil from Atlantic cod for at least 2,500 IU of vitamin A and 250 IU of vitamin D per teaspoon. Adrenal and hypothalamus glandular. Digestive enzymes with protease, lipase, amylase, and betaine HCl. SAMe (400–1,600 mg per day); don't take if on an antidepressant medication or levodopa, as it may lead to serotonin syndrome (also rare with meperidine, pentazocine, and dextromethorphan); not studied in children younger than twelve but can be well tolerated in those older than fourteen; can be taken twenty to thirty minutes before a meal. L-theanine (200 mg per day) can help reduce anxiety and support a relaxed but focused mind-set (meditation in a capsule). N-acetyl cysteine (600–1,800 mg per day). Rhodiola (100 mg working up to 400 mg per day; look for 2–3% rosavin and 0.8–1% salidroside). Curcumin (500–1,000 mg twice daily). Others: chromium picolinate (400 mg twice daily; most helpful in atypical depression with excessive sleep and appetite); ginkgo biloba (60–120 mg twice daily of a standardized product with 24% flavone glycosides and 6% terpene lactones) improves blood flow to the brain and enhances neurotransmitter activity, useful for slow, fatigue-ridden depression.","Depression with agitation or anxiety: inositol (3–6 g two or three times daily). L-theanine (100–200 mg twice daily). Kava — monitor liver function tests, and use only a high-quality product. 5-HTP (50–150 mg twice daily). Passionflower (250 mg or 0.5 ml two to three times daily) relaxes the nerves and is gentle enough to use during the day. Relora (250 mg two to three times per day with food).","If period-related: start with maca and progress to chaste tree. Maca (per label), chaste tree (150–250 mg containing 30–40 mg of dried fruit extract). Inositol is best when blood sugar imbalances are at the root of the menstrual irregularity — it improves insulin sensitivity, reduces male hormones (at doses of 2–4 g daily), and has been studied for anxiety and OCD (at doses of 12–18 g daily). Myo-inositol and D-chiro-inositol can be combined in a 40:1 ratio for the most effective hormone balancing.","Children: B-complex (50 mg with 1 mg of folate), fish oil (500–1,000 mg of EPA), and vitamin C (500–1,000 mg daily); for chronic depression or fatigue, explore adrenal health and consider adaptogens like rhodiola or ashwagandha. If emotional regulation is an issue, select St. John's wort and SAMe as appropriate. If tapering off an SSRI: a broad-spectrum amino acid formula along with a high-quality tryptophan or 5-HTP supplement — tryptophan starting at 500 mg a day taken with a simple carbohydrate on an empty stomach, working up to 3 g per day, or 5-HTP starting at 50 mg three times a day and working up to 200 mg three times per day, taken on an empty stomach, along with the complementary amino acid tyrosine (1,500 mg two to four times daily before meals). Inositol, pharmaGABA, or phenibut can be a helpful calming agent during this time. Amino acids are only needed during the medication taper.","Homeopathy: Arsenicum album for intense fatigue, restlessness, worrying, seeing no hope for a cure, pessimism, exhaustion, always feeling cold, and obsessing about cleanliness; depression with weight loss, anxiety, and insomnia between 1:00 and 3:00 a.m. China for racing thoughts, vivid dreams, oversensitivity to stimuli like noise and light, and feeling cold. Aurum metallicum for deep depression, self-disgust, and even suicidal tendency, improved by being in the sun (suicidal tendencies should always be discussed with a doctor or a suicide hotline). Ignatia amara for depression following emotional trauma, with constant sighing and a lump-in-the-throat sensation. Natrum muriaticum for those who don't show their emotions, are emotionally reserved and withdrawn, and reject sympathy out of embarrassment; they crave salt and are averse to sunlight. Pulsatilla for those who burst into tears at the slightest provocation and want reassurance and attention, with depression worsening around a menstrual cycle or menopause; worse in a warm environment, improved by crying, sweets, open air, and attention. Nux vomica for those who find fault with everyone and are extremely irritable. Sepia for irritability, low libido, tearfulness, and always feeling cold; also for easy crying, memory problems, wanting to be alone, and irritation with consolation, worse with menses, with cravings for sweets and salty/sour foods. Kali phosphoricum for depression from overwork, with mental fatigue a common symptom, and for fatigue, irritability, listless depression, and fear of crowds following overwork or grief. Graphites for anguish, apprehensiveness, and hesitation. Anacardium orientale for poor memory, mental cloudiness, indifference, slowness to understand, and depression, sometimes with a feeling of split personality; improved by eating. Kali bromatum for an agitated, distracted patient who moves constantly and has blunted sensitivity to pain. Lachesis mutus for depression alternating with excitement, characterized by mutism, worry, sensitivity, fear of dying, and jealousy without reason; insomnia usually before midnight. Kali carbonicum for those who are fatigued, hypersensitive, and worried about their health. Lycopodium for exhaustion, depression, low libido during depressive phases, and poor sleep. Natrum carbonicum for depression and poor memory with sensory hypersensitivity, especially to noise, intolerant of sunshine or heat, leading to headaches. Natrum sulphuricum for depression with reticence, worry, and morning irritability; improved by eating, worse in the evening and wet weather. Nitricum acidum for depression with worry about health, annoyance, anger, easy crying, and rejecting comfort; insomnia with unpleasant dreams and vague pain. Phosphorus for irritability, fatigue, and agitation countered by indifference. Psorinum for depression with little reaction, worry about the future, feelings of inferiority, fear of being incurable, and emaciation. Selenium for the need to stay in bed and sleep often, with fatigue accompanied by skin disorders. Phosphoricum acidum for nervous exhaustion with difficulty concentrating and headaches, depression usually after mental shock or worries. Silicea for lack of energy, sensitivity to cold, a tendency to suppuration, and irritability.","Acupressure: GB20, GB21, LV3, LV5, LV14, K27, B38, B23 (with fear or fatigue); B47, GV24.5, K27, CV17 (relieves depression made worse by grief or anxiety); ST36 (depression with digestive symptoms and fatigue); GV19, Tai yang, P6 (eases anxiety and depression).","Aromatherapy: essential oils shown to elevate mood include bergamot, lavender, Roman chamomile, and ylang-ylang. Others that can help are frankincense, cedarwood, cinnamon, neroli, mandarin, orange, lemongrass, helichrysum, jasmine, litsea, basil, geranium, tuberose, ravintsara, clary sage, benzoin, sandalwood, and marjoram.","Other Recommendations: the Fisher Wallace cranial electrical stimulator (CES) generates a low-intensity alternating current transmitted across the skull for twenty minutes twice daily to promote alpha wave activity and modulate neurotransmitters, endorphins, and cortisol. A light box can help with sleep."],"conditions":["Depression"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_eating_disorders","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Eating disorders — the Holistic Rx","body":["Eating disorders — like anorexia, bulimia, and binge eating disorder — can be caused by an obsession with food, body weight, and shape, and can lead to serious and fatal consequences. Symptoms may include extremely restricted eating or overeating followed by attempts to get rid of the food, distorted body image, fatigue, dehydration, and other symptoms that can develop over time and can be fatal. Diagnosis can be made through a detailed history and physical exam with basic labs that can determine specific deficiencies and root cause (per Dr. Greenblatt, MD, in the book Answers to Anorexia). Labs to consider: zinc taste test, amino acids, CBC with differential, celiac screening, cholesterol panel, CMP, copper, essential fatty acids, folate and vitamin B12, homocysteine, iron and ferritin, magnesium, methylmalonic acid, red blood cell trace minerals, thyroid panel, urinary organic acids, urinary peptides, vitamin D 25-OH, zinc, and referenced-EEG.","Supplements: calcium citrate with vitamin D (250–500 mg twice daily). Probiotic (10–20 billion CFU per capsule). Plant-based digestive enzymes with DPP-IV and HCl with meals. Vitamin C (1,000–2,000 mg per day). Essential fatty acids. High-potency multivitamin. Zinc chelate (30–90 mg daily) along with 3 mg of copper — zinc deficiency is common in anorexia and bulimia, and zinc is required for the senses of taste and smell and for appetite. Magnesium glycinate. B-complex (50–75 mg twice daily). 5-HTP (100 mg two or three times daily) supports serotonin levels; do not combine with a pharmaceutical antidepressant or antianxiety medication. Gentian root (ten drops in water, or 300 mg fifteen minutes before each meal) improves digestion and appetite. St. John's wort (300 mg of a 0.3% hypericin extract two or three times daily); do not combine with a pharmaceutical antidepressant or antianxiety medication.","Homeopathy: Natrum muriaticum for one who dislikes sympathy, puts on weight at the bottom and thighs, with a thin neck; also for longstanding grief and depression, craving salty foods, great thirst, and aversion to sun. Calcarea for one who is overweight, always cold, timid, easily forgetful, craves eggs, and is apprehensive. Ferrum for anemic pallor with easy flushing, weakness, and oversensitivity. Pulsatilla for those who weep easily and are timid, uncomfortable in stuffy rooms, and feel better with company and cool air; worse in hot, stuffy rooms. Argentum nitricum for claustrophobia and fear of tall buildings, severe enough to cause diarrhea. Sulphur when no other remedy fits. Ignatia amara for eating disorders with emotional swings and easy crying; the person does not want consolation. Lycopodium for digestive issues like bloating, low blood sugar, irritability, and low self-esteem.","Acupressure: CV12, ST36, SP6, LI4, LV3, LI11, P6, ear shenmen, SP16 — can help bring appetite back into balance.","Aromatherapy: oils used for appetite loss include bergamot, buchu, galangal, dill, tarragon, cinnamon, lemon, lemongrass, fennel, wintergreen, litsea, peppermint, nutmeg, and ginger. Others helpful for anorexia are rose, clary sage, lavender, and ylang-ylang.","Other Recommendations: get professional help; stop the dieting mentality. Focus on a happy and healthy weight rather than a number. Support those who are struggling — withhold judgment, be supportive, avoid commenting on their weight, be present without trying to fix the problem, and set a good example by handling stress without turning to food."],"conditions":["Eating disorders"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_insomnia","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Insomnia — the Holistic Rx","body":["Insomnia is a common sleep disorder that can make it hard to fall asleep or stay asleep — waking up too early, waking frequently through the night, and not being able to get back to sleep — all of which can lead to daytime impairment. Diagnosis can be made with a comprehensive sleep history, a patient diary of nighttime sleep and daytime wakefulness, a full history including psychiatric and substance-abuse history, and by ruling out adverse effects of medication.","Supplements: melatonin (1–3 mg half an hour before bed, for a short time); discuss with your primary care provider before use in pregnancy, breastfeeding, cancer, or in children under six, and use caution combining with other sleep medications as it may intensify their effect. Passionflower (500 mg before bed, or 1–2 ml a half hour before bedtime). Valerian (600 mg before bed, or 2 ml a half hour before bedtime). Calcium and magnesium (500 mg calcium / 250 mg magnesium). Combination formula: valerian (30%), linden (20%), kava (20%), chamomile (20%), and catnip (10%) — promotes GABA release — taken as a tea, or a blend of valerian root, wild lettuce, Jamaican dogwood, passionflower, hops, and L-theanine, one to four capsules at bedtime. Others: 5-HTP (100–400 mg at night); hops (Humulus lupulus; 500 mg or 1–2 ml a half hour before bedtime — a nervine that relaxes the nervous system); vitamin B12 (1,500 mcg in capsule form or 400 mcg sublingual daily); passionflower; reishi; St. John's wort; chamomile; catnip; skullcap; lavender; lemon balm; GABA (500–1,000 mg); magnolia (365 mg); combinations of L-tryptophan and niacinamide (500–1,000 mg at bedtime); theanine (100 mg at night, plus 100 mg in the middle of the night if you wake and can't fall back asleep); lemon balm (80–160 mg; helps with both falling and staying asleep); D-ribose (5 g twice daily); calcium (600 mg); ashwagandha; and phosphatidylserine.","Children: 5-HTP (50–100 mg before bed), melatonin (0.3–3 mg before bed), taurine (1,000 mg before bed), GABA (750 mg before bed), magnesium (65–350 mg before bed), chamomile tea and valerian tea (one cup before bed). DMSA protocol, TMG (250–500 mg once or twice daily), magnesium (400–800 mg at bedtime), 5-HTP (50–100 mg at bedtime), naltrexone transdermal (1–4 mg at bedtime). California poppy tincture (two to four dropperfuls as needed), catnip (one cup two to three times a day), linden tea (one cup three times per day). The above herbs can also be added to bathwater (1 quart of tea to a bathtub of water).","Trouble falling asleep: herbs and stretching, a warm bath before bed, meditation and deep breathing, and massage or acupuncture. Trouble staying asleep associated with adrenal problems, glucose dysfunction, or anxiety: eleuthero, reishi mushroom, and rehmannia may be helpful.","Homeopathy: Coffea for a mind that can't shut off, wide awake at 3:00 a.m. Nux vomica for those tired from mental strain or after rich food or alcohol, who wake around 3:00–4:00 a.m. thinking about business, then fall asleep just before it's time to get up, remaining irritable with nightmares; sensitive to light, noise, or sound. Pulsatilla for insomnia worse after rich food — feeling too hot, throwing off the covers, then too cold, lying with arms above the head, not thirsty, restless in the first sleep. Aconite for insomnia with fear of dying, worse after panic or shock, with nightmares and restlessness. Chamomilla for feeling wide awake and irritable in the first part of the night; can be used for a child who wants to be carried. Lycopodium for waking at 4:00 a.m., excessive dreaming from fear and stress, talking and laughing in sleep, an overactive mind at bedtime, poor confidence, digestive problems, and sweet cravings. Cocculus for someone used to being up at night, too tired to sleep, giddy, weak, dizzy, with trouble thinking and irritability. Ignatia for excessive yawning without being able to fall asleep, insomnia from emotional upset with crying, mood swings, loss of appetite, sighing, and muscle twitches. Arnica for the overtired, fidgety person who dreams of being chased by animals and feels the bed is too hard. Opium for sensitivity to sound, a bed that feels too hot, extreme tiredness with inability to fall asleep, and once asleep, heavy snoring with difficulty rousing. Arsenicum for waking between midnight and 2:00 a.m., anxious, restless, apprehensive, worried with fears and insecurities. Rhus tox for irritability, inability to sleep, and discomfort. Aurum for depression with dreams about work problems, dying, or hunger. Arsenicum album for restlessness, panic about waking someone else, after a terrifying experience. Kali phosphoricum for insomnia from overwork or mental strain, with anxiety, depression, and sensitivity. Sulphur for insomnia from feeling hot or itchy at night, kicking off the covers or sticking the feet out. Zincum metallicum for restless arms or legs, usually from being overworked. Baryta carbonica for memory disorders. Gelsemium for emotional problems. Hepar sulphuris for emotional oversensitivity. Phosphorus for anxiety, easy tiredness, and hyperactivity. Lachesis mutans for mental excitement, nightmares, oversensitivity, and touchiness. Actaea racemosa for strong emotionality worsened by menstrual flow. Argentum nitricum for phobias, vertigo, and emotionality. Phosphoricum acidum for exhaustion from intellectual effort. Staphysagria for those obsessed with injustices, highly sensitive and easily indignant. Stramonium for night terrors needing a nightlight to fall or stay asleep. Hyoscyamus niger for mumbling, laughing, crying, or startling suddenly during sleep, with a tendency to tics. Kali bromatum for night terrors with constant moving of the hands and extremities, occasional sleepwalking, prone to worms. Cina for agitated sleep with teeth grinding or groaning, worse during phases of the moon.","Acupressure: B38, P6, H7, B10, GV16, GV20, GV24.5, CV17, K6, B62, SP6, H7. Disturbed sleep: B5, B20, P6. Difficulty falling asleep: B15, B23, K3. Indigestion: SP16. Pain relief: LI4.","Aromatherapy: essential oils that help you relax and fall asleep include yarrow, dill, neroli, petitgrain, bergamot, lemon, orange, star anise, bay laurel, lavender, Roman chamomile, nutmeg, myrtle, marjoram, allspice, tuberose, sandalwood, valerian, vetiver, ylang-ylang, jasmine, rose, angelica, cedarwood, cistus, clary sage, mandarin, melissa, and spikenard."],"conditions":["Insomnia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_schizophrenia","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Schizophrenia — the Holistic Rx","body":["Schizophrenia is a chronic mental health condition that affects how a person behaves, feels, and thinks; those with the condition interpret reality abnormally. Symptoms may include disordered thinking and speech, hallucinations, and delusions, and can be disabling, impairing daily function. Diagnosis is made by a trained professional with a detailed history, and testing may include imaging.","Supplements: niacin (3,000 mg per day). 5-HTP (300–600 mg). Omega-3 fatty acids. Serine (obtain an amino acid test before initiating). Magnesium.","Homeopathy: Lachesis for paranoia, a sensation of constriction in the throat, aversion to wearing clothes, worse in the morning and after sleep; also for depression. Belladonna for wild, staring eyes and a red face. Stramonium for a violent person who swears or prays and hears voices, very talkative. Hyoscyamus for inappropriate language and behavior with suspicion and paranoia. Tarantula for a person who is always busy, bustling, bothered by music, and irritable. Thuja for strange sensations with anxiety and depression. Anacardium for feeling as though one has two different selves trying to gain control.","Hallucinations are perceptions with the qualities of real perception but without an external stimulus. Absinthium for depression, disorientation, dizziness, hallucinations, memory loss, giddiness, and a tendency to fall backward. Agaricus for hallucinations that transform the visual image, with shaking and trembling. Belladonna for visual hallucinations like monsters with hideous mouths and red, hot, staring eyes, with a feeling of being hot. Cannabis for a sense that seconds feel like hours, with uncontrollable laughter. Hyoscyamus for marked paranoia, feeling persecuted or controlled by external forces, muttering with obscene talk and behavior, greatly suspicious. Opium for a tired person who lapses into heavy sleep. Stramonium for someone who sees ghosts, hears voices, and says prayers, very talkative.","Paranoia involves thoughts of conspiracy or threat with severe anxiety or fearful feelings. Lachesis for mild symptoms. Hyoscyamus for symptoms of an acute, severe nature.","Acupressure: PC7, GV20, ST40.","Aromatherapy: essential oils are calming and, depending on the symptoms, can help bring relief."],"conditions":["Schizophrenia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_psych_substance_abuse","chapter":"psychiatry","title":"Substance abuse and addiction — the Holistic Rx","body":["Substance abuse is the overuse of drugs, alcohol, or tobacco, which may even begin in childhood. Causes may include a chaotic home environment, lack of nurturing and parental attachment, poor social coping skills, inability to handle stress, poor school performance, or association with the wrong peer group. Symptoms vary widely, from inability to keep up with daily activities, forgetfulness, lying and other oppositional-defiant traits, frequent hangovers, pressuring others to drink or use drugs, trouble with the law, irritability, and/or disappearing money or valuables. Abuse may lead to hallucinations, behavioral changes, dilated pupils, involuntary shaking, muscle cramping, and teeth clenching.","Supplements: free-form amino acids (three to six capsules three times per day on an empty stomach, higher end if in acute withdrawal). Glutamine (1,000 mg three times per day). Pancreatic enzyme three times per day with meals. Ester-C (1,000 mg three times per day). B-complex (50 mg twice daily). Melatonin (1–3 mg at night). Calcium citrate (500 mg three times per day). Magnesium glycinate (300 mg three times per day). EFA omega-3 (EPA 400 mg three times per day). Inositol powder (4–6 g three times per day). Others: a multivitamin (as directed, to assist detoxification and improve mood); milk thistle (Silybum marianum, 250 mg three times daily of a product standardized to 80–85% silymarin); chromium (200 mcg two or three times daily, to improve mood and energy); 5-HTP (100 mg three times daily on an empty stomach, taken before bedtime, to help with sleep — do not take if on a pharmaceutical antidepressant or antianxiety medication). Adolescents: inositol (3–6 g two or three times per day), B-complex (50 mg with 1 mg of folate), fish oil (500–1,000 mg of EPA), vitamin C (500–1,000 mg). For true dependence: glutamine (1,000 mg or more three times daily) and calcium/magnesium in a 1:1 ratio (250–500 mg twice daily), plus free-form amino acids including L-tryptophan (three to six capsules on an empty stomach three times daily) for the first few months to help with withdrawal. If highly motivated or chronic, consider neurofeedback training; if severe dependence with impaired nutrition, consider IV nutritional support over the first two months of recovery.","Homeopathy: Arsenicum for fear of being alone, anxiety, fatigue, and restlessness, worse at 2:00 a.m., improved with warmth. Lachesis for feeling persecuted, jealous, paranoid, suspicious, depressed, and talkative, for addictions that turn violent; worse with heat, unable to tolerate anything touching the throat. Aconite for sudden panic, feeling cold, and a sense of dying. Absinthium for depression, disorientation, dizziness, and hallucinations. Hyoscyamus for marked paranoia, feeling persecuted or controlled by external forces, muttering with obscene talk and behavior. Ignatia amara for wanting to be left alone, hysteria, emotional breakdowns, a lump-in-the-throat sensation, anxiety, and twitches or spasms, with constantly changing moods and symptoms.","For alcoholism specifically: Nux vomica for morning hangover, emotional withdrawal with digestive symptoms, irritability, anger, and sensitivity to touch, odors, sound, and light. Capsicum for stomach pain after heavy drinking. Lachesis for social binge drinking, hatred of tight clothing, and excessive talking. Avena for depression and irritability greater than usual. Zinc for sensitivity to noise, fatigue, apprehension, and trembling. Sulphur for craving alcohol and going on binges, with abnormal flatulence, nervous exhaustion, and solitary drinking, feeling warm and better from cool air and drinks, craving spicy food. Kali bichromicum for nausea and vomiting associated with alcohol, with stringy catarrh. Lycopodium for low self-esteem, irritability, chilliness, digestive problems, and sweet cravings; can be used in alcoholism or drug addiction.","Acupressure: ST36, SP10 help in the detoxification process. LU1 helps with depression or anxiety. GB20 helps with fatigue. Addiction to nicotine: LI4, LI20, ST36, LV3, GV20.","Aromatherapy: essential oils like peppermint, clove, cinnamon, cilantro, and grapefruit can help curb cravings, while lavender, sandalwood, orange, marjoram, and grapefruit can help improve withdrawal symptoms. Juniper helps break up toxins and speed their exit from the body. Calming and mood-lifting oils can all help improve symptoms, including rose oil (lifts depression), bergamot (relieves anxiety, depression, and compulsive behavior), and clary sage.","Other Recommendations: hypnotherapy is a therapeutic technique that uses hypnosis to treat the condition."],"conditions":["Substance abuse and addiction"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_pulm_general_dd","chapter":"pulmonology","title":"Pulmonology — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["By controlling our breath, we can control our universe from the inside out — the goal is to lower inflammation so we can maximize the moments that take our breath away. Healing the gut and avoiding food sensitivities like dairy helps reduce excess mucus production. Detoxifying your environment regularly is key, and maintaining the health of the mucous membranes is important: eat foods rich in carotenoids and vitamin A, like sweet potatoes, carrots, berries, and leafy greens. Magnesium can improve muscle spasms and promote muscle relaxation. Quercetin, found in garlic, mustard seeds, and onions, helps fight bacterial infections and improves overall immune health. Folate protects the lungs and can be found in green leafy vegetables, nuts, and lentils. Omega-3 fatty acid foods, like fish, nuts, and flaxseeds, reduce airway inflammation. Foods with vitamins B5 and B6 help support stress and adrenal function. Staying hydrated is important for airways to function appropriately.","The Four Big S's — special consideration: stress can worsen breathing issues and airway inflammation, so incorporating relaxation skills and targeting specific emotional imbalances are vital steps for optimal health and can improve airway function. Exercising in cold air can exacerbate attacks, so cover the nose and mouth as needed."],"conditions":["Asthma","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Bronchitis","Pneumonia","Cough"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_pulm_asthma","chapter":"pulmonology","title":"Asthma — the Holistic Rx","body":["Asthma is a chronic inflammatory condition of the lungs that leads to narrowed airways and affects breathing. Common symptoms are shortness of breath, chest pain and pressure, coughing, and wheezing. Triggers include allergens, pollution and other irritants, infections, cold air, heavy physical exertion, poor digestive function, hormonal imbalances, stress, and anxiety. Diagnosis is by history and lung function tests, plus blood tests, imaging of the chest and sinuses, and allergy testing as needed.","Supplements: optimize vitamin D, as it supports immune health and slows declining lung function. Magnesium can reduce asthma severity. Vitamin C (500–1,000 mg daily) acts as an antioxidant that reduces free-radical damage and inflammation. B vitamins support cognitive function and immune health — specifically B3 and B12 lower wheezing and act as an antihistamine. Choline (3 g daily) reduces the severity and frequency of asthma attacks. Essential fatty acids (1–2 tbsp flaxseed oil or 4–8 g fish oil daily). Zinc supports adrenal health and relieves stress; quercetin reduces the release of histamine. Pycnogenol (1 mg per pound of body weight daily) is a natural anti-inflammatory herb — don't take it if you have diabetes or high blood pressure. Lycopene (130 mg daily) has been shown to help with exercise-induced asthma. Boswellia (800 mg three times a day) improves lung function. Others: carnitine, ginkgo biloba, adrenal nutrients, N-acetyl cysteine (NAC), bromelain (500 mg three times a day on an empty stomach), and astragalus (500–1,000 mg twice daily) to help prevent the respiratory infections that can trigger asthma — do not use astragalus if you have a fever. Thymus extract (as directed on the label or by a functional/integrative doctor) reduces asthma attacks in children.","For children, prevention can begin during pregnancy: fish oil EPA/DHA (2–4 g daily for the mother), vitamin C (1,000 mg daily), magnesium (200 mg daily), vitamin D (1,000–4,000 IU daily), vitamin B6 (1–20 mg daily), and Cordyceps sinensis (100–500 mg daily, or a 5:1 tincture, thirty drops twice daily).","Homeopathy: during an asthma attack, take the indicated remedy at 30C every ten to fifteen minutes until symptoms subside; long-term use of the correct constitutional remedy can reduce susceptibility to attacks, taken at 6C every fifteen minutes for up to ten doses. Aconitum napellus (30C) — sudden attacks, especially after exposure to cold dry wind, fear of dying, anxiety, worse at night. Antimonium tartaricum — exhaustion and weakness with rattly mucus in the lungs that can't be coughed up, pale, cold, clammy skin. Arsenicum album — attacks between midnight and 2:00 am, restless, anxious, cold, thirsty for sips of warm water, better sitting up. Carbo vegetabilis — chilly and faint, better when fanned or near a window, fullness in the upper abdomen and chest, relief from burping, worse from talking, eating, or lying down. Chamomilla — irritable, fault-finding, wants to be carried. Cuprum — attacks involving vomiting after each spasm. Ignatia — attack triggered by an emotional breakup, grief, or upset. Ipecac — nausea with a heavy chest and mucus causing coughing, gagging, and vomiting. Hepar sulph — improves in damp conditions. Kali carbonicum — worse between 2:00 and 4:00 am, relief from sitting with face on knees leaning forward, chilly, tired, pale. Lachesis — worse in the morning on waking, sits hunched forward, throat constriction worse from anything worn around the neck. Medorrhinum — chronic asthma with craving for fruit like oranges, prone to respiratory infections. Natrum sulphuricum — associated with early-morning diarrhea, worse in damp cold weather and 4:00–5:00 am. Nux vomica — worse after digestive upset and around 4:00 am, irritable and chilly. Phosphorus — much coughing and thirst, better when comforted or held. Sambucus nigra — sudden attacks in the middle of the night with blue extremities, unable to lie down, better sitting, congested nose. Pulsatilla pratensis — yellow-greenish phlegm with gagging, better in fresh air and comforting, worse after fatty foods and in a hot stuffy room. If mucus production is the major symptom: ipecac, Kali carb, and Blatta orientalis (acute/subacute attacks with bronchial obstruction and coarse rales). If spasm or edema is the major symptom: Arsenicum album, Antimonium tartaricum, drosera (dry hoarse cough with temporary facial cyanosis from speaking or laughing), lachesis, sambucus, aralia racemosa (attack every night on going to bed), cuprum metallicum (cough at night or on breathing cold air, improved after drinking cold water), and lung histamine (15C) or histaminum (7C) for histamine-dependent symptoms. If anxious: ignatia amara, gelsemium, arsenicum, or lachesis.","Acupressure: LU1, LU6, LU9, LU10, K27, LU7, ST13, ST16, B36, CV17, B12, B13, K6, and the Ding Chuan point. For allergic asthma, add LI4 and K6.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can help manage allergies and mucus production. Make a homemade vapor rub with eucalyptus, lemon, and peppermint oils to help open airways. Frankincense can improve inflammation and anxiety, and can be combined with sandalwood and lavender especially when symptoms are tied to emotional upset. Asthma from allergen exposure benefits from chamomile and helichrysum, which have an antispasmodic action. Others: caraway, Roman chamomile, niaouli, lemon balm, marjoram, rosemary, geranium, myrtle, and clove.","Other recommendations: consider Nambudripad's Allergy Elimination Techniques (NAET) and salt inhalation therapy with a Himalayan salt crystal lamp."],"conditions":["Asthma"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_pulm_copd","chapter":"pulmonology","title":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease — the Holistic Rx","body":["COPD is a progressive disease that causes coughing, large amounts of mucus, wheezing, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and other symptoms; it usually refers to emphysema and long-lasting bronchitis, which lead to blockage of the bronchial tubes and air sacs. Other symptoms include frequent respiratory infections, increased mucus production, and difficulty exhaling. Diagnosis is with chest X-ray, CT scan to assess severity, and blood and sputum testing.","Supplements: NAC (1,200–1,800 mg daily). Ginseng, especially Panax ginseng, helps decrease bacteria in the lungs. Magnesium. Others: Pelargonium sidoides for stages II and III COPD (thirty drops three times a day for twenty-four weeks), and L-carnitine — but don't take L-carnitine if you have hypothyroidism or a history of seizures.","Homeopathy: Bryonia (30C) — painful, stabbing, dry cough with chest pain and headache, relieved by supporting the elbows on the back of a chair, very thirsty. Belladonna — sudden onset, flushed face, high temperature, worse at night and when lying down, with headache. Kali bicromium — stringy phlegm that's difficult to cough up, worse between 4:00 and 5:00 am. Causticum — coughing that leads to involuntary passing of urine. Hepar sulph — chilly, irritable, choking cough made worse by uncovering a part of the body. Rumex — dry, tickly cough at the back of the throat, worse from talking and cold air, worse at night, prevents sleep, better with head under the blanket. Stannum — sweetish-tasting phlegm, sore dry throat, violent cough in the evening, hardly able to talk, worse from talking or laughing. Sticta — dry hacking cough at night, worse from bringing up phlegm.","Acupressure: for COPD, use LU1, LU6, LU9, K27, and ST13 (for bronchitis specifically, use LU1, LU9, K27, and ST13).","Aromatherapy: eucalyptus oil can be helpful for COPD (not suitable around young children). Others: basil, cedarwood, peppermint, hyssop, and thyme thymol."],"conditions":["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_general_dd","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Rheumatology — the Holistic Rx (diet, detox and lifestyle)","body":["Tired of being achy and breaky? Extinguish the fire in your muscles and joints. Food sensitivities can lead to pain, so consider eliminating nightshades along with the general foundations of good health: incorporating anti-inflammatory herbs like boswellia, garlic, ginger, turmeric, and devil's claw. Foods high in sulfur reduce joint inflammation. Natural forms of collagen include bone broth, amino acids, chondroitin sulfates, and glucosamine, plus antioxidants to lower inflammation. High-antioxidant and high-fiber foods are important, along with foods rich in B12, C, D, folic acid, and magnesium. Melatonin-rich foods matter too. Eat foods high in tryptophan (turkey, nuts, grass-fed dairy, wild fish, free-range chicken, and sesame seeds). Sea veggies, wild-caught fish, alkaline foods, and green leafy vegetables are high in minerals for bone formation and optimal health. Muscle spasms can be due to magnesium deficiency and low potassium.","The Four Big S's — special considerations: managing stress is key to improving immunity by combining weight-bearing activities (three times a week) with aerobic exercise, flexibility work, and resistance and strength training to help strengthen bones and build bone mass. Yoga, lumbar stabilization exercises to improve core strength, tai chi, and Pilates can help increase range of motion and improve stiffness and pain. Mindfulness-based stress reduction techniques, EFT tapping, journaling, forgiveness work, and properly addressing trauma can all improve overall inflammation and pain."],"conditions":["Osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Chronic pain","Fibromyalgia","Gout","Osteoporosis","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Back pain","Neck and shoulder pain","Muscle cramps and spasms","Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_arthritis","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Arthritis (osteoarthritis, rheumatoid, infective) — the Holistic Rx","body":["Arthritis is a chronic condition characterized by inflammation of the joints. Three common types are osteoarthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and rheumatoid arthritis. Osteoarthritis is a degenerative joint disease that occurs when joint cartilage breaks down, leading to swelling, redness, pain (worse with activity), morning stiffness that resolves after movement, limited range of motion, warm-to-touch joints, muscle weakness, bony knobs near the outermost finger joints, and clicking sounds in the joints. Diagnosis is made via a detailed history, physical exam, and imaging.","Supplements: fish oil (1,000 mg of EPA/DHA per day); turmeric (1,000 mg a day); proteolytic enzymes like bromelain; ginger (up to 2 g per day in divided doses); glucosamine sulfate (1,500 mg daily) combined with chondroitin (800–1,200 mg per day) — caution if severely allergic to shellfish, and note chondroitin is made from bovine cartilage, so vegetarians should be cautioned; SAMe (600–1,200 mg per day); capsaicin cream (0.015–0.075%, up to four times per day). Others: vitamin B6 (10–20 mg/day), vitamin C (1,000 mg/day), vitamin D, vitamin E, calcium, green tea (one cup brewed, at least 279 mg EGCG split into two to three doses), silymarin (200 mg twice daily), carotenoids (lutein + astaxanthin, 2–4 mg), grapeseed extract (200 mg divided AM and PM), resveratrol (250 mg), boswellia (300 mg twice daily), pomegranate (250–500 mg twice daily), ginger (500–1,000 mg), licorice (1.5 g), glycyrrhizic acid (400 mg, take no more than one month, then take one month off), NAC (600 mg), methylsulfonylmethane/MSM (2,000–8,000 mg daily), and collagen (40 mg daily of undenatured collagen).","Homeopathy for arthritis: Calcarea fluorica — malnutrition of the bones with enlarged hypermobile joints, bone spurs, depression, and increased night urination; better with heat and warmth, worse with rest and weather changes. Rhus tox — stiffness worse in the morning, improves with movement and warmth, aggravated by cold, damp, and immobility. Bryonia — severe pain improved by cold and rest, worse with heat and movement; swollen hot joints, irritability. Pulsatilla — joint pain aggravated by heat, improved by coolness, pain wanders joint to joint, weepy patient. Calcarea phosphorica — joint and bone pain and spurs (mostly neck) that feel numb, stiff, and cold, aggravated by cold and weather changes, weakness climbing stairs. Ledum — painful, swollen, warm joints progressing up the body, used after steroid injections or for small joints like the toes; worse at night from bed warmth, relieved by cold and rest; useful in gouty arthritis, especially with alcohol cravings. Arnica — bruising joint pain triggered or worsened by injury. Aconite — sudden severe flare-ups in cold or dry weather with anxiety and restlessness. Apis — joints painful to touch, hot, or swollen with pinkish edema; better with cold, worse with pressure/touch. Belladonna — red, hot, burning, swollen joints with sudden onset, worse with motion. Dulcamara — joint pain aggravated by cold, damp weather, after overheating, and after rest, improved with movement; may have digestive symptoms like diarrhea. Mercurius — aches worse at night, with heat and cold, and offensive sweats. Ruta graveolens — articular pain from trauma, improved with movement, aggravated by rest. Radium bromatum — knee and lumbosacral pain, joints feel like they'll give way, improved by heat/movement, worse with prolonged rest and at night. Rhododendron — erratic/searing pain aggravated by stormy weather, improves as the storm breaks. Kali bichromicum — sudden well-localized pain improved with motion; sciatica on the left side; coccydynia worse with sitting. Calcarea carbonica — joint pain worse from cold and dampness in an overweight, cold-sensitive person. Sulphur — burning pains improved by cold. Colchicum — pain and stiffness worse in winter and at night.","Infective (septic) arthritis — use homeopathics alongside antibiotics: Hepar sulph for joints swollen with pus, sensitive to slightest pressure and drafts; Aconite for sudden-onset infection with pain, fever, and apprehension; Bryonia for painful, swollen joints sensitive to the slightest touch and movement.","Knee pain: Benzoic acid — painful swollen knee joints with a cracking sound, skin over the joint very dry. Causticum — knees that crack on walking; also neck/jaw pain that improves with warmth or wet weather. Berberis — stiff, sore knees. Agnus — knees that feel cold.","Shoulder pain: Sanguinaria — right shoulder pain worse with movement or pressure, wears off during sleep. Chelidonium — right shoulder pain and pain around the shoulder blade with headaches. Ferrum — shoulder pain worse around midnight after being cold, overheated, or sitting still, improved with walking. Rhus tox — burning shoulder pain, wears off with gentle exercise, worse in cold damp weather and after sleep/rest. Sulphur — shoulder pain with a tendency to bring the shoulder forward, feels heavy and dead. Small joints of the hand: Actaea spicata (pain at the proximal interphalangeal joints with Bouchard's nodes), Polygonum aviculare (pain at the distal interphalangeal joint with Heberden's nodes) — 3X of each, twenty drops daily in water, held in the mouth before swallowing; Caulophyllum for intermittent pain wandering between small joints; Viola odorata for wrist and metacarpal pain radiating to the arm, worse in the morning and improved after rising. Heel pain: Hekla lava, especially with calcaneal spurs (5C twice daily, with Medorrhinum 15C twice monthly).","Acupressure: K3, K6, ST41, B60, B62, and LI11 (elbow and shoulder pain); TW5, LI4 (upper extremity and neck pain); SP5 and K3 (ankle pain); GB20 and ST36. Others: Ba Feng, Ba Xie, and Yanglingquan.","Aromatherapy: essential oils reduce stress, relax tight muscles, and improve pain and well-being. Frankincense reduces inflammation. Lavender is a mild analgesic and antispasmodic. Marjoram relieves arthritis pain. Black pepper, eucalyptus, and ginger stimulate circulation and have analgesic, anti-inflammatory properties. Nutmeg, peppermint, and yarrow oils decrease inflammation. Lemongrass with anti-inflammatory rosemary can improve pain. Other helpful oils: myrrh, turmeric, orange, lemon verbena, mugwort, birch leaf, cedarwood, cypress, wintergreen, helichrysum, star anise, juniper, parsley, benzoin, plai, jasmine, rose, chamomile, angelica, Canadian balsam, bay laurel, bergamot, silver fir, and rosemary. Knee pain benefits from peppermint and eucalyptus."],"conditions":["Osteoarthritis","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_chronic_pain","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Chronic Pain, Back Pain and Neck Pain — the Holistic Rx","body":["Pain that comes on suddenly and lasts up to six weeks is acute; pain lasting more than three months is chronic. Back pain is a shooting, stabbing, or aching pain in the back that may radiate down the leg, limiting range of motion. Causes include muscle or ligament strain, a bulging/prolapsed/ruptured disk, osteoporosis, skeletal irregularities, and/or arthritis. Diagnosis follows a detailed history, physical exam, and imaging that may include X-rays, MRI/CT, bone scan, nerve studies, and blood work to find the root cause.","Supplements for back pain: fish oil (2,000 mg daily); turmeric (1,000 mg daily); proteolytic enzymes and bromelain (500 mg three times daily between meals) and papain (500 mg three times daily); MSM (2,000–8,000 mg daily); magnesium (400–500 mg daily) and calcium (500 mg). Others: devil's claw (50–100 mg harpagoside), white willow (240 mg salicin daily, or 5 ml of tincture three times daily), collagen (40 mg daily of undenatured collagen, to reduce back pain caused by osteoarthritis), ginger (1,000 mg/day), turmeric (300 mg three times daily).","Supplements for chronic pain generally: vitamin C for chronic regional pain syndrome (500 mg on the day of fracture and daily for fifty days thereafter, to help prevent chronic regional pain syndrome); antioxidants and vitamins; bromelain (2,000 mg in two divided doses) with quercetin (500 mg twice daily) — contraindicated in pregnancy, bleeding disorders, and uncontrolled blood pressure. Topical: castor oil, charcoal poultices, hot ginger tea, salt packs, cold mashed tofu, Topricin, homeopathic cream, and mustard foot baths. SAMe, MSM, and DLPA (don't combine DLPA with MAO inhibitor drugs). Enzyme supplementation (1 capsule three times daily with meals). B vitamins. Carnosine, alpha lipoic acid, acetyl-carnitine, and chromium. Omega-3, vitamins A, C, E, calcium, and magnesium. Antioxidants: CoQ10, DMAE, and manganese. Adaptogens: Arctic root, ashwagandha, ginseng, astragalus, cordyceps, dong quai, eleuthero, maitake, and milk thistle/reishi. Glucosamine and chondroitin (glucosamine 1,500 mg/day). Turmeric. Resveratrol. Melatonin.","Homeopathy: for acute back pain, take a 30C potency four times daily; for chronic back pain, take twice daily for two weeks to assess improvement. Cervical spondylosis (wear and tear of spinal disks): Argentum nitricum for headaches behind the forehead with flatulence and limb numbness; Agaricus for tendinitis of the back of the neck with weakness, shaking, and chest constriction; Picric acid for shooting burning pain up the spine into the head. Coccydynia (tailbone pain): Hypericum for coccyx pain from a fall; Causticum for an achy, bruised coccyx improved by wet, damp weather; Silicea for pain and constipation increased by pressure and drafts; Antimonium tartaricum for a heavy-feeling coccyx; Cicuta for jerking, tearing, recurrent pain; Kali bichromicum when pain worsens with walking or sitting.","Homeopathy for back pain: Aconite for sharp pain worse with drafts and cold, dry weather. Arnica for pain after injury, bruised and sore feeling. Rhus tox for lower back pain that feels bruised, improves with movement, worsens with damp cold weather and rest — best for sprained muscles or ligaments. Bryonia for lower back pain and stiffness in dry cold weather, worse with the slightest movement, tender and bruised, better when rubbed. Aesculus for lower back or sacral pain radiating to the right hip, worse with sitting, walking, or stooping. Antimonium tartaricum for continuous pain with nausea, vomiting, fatigue, and cold sweats, worse with cold and eating, better standing and moving. Sulphur for violent stitching pain worse with movement and bed warmth. Cimicifuga for lower back pain, stiffness, and bruised aching muscles that prevent sleep and cause restlessness, improved with warmth. Ignatia for cramping and back spasms following emotional stress. Magnesia phosphorica for back spasms improved with warmth. Dulcamara for pain worse with exertion, cold, and stooping. Rhododendron for back pain worse in cold weather or before a thunderstorm. Nux vomica for pain aggravated by cold or movement, with irritability, cold sensitivity, and constipation, improved with warmth. Ruta graveolens for neck and lower back pain, best for sprains or strains, worse at night.","Homeopathy for neck pain: Cimicifuga for neck stiffness with sensitivity of the whole cervical and upper thoracic spine. Lachnanthes for pain in the upper arm, elbow, and right side of the neck with perspiration, aggravated by movement, and for a stiff neck forcing the head to bend. Dulcamara for pain at the top of the nape, aggravated by cold, improved by heat. Bryonia for pain worse with movement and the slightest touch. Causticum for pain at the top of the nape with stiffness between the shoulder blades, improved by wet weather. Actaea racemosa for cervical pain in those who sit bent forward for work, aggravated by cold and menses, improved by heat.","Acupressure: B25, B31, B40, B20 (middle back pain); GB20 and GV14 (upper back pain); B48 (stress); B54 (arthritis-related pain). Others: B23, B22, B25, CV6, B18, B30, B60, B53, GV4. Ankle/foot pain: K3, K6, ST41, B60, B62, GB34, GB39, GB40, SP4. Hip and pelvis pain: GB29, GB30, GB31, GB34, B28, B48. Knee pain: B54, B57, ST36, SP10, SP8, SP6. Upper/mid back pain: B13, B38, B18, B42, SI11, SI10. Lower back: B23, B47, B28, B48, B54, GB30, B60, GV4.","Aromatherapy: peppermint and wintergreen oil cool inflamed areas; frankincense and cypress oil reduce inflammation; lemongrass essential oil can improve back spasms; peppermint and lavender oils help relax inflamed areas and ease cramps. Others for pain and cramps: tarragon, coriander, camphor, grapefruit, bay laurel, niaouli, marjoram, geranium, allspice, black pepper, rosemary, ginger, anise, Canadian balsam, basil linalool, clove bud, citriodora, fennel, jasmine. Cinnamon and eucalyptus also help chronic pain. Back pain also improves with ginger, juniper, thyme, and clary sage."],"conditions":["Chronic pain","Back pain","Neck and shoulder pain"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_chronic_fatigue","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Chronic Fatigue Syndrome — the Holistic Rx","body":["Chronic fatigue syndrome is a chronic disorder marked by extreme fatigue unexplained by any underlying medical condition. Symptoms include fatigue, sore throat, pain that moves from joint to joint without swelling, headaches, unrefreshing sleep, extreme exhaustion lasting more than twenty-four hours after mental or physical exertion, loss of memory or concentration, mood changes, swollen lymph glands in the armpits or neck, and unexplained muscle pain. Hormonal causes can include thyroid dysfunction, adrenal insufficiency, DHEA or testosterone deficiency, or parasitic/persistent infections like mycoplasma, HHV-6, CMV, EBV, and Chlamydia. Diagnosis follows a detailed history and physical, meeting at least four of the above symptoms, with persistent fatigue for six months or more.","Supplements: ashwagandha/Indian ginseng (250 mg daily); green superfood powder (wheatgrass, spirulina, matcha); vitamin B-complex with B6 and B12 for energy metabolism; rhodiola (300 mg daily); magnesium (200–300 mg daily); zinc (50 mg daily for three months, then 15–25 mg daily).","Homeopathy: Arnica montana for fatigue with a bruised-muscle feeling. Kali phosphoricum for fatigue and mental weariness, especially following an illness like the flu, with anxiety, depression, nightmares, insomnia, agoraphobia, fear of crowds, memory loss, and muscle weakness/pain worse with exercise, cold, and mental effort but better with sleep, eating, and gentle movement. Gelsemium for weakness, muscle aches, dizziness, trembling, twitching, a bruised-muscle feeling, dull head heaviness, and blurred vision, worse in cold damp weather with anxiety, sometimes following emotional grief, improved after profuse urination. Phosphorus for extreme fatigue with a strong craving for carbonated drinks. Lacticum acidum for fatigue with bone pain, aversion to exercise, and a plugged-throat sensation. Mercurius solubilis for fatigue after Epstein-Barr virus, persistent sore throat, enlarged glands, excess salivation, sensitivity to cold and heat, memory loss, and poor name recall. Physostigma venenosum for a strong sense of weakness and fatigue, sore and stiff all over as if from a cold, with violent trembling. Rhus toxicodendron for fatigue with pain relieved by heat, aggravated by cold. Phosphoric acid for weakness and exhaustion with prolonged diarrhea, depression, blue rings around the eyes, a worn-out thin pale appearance, poor appetite, thirst, and sensitivity to cold and music; also for nervous exhaustion and brain fog after intense mental activity, with heaviness and burning in the limbs or spine. Calcarea carbonica for fatigue with headaches, constipation, swollen glands, anxiety, cold sensitivity, easy spreading of symptoms, and multiple food allergies. Arsenicum album for fatigue with insomnia between midnight and 2:00 am, anxiety, depression, and cold sensitivity, worse from cold. Silica for those who are thin, cold-sensitive, prone to constipation, and get sick easily. China for exhaustion and weakness after dehydration.","Acupressure: GB14, ST36, TW4, K27, SP4, B62, CV6, GV14. Others: SP3, SP6, SP9, ST36, GB21 (improves circulation, decreases anxiety, headaches, irritability); P6 (sleep, anxiety, indigestion); B23 and B47 (fatigue, confusion, sleep support); LU3 (fatigue, confusion, lightheadedness, head pain); TW5 (joint pain); GV20; CV4.","Aromatherapy: frankincense supports neurological health and healing to improve chronic fatigue syndrome. Peppermint oil improves focus and fatigue. Lavender and rosemary stimulate the nervous system and muscles. Tea tree oil strengthens the immune system, along with cinnamon, cistus, clove, myrrh, eucalyptus radiata, marjoram, rosewood, thyme linalool, and vetiver. Energizing oils like mandarin, lemon, and grapefruit can also help."],"conditions":["Chronic fatigue syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_fibromyalgia","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Fibromyalgia — the Holistic Rx","body":["Fibromyalgia is a chronic condition characterized by pain in the muscles, tendons, and ligaments with multiple tender points on the body, fatigue, sleep disturbance, cognitive difficulties like \"fibro fog\" and trouble concentrating, and mood issues. Headaches, depression, and pain/cramping may also occur. Diagnosis follows a detailed history and physical with eighteen specific tender points, ruling out other diagnoses via blood testing.","Supplements: magnesium (500 mg per day); fish oil (1,000 mg per day); probiotic; vitamin D; digestive enzymes; activated B vitamins; supplements for sleep, detoxification, and treating chronic low-grade infections (see the autoimmune protocol). For mitochondrial support: acetyl-L-carnitine (1,500 mg per day), alpha lipoic acid (400–600 mg per day), CoQ10 (100–300 mg per day), malic acid (1,200–2,400 mg per day in divided doses). Others: turmeric with black pepper (1,000 mg per day); ashwagandha and rhodiola (500–1,000 mg per day); 5-HTP (50 mg one to three times per day); D-ribose (5 g three times per day for three weeks, then twice daily); malic acid (1,000–1,200 mg twice daily); methylsulfonylmethane/MSM (start 1,000 mg three times daily, increasing until pain relief is evident, up to 10,000 mg daily; reduce dose if diarrhea occurs); arginine (500–600 mg/day divided between morning and bedtime); SAMe; Myers' Cocktail; ascorbigen (100 mg/day); broccoli powder (400 mg/day); vitamin B1/thiamine (25–100 mg/day); vitamin C (3,000 mg/day, with a vitamin C flush once a week); quercetin (500–1,000 mg three to four times per day); glutamine (start 8 g/day for four weeks); NAC (500–1,000 mg, or 650 mg/day for three months); and iron and hormonal support if needed for the adrenals, thyroid, and DHEA. For children: bromelain (50–100 mg daily), fish oil (500–1,000 mg daily of EPA/DHA), magnesium (200 mg daily), zinc (10 mg daily), calcium (200–1,000 mg daily), CoQ10 (100 mg daily).","Homeopathy: Rhus tox for pain and stiffness worse with stillness, improved with movement. Ruta grav for a bruised feeling, worse in cold damp weather with no definite benefit from warmth. Rhododendron for pain worse with storms and weather changes. Arnica for pain that feels bruised and tender, worse after exertion, as if the bed is too hard. Causticum for soreness and stiffness worse in dry weather, exertion, and cold, improved with warmth and rain. Bryonia for pain worse with the slightest movement and warmth, improved with coolness — patient doesn't want to be touched. Kalmia latifolia for shooting muscle pain with numbness or cold sensations. Cimicifuga for sore, bruised muscles with neck stiffness, worse in cold, prone to hormone imbalances and depression. Calcarea carbonica for muscle soreness, clammy hands and feet, anxiety, easy fatigue, and a contracted feeling in joints and muscles, improved with warmth, aggravated by cold, dampness, and exertion. Ignatia for tight, cramping muscles triggered by emotional upset or stress. Magnesia phosphorica for cramping muscles improved with warmth. Nux vomica for tight muscles with cold sensitivity, digestive issues, irritability, and fatigue, improved with warmth, worse in cold weather. Pulsatilla for painful joints that move from joint to joint, possibly tied to hormonal imbalance or the menstrual cycle — patient depressed and tearful. Angustura vera for stiffness, muscle spasms, and cramps, especially in the lower back, and muscle aches from poor posture, improved with stretching and cold contact, aggravated by pressure and inactivity.","Acupressure: LU1, LV3 (relaxed muscles and nerves); GV16, GB20, GV24.5, CV6, SP21, SP6, SP10, ST36, LI4, LI11, P6 (anxiety, heart palpitations, indigestion); CV17, B38 (increases sleep, relieves stress); CV17 (immune system, decreases depression and anxiety).","Aromatherapy: helichrysum oil improves nerve tissue healing and circulation. Lavender oil reduces the emotional stress associated with fibromyalgia. Muscle aches and pains can be relieved with eucalyptus, lavender, peppermint, nutmeg, rosemary, black pepper, cypress, clove, thyme, chamomile, and ginger, which reduce inflammation and improve circulation to the muscles. Juniper and Melissa help break down toxins. Essential oils for stress and sleep are also key: chamomile, jasmine, lavender, and rose.","Other recommendations: cranial electrotherapy stimulation, myofascial release, floatation therapy, and magnetic therapy to help improve pain; postural therapies, the Feldenkrais method, the Alexander technique, prolotherapy, and laser therapy."],"conditions":["Fibromyalgia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_gout","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Gout — the Holistic Rx","body":["Gout is a kind of arthritis caused by too much uric acid in the blood, leading to swelling, stiffness, and burning pain in the joints, most commonly the big toe. Other symptoms include purplish skin, limited movement, peeling or itching, and warmth of the skin around the affected joint. Diagnosis follows a detailed history, exam, blood work, and imaging.","Supplements: celery seed extract (450 mg two to three times daily); black cherry juice/extract; nettles (250 mg three times daily) as an anti-inflammatory; fish oil (1,000 mg of EPA/DHA per day); proteolytic enzymes; magnesium; vitamin C (500 mg two to three times daily) to reduce the risk of gout.","Homeopathy: Arnica for deep, bruising pain and gout attacks after repeated trauma or physical effort, with joints sensitive to any contact. Colchicum for painful joints, especially at night, worse with movement, and associated with irritability, weakness, nausea, and depression. Ledum for hot, throbbing, slightly swollen joints affecting the knees or feet, improved by ice, cold water, and movement, worse at night and with bed warmth. Urtica for joints that burn and itch. Benzoic acid for symptoms accompanied by strong-smelling urine. Lycopodium for symptoms worse from 4:00 to 8:00 pm, with one foot hot and the other cold. Pulsatilla for pain that moves joint to joint, improved with cold and gentle motion. Bryonia for pain worse with the slightest touch in an irritable individual, with painful edema, improved with rest and heat. Belladonna for red, hot, throbbing joints with sudden-onset pain. Rhododendron for big-toe pain that flares before a storm, improved with warmth. Sulfur for burning sensation and itching of the skin, aggravated by heat, improved by warmth. Rhus toxicodendron for dark red, swollen joints, improved with movement and heat. Apis mellifica for reddish-pink, swollen, painful joints improved with cold. Lachesis mutus for pseudo-attacks with purplish swelling and throbbing pain with extreme sensitivity to touch.","Acupressure: LV3, SP3, SP6, ST36, ST40.","Aromatherapy: peppermint oil reduces pain, and frankincense can reduce swelling. Juniper oil helps break down toxic deposits and carry them away from the body — add to a hot sitz bath or a compress, dilute in a carrier oil, and/or use in massage (but don't rub the affected joint directly). Others: basil linalool, cajeput, Roman chamomile, cinnamon, cypress, fennel, fragonia, juniper, lemon, peppermint, pine, rosemary (cineole and camphor), and rosewood.","Other recommendations: magnet therapy can be effective in alleviating the pain of gout."],"conditions":["Gout"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_muscle_cramps","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Muscle Cramps and Spasms — the Holistic Rx","body":["Muscle cramps, pain, and spasms anywhere in the body can be due to poor circulation of the legs, exercise, dehydration, magnesium or potassium deficiency, insufficient stretching, overexertion, certain medications (like statins and blood pressure medications), or malfunctioning nerves. Diagnosis to find the root cause is determined through a detailed history, physical exam, blood work, and imaging as needed.","Supplements: magnesium (250 mg twice daily); potassium (300 mg daily); green superfood powder (chlorella or spirulina); calcium (500 mg twice daily); MSM (500 mg three times daily).","Homeopathy: Cuprum for severe cramping beginning with muscle twitching, mainly in the calves and feet, and can also help prevent cramps (may be taken for fourteen days). Nux vomica for cramps, headache, constipation, nausea, loss of appetite, heartburn, fatigue, irritability, and cold sensitivity, worse at night and in cold weather, improved with warmth. Arnica for cramping with fatigued, bruised, tender muscles, worse after exertion. Calcarea for muscle spasm and soreness after exertion and in cold damp climates, with head sweats in an overweight, pale, fatigued, cold-sensitive person who craves sweets and eggs. Camphora for cramps in cold-feeling calves and feet. Veratrum for calf cramps with recent diarrhea and vomiting, improved with massage, worse with walking. Chamomilla for cramps in the thighs or legs. Rhus toxicodendron for pain and stiffness worse in the morning or after immobility or cold, improved with movement and warmth. Magnesia phosphorica for cramping improved with warmth. Ignatia amara for tight, spasmodic cramps after emotional upset or stress. Cimicifuga for bruised, sore muscles worse in cold, in those prone to hormonal imbalances and depression. Causticum for painful joints that become stiff and sore from overuse, improved with warmth, aggravated by cold or dry weather. Others: cactus grandiflorus, lachesis mutus, lacticum acidum, and viburnum opulus.","Acupressure: B57, GB34, ST36, LV3, GV26 (lower extremity). Leg cramps: B40, B60, B57. Arms: LI11, TW15, P6, TW14, TW5. Foot cramps: LV3 and LV2.","Aromatherapy: muscle aches can be relieved by peppermint oil, coconut oil, and cypress oil rubbed on sore muscles; if aches or cramps occur at night, use black pepper instead of peppermint. Frankincense and cypress oil reduce inflammation. Lemongrass essential oil can help improve back spasms. Peppermint and lavender oils help relax inflamed areas and ease cramps. Others for leg cramps: marjoram, rosemary, and hyssop."],"conditions":["Muscle cramps and spasms"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_rheum_osteoporosis","chapter":"rheumatology","title":"Osteoporosis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Osteoporosis is a chronic condition where bones gradually become brittle and weak, as new bone formation doesn't keep up with the removal of old bone, increasing fracture risk. As a \"silent disease,\" it presents with few symptoms until it causes extensive vertebral damage leading to spinal curvature (kyphosis), loss of height, stooped posture, and back pain. Causes include aging, inactivity, steroid use, low vitamin D, nutritional deficiencies, emotional stress, kidney failure, chronic thyroid or adrenal disease, excessive alcohol, tobacco use, eating disorders, autoimmune disease, and long-term use of medications like gastric reflux drugs, cancer medications, and corticosteroids. Diagnosis is made via a bone mineral density test or other imaging as needed.","Supplements: magnesium (500 mg per day); calcium (500–600 mg twice daily in divided doses — calcium citrate is best absorbed; others include citrate-malate, chelate, and hydroxyapatite); fish oil (4 g daily with 3,000 mg of evening primrose oil); vitamin D3 (5,000 IU per day); vitamin K2 (100 mcg MK7 daily). Others: strontium (680 mg daily) and silicon (50–20 mg daily), B6 (25–50 mg per day), folic acid (800 mcg per day), B12 (800 mcg per day), and green tea (three to five cups per day, or 250–500 mg of polyphenols per day).","Homeopathy: you may take the indicated remedy as long as symptoms continue and stop when they improve (bone density improvement isn't directly felt, but overall bone-health symptoms can be observed) — work with a professional to find the constitutional remedy. Homeopathy is most effective for prevention and treatment of mild osteoporosis. Calcarea fluorica for malnutrition of the bones with enlarged hypermobile joints, bone spurs, depression, and increased night urination, improved with heat and warmth, worse with rest and weather changes. Folliculinum for osteoporosis related to menopause. Silica for those who are nervous, cold, get sick constantly, and are thin. Calcarea carbonica for osteoporosis with muscle cramps, swollen joints, aching bones, fatigue, feeling overwhelmed, and being overweight, aggravated by cold and dampness, craving sweets, eggs, and milk. Calcarea phosphorica for fractures or osteoporosis, helping boost bone building, and for asymptomatic bone support; may be associated with calcium deposits and neck/back pain with stiffness, with sensitivity to cold drafts. Phosphorus for weak bones or slow-healing fractures in tall, very social, thin people who crave ice-cold drinks. Symphytum to reduce pain and heal fractures.","Acupressure: K10, SP3, LV1, P3, ST36 (improve digestion); LU1 (stress); LI4 (pain); GV24.5 (hormonal balance); SP5 and K3 (ankle pain).","Aromatherapy: essential oils like cypress, helichrysum, and fir oil can aid bone repair. Black pepper and rosemary help soothe aching joints and bones. Oils to relieve stress — lavender, rose, and geranium — also support overall health."],"conditions":["Osteoporosis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_uro_general_dd","chapter":"urology","title":"Urologic health — the Holistic Rx (diet and special considerations)","body":["Optimize prostate health by eating green leafy vegetables (Brussels sprouts and other cruciferous veggies), cilantro, watercress, rosemary, dandelion leaves, basil, thyme, blueberries, green tea, and lycopene-rich foods like cooked tomatoes. Optimize vitamin D, E, and omega-3s. Herbs like ginger, rosemary, and turmeric have anti-inflammatory properties. Improve testosterone levels with sesame, watercress, Brazil nuts, and zinc-rich foods like pumpkin seeds. Optimize urinary health with cranberries, peaches, wheatgrass, celery, black raspberries, parsley, garlic, thyme, and oregano.","Improve libido with natural aphrodisiacs like avocados, figs, and bananas; foods rich in vitamin C, zinc, magnesium, collagen, and iron; and dark chocolate, watermelon, asparagus, celery, sweet potatoes, Brazil nuts, almonds, walnuts, beets, and plenty of water. Tyrosine (for dopamine) is found in seaweed, soy protein, egg whites, salmon, turkey, wild game, shrimp, and fish eggs. Add spices like cloves, garlic, ginger, and nutmeg. Avoid liquids within two hours of bedtime, and reduce medications and substances that can lead to urinary retention, especially those with antihistamine and anticholinergic properties.","The Four Big S's: follow the general guidelines for sleep, stress, sweat (exercise), and social connection — lowering inflammation through these foundations can decrease urinary retention and optimize urinary and prostatic health."],"conditions":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Prostatitis","Erectile dysfunction","Urinary incontinence","Interstitial cystitis","Urinary tract infection"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_uro_bph","chapter":"urology","title":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia — the Holistic Rx","body":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a noncancerous enlargement of the prostate, the organ that produces the fluid that carries sperm during ejaculation. The prostate can become inflamed and enlarged, compressing the urethra and leading to problems with urination, bladder infections, and bladder stones. It is driven by inflammation, hormonal changes (estrogen excess), zinc deficiency (itself influenced by stress, infections, and diet), and deteriorating blood vessels. Symptoms include increased bladder pressure, sudden urge to urinate, and weak urinary flow with difficulty starting urination, a feeling of incomplete emptying, increased risk of infection and stones, dribbling after urination, urinary frequency and pain, incontinence, and nighttime urination. BPH is diagnosed with history, digital rectal exam, transrectal ultrasound, urinalysis, and lab tests such as prostate-specific antigen (PSA).","Supplements: saw palmetto (160 mg twice daily, or 320 mg three times daily for four months then down to 320 mg once daily). Zinc (50 mg daily) for prostate health, taken with 3 mg copper. Fish oil (1,000 mg daily). Pumpkin seed oil (8–10 g, 320 mg daily, or 1 tbsp daily). Nettle root (120 mg twice daily). Optimize vitamin D. Others: beta-sitosterol, also found in avocados (60–195 mg daily; divided doses up to 2,000–3,000 mg/day), pygeum (100–200 mg daily), flaxseed and SDG (300–600 mg/day), rye grass pollen (126 mg three times daily), and green tea and selenium.","Homeopathy: Thuja occidentalis is for an enlarged prostate with frequent and urgent desire to urinate. Argentum nitricum helps those who lose their erection with penetration, have pain with intercourse, or have loss of sexual desire. Baryta helps those with a slow urine stream, urinary frequency, immature impotence, and thin, underweight persons. Causticum helps those who lose urine from sneezing or coughing, with lessened sexual pleasure during orgasm and a pressure feeling from bladder into prostate. Chimaphila umbellata helps urinary retention with the sensation of a ball in the pelvic floor — swelling, soreness, and pressure worse when sitting. Clematis helps hypertrophy with dribbling and a weak stream. Ferrum picricum is for frequent nighttime urination with pressure on the rectum and sensation at the bladder neck. Iodum is for shrunken testes, a hard prostate gland, and loss of potency. Lycopodium helps an enlarged prostate with sexual dysfunction like impotence in patients who feel cold, crave sweets, and have digestive problems. Pulsatilla helps end-of-urination bladder pain in patients who feel better in fresh air and warm easily. Sabal serrulata helps painful or difficult urination with bladder or urethral spasm, urinary retention, and a cold sensation of the prostate or bladder. Selenium helps an enlarged prostate with dribbling and impotence, worse after urination and walking. Staphysagria helps chronic dribbling, urinary retention, burning of the prostate or urinary tract, impotence, and suppressed emotions. Conium maculatum is indicated for sclerosed hypertrophy of the prostate with position-changing vertigo.","Acupressure: SP6 may reduce discomfort related to prostate enlargement. Bladder points along the groin (B27–B34) can increase circulation to the prostate and reduce inflammation.","Aromatherapy: anti-inflammatory and antipathogenic essential oils can calm swelling — frankincense, myrrh, and rosemary. Massage oils around the genital area regularly to improve prostate health and stimulate blood flow."],"conditions":["Benign prostatic hyperplasia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_uro_chronic_prostatitis","chapter":"urology","title":"Chronic prostatitis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Chronic prostatitis (chronic pelvic pain syndrome) is a nonbacterial inflammation of the prostate that can cause urinary frequency, hesitancy, and persistent discomfort or pain in the lower pelvic region, mainly at the base of the penis and around the anus. Other symptoms include sexual problems like difficulty getting an erection, painful ejaculation, or pain after sex. It's diagnosed when pain persists for at least three months within the previous six months; the workup may include a prostate exam, urine sample, imaging of the urinary tract, and blood tests.","Supplements: zinc picolinate (60 mg daily until symptoms improve, then 30 mg daily). Quercetin (500 mg twice daily) — absorption improves when combined with an equivalent dose of bromelain. Omega-3 fatty acids (2–4 g EPA/DHA per day). Others: rye grass pollen extract (Cernilton), SAMe (600–1,200 mg per day), beta-sitosterol (60 mg two to three times daily; reduce to 30–60 mg daily for four more weeks once symptoms subside).","Homeopathy (6C potency most often used): Sabal is for an enlarged, cold-feeling prostate, or painful intercourse/ejaculation. Thuja helps frequent urges to urinate with a burning sensation at the bladder neck. Pulsatilla is for urgent need to urinate, worse lying on the back, possibly with a thick yellow penile discharge.","Acupressure: P6 and ST36.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can reduce swelling and pain, prevent infection, increase sexual function, and improve urinary function and blood flow — frankincense, myrrh, rosemary, and thyme, along with juniper, lavender, bergamot (antibacterial), chamomile, eucalyptus, cypress, myrtle (green or orange), pine, yarrow, and niaouli.","Other recommendations: biofeedback can be helpful."],"conditions":["Prostatitis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_uro_erectile_dysfunction","chapter":"urology","title":"Erectile dysfunction — the Holistic Rx","body":["Erectile dysfunction is the inability to achieve or maintain an erection, with many degrees and both mind- and body-related causes. Causes include endocrine disorders, nerve or neurological disorders, medications (beta blockers, chemotherapy drugs, CNS depressants like Xanax, diuretics, SSRIs), cardiac conditions (high cholesterol or hypertension), lifestyle factors, emotional disorders (anxiety, depression, substance abuse), low testosterone, stress, and heavy metal poisoning.","Supplements: L-arginine (1,000 mg two to three times daily on an empty stomach) improves blood flow and vessel dilation. L-citrulline, free form (1,500–6,000 mg per day maximum). Panax ginseng, ideally Korean red ginseng (1,800–3,000 mg/day in divided doses, depending on ginsenoside content). Niacin (250 mg three times daily) can improve blood flow. Others: DHEA (25–50 mg daily) under a doctor's supervision to improve testosterone; maca, an adaptogen herb (500 mg three times daily) to improve sexual desire; potency wood/muira puama (500 mg three times daily); Ginkgo biloba (120 mg twice daily) to improve blood flow; Cordyceps sinensis (800 mg twice daily of Cs-4 mycelium extract) to improve libido.","Homeopathy: Agnus castus helps men with recent-onset erectile difficulty, an erection not firm enough for penetration, and a small, cold penis; these men are anxious with memory/concentration issues. Selenium metallicum is for ED with increased desire for sex and dribbling of semen during sleep with erotic fantasies, in tired patients. Lycopodium is for anticipation failure, a cold small penis, a surge of desire, digestive issues, and lack of confidence. Staphysagria helps shy men with deep emotions whose ED began after an emotional or embarrassing event. Arnica is for erectile issues following a bruising injury to the penis. Hypericum is for erectile issues following a bruised spinal cord injury. Conium helps men whose erection doesn't last, with cold, cramped legs, and a great surge of sexual feeling after long abstinence. Caladium helps those who have an erection while asleep that disappears on waking, with a flaccid penis despite arousal, frequent wet dreams, and cravings for tobacco.","Acupressure: B23 and B47 can reduce fatigue and improve sexual performance. K1, K3, CV4, and B27 or B34 treat lower back pain, sexual/reproductive problems, sacral pain, and impotence. CV6 treats ED, seminal emission, nighttime urination, and hernia or reproductive problems. SP12 treats male infertility. SP13 improves ED and abdominal discomfort. H7, B15, B31, K12, CV3, CV4, CV6, and GV4 can also help; for premature ejaculation use H7, B15, B23, K12, CV3, CV4, CV6, and GV4.","Aromatherapy: essential oils can increase libido — sandalwood, ylang-ylang, and rose, along with jasmine, patchouli, anise, black pepper, cedarwood, clary sage, clove, ginger, neroli, nutmeg, peppermint, pine, rosemary (cineole and camphor types), and vetiver."],"conditions":["Erectile dysfunction"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_uro_incontinence","chapter":"urology","title":"Urinary incontinence — the Holistic Rx","body":["Urinary incontinence is the loss of bladder control resulting in unintentional release of urine, ranging from occasional leaking with sneezing or coughing to an urge so strong that clothes are wet before reaching the bathroom. Types include stress, urge, overflow, functional, and mixed incontinence. Workup may include urinalysis, a bladder diary, post-void residual measurement, urodynamic testing, and imaging like cystoscopy, cystogram, and/or pelvic ultrasound.","Supplements: fish oil (3–4 g daily). Flaxseed oil (1 tbsp daily). Vitamin C (500 mg two to three times daily). Vitamin E (400 IU daily) for anti-inflammatory support. Magnesium can help reduce bladder muscle spasms. Bromelain (400 mg three times daily between meals). Others: look for herbal formulas combining two or more of buchu (soothing diuretic and antiseptic for the urinary system), corn silk (soothing, diuretic), marshmallow root (soothing, demulcent), horsetail (astringent, mild diuretic, tissue-healing), cleavers (traditional urinary tonic), and usnea (soothing, antiseptic) — an herbalist can compound a formula, or follow label instructions. Agrimony (30 drops three times daily) can also help.","Homeopathy: Causticum is for incontinence worse with laughing or coughing. Gelsemium helps those with weak, trembly legs and drooping eyelids. Ferrum phosphoricum is for inability to control the bladder and frequent urge to urinate, with bladder-neck pain relieved by urinating. Nux vomica helps involuntary dribbling of urine in very irritable patients. Pulsatilla helps those who leak urine when lying down, coughing, sneezing, passing gas, or getting angry, often with an irritating tickly cough worse at night and looser yellow-green mucus in the morning. Sepia is for leaking urine due to shock, laughing, sneezing, or coughing, with a strong bearing-down sensation in the pelvis. Natrum muriaticum is for stress incontinence, pain during sex, and vaginal dryness, especially with a history of grief. Pareira is for urinary retention from an enlarged prostate. Zincum is for stress incontinence, inability to urinate while standing, and prostate problems leading to urinary retention.","Acupressure: SP6, B23, B28, B60, GV20, CV3, CV8, CV4, and CV6.","Aromatherapy: cypress essential oil has relaxing properties and can be massaged onto the lower abdomen.","Other recommendations: biofeedback can improve symptoms."],"conditions":["Urinary incontinence"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_uro_interstitial_cystitis","chapter":"urology","title":"Interstitial cystitis — the Holistic Rx","body":["Interstitial cystitis is a chronic inflammation of the bladder marked by a persistent need to urinate frequently and pelvic pain; it can mimic a UTI but lacks bacterial infection. It causes chronic discomfort or pain in the bladder or pelvic area, usually in flares, with urgent need to urinate, pain with urination, frequent urination, pelvic pain, and pain with intercourse; symptoms can worsen during menstruation. About half of women spontaneously improve without treatment, and conventional medicine has limited options. Assessment includes a detailed history and bladder diary, pelvic exam, urinalysis, cystoscopy, possible biopsy, urine cytology to rule out cancer, and possibly a potassium sensitivity test. It's usually a diagnosis of exclusion.","Supplements: probiotics can help reduce inflammation. Calcium glycerophosphate (two to three tabs, or 1/4 tsp, twice daily or before high-acid meals). Glucosamine and chondroitin (dose varies) to reduce pain. Quercetin (1,000–3,000 mg daily) and grapeseed extract (10 mg daily). Others: L-arginine (500 mg three times daily), marshmallow root, aloe vera.","Homeopathy: Cantharis is for burning pains in the lower abdomen, a nonstop urge to urinate, and inability to empty the bladder. Nux vomica helps painful, frequent urges with little result. Apis is for sharp, stinging lower-abdominal pain with scanty, hot, occasionally bloody urine, worse in heat and better with cold, with urinary frequency. Belladonna is for burning along the urethra that persists after urination, with continuing urges. Berberis is for slimy, fine-mucus urine with radiating pain worse at rest and while urinating. Causticum is for frequent urge to urinate with a change in vaginal discharge, worse with coughing and sneezing. Dulcamara is for an attack after getting cold, or after exertion in the fall. Sarsaparilla helps pain that comes on as urination stops, with urgency and pressure as urine passes, and thick, milky-looking urine. Staphysagria is for an attack after sex or catheterization, with constant trickling and a constant burning sensation unrelated to urination. Clematis helps an intermittent or slow urine stream. Camphora is for pain worse at the start of urination with bladder-base spasm, worse with cold, and no urine passed despite urgent straining. Arsenicum helps burning lower-abdominal pain in anxious, restless, cold patients.","Acupressure: ST40, SP9, B28, B32, LV2, LV3, CV2, and CV3; others include CV4, CV6, and GV4.","Aromatherapy: essential oils that benefit urological conditions include lavender, sandalwood, peppermint, clary sage, and thyme (antispasmodic and antiseptic), along with cypress, geranium, and juniper."],"conditions":["Interstitial cystitis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_inflammation_basics","chapter":"foundations","title":"Understanding the silent fire within — inflammation as the root of chronic illness","body":["Inflammation literally means \"fire inside,\" and it is the immune system's built-in response to infection, injury, or threat. Seemingly unrelated conditions — mood disorders, thyroid disease, autoimmune disorders, digestive issues, skin disorders, chronic pain, and fatigue — share one common root: chronic inflammation. Rather than treating each symptom separately, addressing this root cause is the foundation of the holistic, functional, and integrative approach.","There are two forms of inflammation. Acute inflammation (good inflammation) lasts from seconds to a few days: the innate immune system sends white blood cells and hormone-like substances to the site of injury or infection, producing redness, heat, swelling, and pain — a healthy sign the body's defenses are working. Chronic inflammation (bad, or systemic, inflammation) is a hidden, smoldering, often painless fire created by the adaptive immune system as it tries to fight off modern life's constant exposures: unhealthy food, stress, toxins, allergens, an overgrowth of bad \"bugs,\" and low-grade infections. These triggers drive the release of inflammatory cytokines; with constant exposure, cytokine responses go into overdrive and become self-perpetuating, damaging vascular tissue, organs, and every body tissue — the common denominator of most chronic diseases and hard-to-explain symptoms.","Common symptoms of chronic inflammation include body aches and pains, fever, congestion, frequent infections, stiffness, dry eyes, digestive symptoms, shortness of breath, fatigue, allergies, mood disorders, light-headedness and brain fog, memory and concentration issues, weight gain or inability to lose weight, and headaches. Depending on which tissue is chronically inflamed, specific disease can result: inflamed skin or lungs raise the risk of acne, eczema, asthma, or COPD; inflamed brain/nerves or joints are linked to ADHD, depression/mood disorders, and arthritis; inflamed arteries can lead to heart attack and stroke; and inflammation interferes with mitochondrial fat-burning, contributing to obesity, and can ultimately drive autoimmunity and cancer.","Chronic disease is on the rise, and it tracks directly with rising inflammation. Standard medicine tends to manage such conditions with medications that address symptoms rather than identifying and eliminating the underlying inflammation, holding that they can be managed but rarely cured. The holistic approach instead targets chronic inflammation itself as the underlying cause, aiming to put out the fire rather than band-aid the blaze — addressing root causes can help resolve not just one but multiple chronic conditions and rebalance hormones at the same time."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Chronic pain","Fibromyalgia","Rheumatoid arthritis","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Fatigue and low energy","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Depression","Anxiety","Psoriasis","Eczema"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_inflammation_drivers","chapter":"foundations","title":"Why chronic disease and inflammation are on the rise","body":["Several modern-life factors drive up inflammation and, with it, chronic disease. Epigenetics — the study of how genes are expressed without changes to DNA structure itself — means genes \"load the gun,\" but our diseases, medications, and internal/external environment pull the trigger: our microbiome, nutrition, toxin exposure, stressors, exercise, sleep, optimism, and spiritual health can all turn genes on or off, influencing pro-inflammatory cytokine release and the likelihood of developing chronic conditions — changes that can even be passed on to offspring.","Antibiotic exposure harms the gut microbiome, triggering a process that can increase inflammation; the risks of an antibiotic course should always be weighed against its benefits. Altered nutrition also plays a major role: more than 80 percent of food in North America contains GMOs, and a 2014 MIT study correlated the sharp rise in chronic conditions with increased glyphosate use (an herbicide common in GMO seed) impairing detoxification. When food is genetically modified or full of chemicals, artificial flavors, and colorings, the immune system treats it as a foreign invader rather than nourishment, and inflammation is triggered.","The hygiene hypothesis holds that germs actually help build the immune system; an overly clean, oversheltered environment limits germ exposure and restricts immune development, so that without significant germs to respond to, the immune system overreacts to less important triggers, producing inflammation. Environmental toxins damage the body's own detoxification mechanisms, making it harder to clear them; regular accumulated toxin exposure shifts the immune system, raises inflammation, and makes the body more prone to allergies and chronic disease. Finally, methylation — a key biochemical/cellular process central to detoxification — when impaired, causes trouble with detoxification, immune function, mood balance, DNA repair and maintenance, energy production, and inflammation control."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Fatigue and low energy","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Atherosclerosis","Depression","Anxiety"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_inflammation_puzzle","chapter":"foundations","title":"The pieces of the inflammation puzzle — digestion, detoxification, and the Four Big S's","body":["Inflammation is a complex, multifactorial process; a deficit in any one area below can cause or worsen it. Each person's situation is unique — you may eat well but carry a heavy relationship burden, or exercise little but have a strong social circle — and improving physical, mental/emotional, social, and spiritual health together is critical to healing and preventing chronic conditions.","Digestive health and detoxification: the gut is the largest gate between the outside world and our internal environment, and about 70 to 80 percent of the immune system lives in the digestive tract. When immune cells there are activated, they release inflammatory molecules that travel throughout the body, driving inflammation in joints, skin, blood vessels, and brain. Proper nutrition — an anti-inflammatory diet — keeps digestion happy and regulates glucose and insulin; anything that causes insulin resistance also causes inflammation, and vice versa, and unresolved insulin resistance can accelerate aging and contribute to stroke, dementia, heart disease, and cancer. We live in a world full of largely invisible toxins that accumulate with regular exposure and burden the liver; when the liver is overwhelmed it stores rather than clears toxins, further increasing inflammation.","The Four Big S's — Stress: stress is behind more than 80 percent of complaints presented to a doctor, taxes the immune system, and chronically raises cortisol, driving inflammation and its accompanying diseases; managing stress is essential to healing.","Sleep: during sleep the body uses less energy for daily function, freeing energy to clear toxins, make hormones, and fight infection; insufficient rest raises the risk of toxin buildup, inflammation, and hormone imbalance, so improving sleep is a powerful lever for health.","Social health: loneliness and negative relationships raise cortisol and suppress immune function, worsening inflammation, while positive relationships that leave you feeling loved release hormones that strengthen immunity and support healing.","Spirituality: negative spiritual energy is linked to more stress, more depression, and a weakened immune system, all increasing inflammation, while healing the soul and cultivating positivity support prevention and recovery from chronic conditions."],"conditions":["Chronic pain","Fibromyalgia","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Fatigue and low energy","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Depression","Anxiety"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_gut_overview","chapter":"foundations","title":"The gut as the gateway to health — digestion and the microbiome","body":["Chronic disease healing begins in the digestive system. The gut and digestive tract make up the largest gate between the outside world and our internal environment, and the integrity of that intestinal barrier is a core piece of the inflammation puzzle. The digestive system does more than process food: it converts and absorbs nutrients and regulates metabolism; it is integral to immunity, housing about 70 to 80 percent of the immune system, keeping toxins and pathogens out and repairing damage they cause; it is a key organ of detoxification alongside the liver, kidneys, skin, and lungs; and it is the body's neurotransmitter factory — roughly three-fourths of the body's neurotransmitters, including about 90 percent of its serotonin and 50 percent of its dopamine, are found in the gut.","Humans are superorganisms: our own cells are outnumbered by about one hundred trillion microbes, with roughly 3.5 to 4.5 pounds of bacteria in the digestive system alone. This community of good bugs (mutualists), bad bugs (pathogens), and neutral bugs (commensals) — bacteria, viruses, protozoa, helminths, and microfungi — directly affects epigenetics and immune function. Most experts consider a ratio of about 85 percent good/neutral to 15 percent bad ideal; lower bacterial richness is linked to higher weight and fat, higher cholesterol and triglycerides, insulin resistance, more pronounced inflammatory markers, and a greater likelihood of leaky gut. A study of an Amazonian indigenous tribe free of chronic illness found the most diverse microbiome ever documented in humans, underscoring how central diversity is to health.","The microbiome is seeded starting in utero via the placenta and shaped decisively at birth: babies born vaginally are colonized with lactobacillus and other beneficial bacteria similar to the mother's vaginal flora, while babies born by C-section tend to acquire more staphylococcus-type bacteria similar to skin flora, which is associated with a higher chance of developing asthma, allergic rhinitis, dermatitis, and autoimmune disease. Formula feeding versus breastfeeding likewise shapes the microbiome differently, influencing gene transcription and creating lasting metabolic effects. The microbiome continues to grow and diversify with daily exposures throughout life.","A healthy microbiome performs several essential jobs: digestion (breaking down nutrients, balancing intestinal pH, synthesizing hormones and vitamins, regulating peristalsis); immunity (the gut-associated lymphatic tissue, or GALT, is the first line of defense, training T cells, fighting infection, and even showing antitumor effects); energy extraction from otherwise undigested food; metabolism regulation (producing vitamins and folic acid, aiding mineral absorption, manufacturing fatty acids, and helping regulate weight); gene expression (influencing whether genes are told to burn or store fat); and mood and behavior, since the gut holds the same neurotransmitters as the brain and communicates with it via the vagus nerve — an unhappy microbiome can trigger cytokine release that raises stress, inflammation, and mood disturbance."],"conditions":["Irritable bowel syndrome","Bloating and gas","Dyspepsia","Autoimmune conditions","Fatigue and low energy"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_leaky_gut_causes","chapter":"foundations","title":"Leaky gut and dysbiosis — how the gut barrier breaks down, and what breaks it","body":["Normally the cells lining the intestine sit tightly together, forming a barrier permeable enough to let properly digested fats, proteins, and starches through the tight junctions and into the bloodstream, while keeping out larger, damaging particles. When an imbalanced microbiome (dysbiosis) persists, pathogenic microbes or improperly digested proteins can trigger an inflammatory response from immune cells; that inflammation damages the gut wall's epithelial cells, and the tight junctions become increasingly leaky — increased intestinal permeability, or leaky gut syndrome. Once leaky, the barrier lets gluten, undigested food particles, viruses, yeast, bad bacteria, and toxins cross into the bloodstream, further destabilizing the microbiome in a self-reinforcing cycle. In 2000, Dr. Alessio Fasano at the University of Maryland identified zonulin, the substance that directly controls these tight junctions; its release is triggered by exposure to bacteria and to gluten, and modern diets expose us to more zonulin than ever before.","If the immune system stays switched \"on\" for months, it becomes hyper-reactive to stimuli it would previously have ignored. This chronic state can lead to translocation of bacteria and bacterial products like lipopolysaccharides (LPS, an endotoxin) into the bloodstream, triggering cytokine release and inflammation throughout the body and central nervous system — contributing to impaired glucose metabolism, insulin resistance, obesity, metabolic syndrome, type 2 diabetes, food sensitivities, nutrient deficiencies (B12, magnesium, iron), neurotransmitter deficiencies, allergies, chronic intestinal disease such as inflammatory bowel disease, autoimmunity, and even carcinogenesis. The process can begin in utero if the mother herself has an infection or chronic inflammation.","Several everyday exposures drive dysbiosis and leaky gut. Nutrition: processed, sugar-heavy, alcohol-containing diets low in fiber and nutrients — and foods a person has become sensitive to — throw off the bacterial balance. Chronic stress: elevated stress hormones lower probiotic diversity, allow yeast overgrowth, alter gut permeability, and influence epigenetics — a relationship that also runs in reverse, as an unhealthy microbiome itself generates more stress and inflammation. Environmental contaminants: daily exposure to household and environmental chemicals, oversanitizing, too much time indoors, and pesticide-laden, genetically modified food all strain the microbiome (the United States uses roughly 24 percent of the world's pesticides with under 2 percent of its population). Medications: NSAIDs, birth control pills, steroids, chemotherapy drugs, sleep medications, acid blockers/antacids, and opiates can all disturb the microbiome, but antibiotics deserve special mention — while sometimes lifesaving, they are frequently overused, kill beneficial bacteria along with pathogens, damage the intestinal villi, and have been linked to rising rates of obesity, type 1 diabetes, inflammatory bowel disease, allergies, and asthma; overuse in childhood can even kill off good bacteria permanently. Overly strenuous exercise raises pro-inflammatory cytokines and can harm the intestinal lining, though probiotic use may offer some protection. Genetics plays a role in autoimmune susceptibility, but fewer than 10 percent of people carrying autoimmune-linked genes actually develop the disease — epigenetics and environment matter. Unresolved bacterial, viral, fungal, or parasitic infections also disturb the microbiome.","Because the gut barrier touches nearly every system, research links leaky gut to a strikingly wide range of conditions: autoimmune disease (Hashimoto's, celiac disease, non-celiac gluten sensitivity, lupus, multiple sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis); cardiovascular problems (hypertension, hyperlipidemia, cardiovascular disease); skin inflammation (eczema, psoriasis, rosacea, dermatitis, acne); ENT issues (allergies, sinus problems); endocrine disease (metabolic syndrome, type 1 and type 2 diabetes); gastrointestinal disease (Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, gas, bloating, digestive pain, irritable bowel syndrome, ulcers, fatty liver disease, pancreatic disease); gynecological issues (yeast overgrowth, PCOS, and estrogen-dominance symptoms such as painful breasts, heavy periods, infertility, and higher risk of hormone-sensitive cancers); neurological disease (ALS, Alzheimer's, migraines, Parkinsonism); cancer and anemia; psychological disturbances (alcoholism, anxiety, depression, ADHD, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, sleep disturbance); asthma; rheumatologic disease (arthritis, chronic fatigue syndrome, fibromyalgia, restless leg syndrome); pediatric autism; interstitial cystitis; and general problems like malnutrition, nutrient deficiencies, infections, food sensitivities, and even acute inflammation like sepsis. Leaky gut typically starts as general inflammation and, left unaddressed, advances toward nutrient malabsorption and then food and chemical sensitivities — which is why healing the intestinal lining is the foundation of this whole approach."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Irritable bowel syndrome","Autoimmune conditions","Eczema","Psoriasis","Food intolerance","Bloating and gas","Fatigue and low energy","Chronic fatigue syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_food_triggers","chapter":"foundations","title":"Foods that fuel gut inflammation — gluten, sugar, and the frankenfoods","body":["Food is one of the most direct levers on gut and microbiome health, and certain foods are common triggers of leaky gut and food sensitivities. When the gut is leaky, incompletely digested food particles cross the barrier and the immune system reads their \"name tags\": if it labels a particle an invader, it makes antibodies against that food. A true food allergy — mediated by IgE antibodies — produces an immediate reaction such as hives, swelling, or difficulty breathing. A food sensitivity or intolerance, mediated by IgG antibodies, tends to be less dramatic but still drives inflammation and can be harder to connect to a specific food because reactions are often delayed and can appear outside the digestive tract (heart palpitations, postnasal drip, cough, congestion, cramping, fatigue, brain fog). An allergist or functional/integrative physician can test for both IgE and IgG antibodies.","Gluten, a protein found in wheat, barley, rye, kamut, and spelt, is estimated to cause non-celiac sensitivity in about twenty-one million Americans, contributing to inflammation linked with depression, schizophrenia, autism, dementia, obesity, heart disease, and even cancer. Decades of hybridizing wheat for higher yield have produced a larger, harder-to-digest gluten molecule with more phytic acid and amylopectin but fewer nutrients; when it reaches the small intestine only partly broken down, it sparks immune activation and triggers zonulin release, loosening tight junctions and driving leaky gut. Gluten also spikes blood sugar more than table sugar and binds the same opioid receptors as morphine and heroin, which is why cravings and a withdrawal-like effect follow when it's removed. Corn, dairy, millet, oats, rice, and yeast can cross-react with the immune system in a gluten-like way and increase inflammation through the same leaky-gut pathway.","Sugar appears in about 74 percent of packaged foods and stimulates the brain's reward centers, producing withdrawal symptoms when stopped abruptly. A major Harvard School of Public Health study published in JAMA found that people getting 25 percent or more of daily calories from sugar were twice as likely to die of heart disease as those under 10 percent. Sugar feeds pathogenic bacteria and yeast, drives blood sugar swings, lowers HDL and raises LDL cholesterol, promotes belly fat, increases appetite and cravings, contributes to fatty liver, and raises oxidative stress — all within minutes of eating it and lasting for hours. Artificial sweeteners are no safer: they're linked to gut irritation, insulin resistance, and inflammation, cause hunger via leptin resistance, gas, bloating, and headaches, and sucralose (Splenda) specifically has been shown at Duke University Medical Center to reduce beneficial gut bacteria and raise fecal pH, impairing nutrient absorption; artificial sweetener users show higher rates of weight gain, waist circumference, type 2 diabetes (a 67 percent increase), and metabolic syndrome.","Alcohol provokes leaky gut, increases intestinal permeability, inflames the liver, impairs impulse control (leading to more mindless eating), spikes blood sugar like any other sugar, and can trigger histamine release. Dairy loses much of its digestibility after age five, when the body stops producing lactose-digesting enzymes; commercial milk's pasteurization and homogenization destroy probiotics, enzymes, and nutrients while altering casein into a gluten-like molecule that can drive leaky gut, and A1 beta-casein releases an opioid (beta-casomorphin-7) that has been linked to autism and schizophrenia, along with natural cow estrogens tied to hormone-sensitive cancer risk and early puberty. Processed \"frankenfoods\" bring hydrogenated oils that damage gut flora and genetically modified ingredients carrying glyphosate residue (an active ingredient in Roundup), which disrupts beneficial bacteria, damages the intestinal wall, and may impair the liver's ability to activate vitamin D3.","To get a clearer picture of what's driving symptoms rather than guessing, useful tests include a comprehensive stool test (organism balance, bacterial diversity, presence of yeast), IgG/IgE panels for food sensitivities and allergies, a lactose or hydrogen/methane breath test for SIBO, and an organic acids test to flag vitamin and mineral deficiencies alongside leaky gut."],"conditions":["Bloating and gas","GERD","Gastritis","Irritable bowel syndrome","Food intolerance","Obesity","Dyspepsia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_gutrx_remove_replenish","chapter":"foundations","title":"The Gut Rx, steps 1–2 — remove the triggers, replenish digestion","body":["The Gut Rx is a structured, time-limited approach: three to four weeks of elimination, followed by careful food reintroduction. Step one is Remove — take out all the likely offending triggers at once, since removing everything together tends to bring relief faster and is less discouraging than removing foods one at a time. Remove all grains (amaranth, barley, brown rice, buckwheat, bulgur, corn, farro/emmer, grano, kamut, millet, oats, oatmeal, popcorn, muesli, rolled oats, quinoa, rye, sorghum, spelt, teff, triticale, wheat, wild rice — quinoa and corn aren't technically grains but carry similar proteins), dairy, sugar, alcohol, starchy vegetables, and nutrient-depleted or processed foods (poor-quality oils, hydrogenated fats, trans fats, GMOs). If you can't remove everything at once, start with gluten and dairy. Also address infections and overgrowth that raise intestinal inflammation — viruses, parasites, yeast, and small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) — using berberine (active against Candida albicans and Staphylococcus aureus), wormwood, caprylic acid, grapefruit seed extract (antimicrobial/antifungal), garlic (antibacterial, antifungal, antiviral, antiparasitic), oregano oil, and peppermint oil. If symptoms haven't improved after a couple of weeks, consider also eliminating legumes/beans (some tolerate lentils and navy beans), soy, eggs, nightshades, and nuts, plus any food you suspect you're sensitive to.","Step two is Replenish — restoring the nutrients and digestive capacity that diet, medication, disease, or aging may have depleted. Build every meal around non-starchy vegetables (fiber, vitamins, antioxidants, minerals), quality protein (grass-fed beef, pastured chicken, pasture-raised eggs, wild-caught seafood, hemp seeds), healthy fat (coconut oil, avocado, olive oil, nuts and seeds if tolerated), and filtered water. Bone broth and collagen — at least one cup a day, ideally slow-cooked at least eight hours — supply L-glutamine, glucosamine, chondroitin, proline, and glycine along with bioavailable calcium, magnesium, phosphorus, silicon, and sulfur that help repair, seal, and soothe the gut lining. Anti-inflammatory herbs like black seed, turmeric, rosemary, and ginger, plus fermented foods once tolerated, round out the healing plate.","Digestion itself needs attention: stomach pH has to be right for proper breakdown of food. Too much acid causes reflux; too little causes bloating, poor breakdown of food and enzyme activation, and yeast or bacterial overgrowth — low stomach acid is commonly caused by alcohol, antibiotics, caffeine, hypothyroidism, an imbalanced microbiome, nicotine, and especially chronic stress. A simple baking soda test can check acid level: first thing in the morning, before eating or drinking anything, mix 1/4 teaspoon of baking soda in 4–6 oz of cold water, drink it, and time how long it takes to belch, up to five minutes — belching after three minutes suggests low acid. Betaine hydrochloric acid supplements can help while the gut heals: start with one capsule at the beginning of each meal, increase by one capsule per meal until you notice a warm feeling in the stomach, then drop back to the dose just below that; stay on it one to two months, then reassess. Starting the day with lemon water, drinking half a cup of water with half a teaspoon of raw apple cider vinegar with meals, and using digestive bitters can also boost stomach acid naturally. If symptoms haven't improved within four weeks, add a plant-based digestive enzyme blend containing protease, amylase, and lipase to help break down macronutrients and absorb nutrients."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Bloating and gas","Constipation","Diarrhea","Dyspepsia","GERD","Gastritis","Irritable bowel syndrome","Food intolerance","Candidiasis","Parasites, intestinal"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_gutrx_repopulate_repair","chapter":"foundations","title":"The Gut Rx, steps 3–4 — repopulate with probiotics and prebiotics, repair the lining","body":["Step three is Repopulate — restoring the balance of good bacteria with probiotics and prebiotic, fiber-rich foods. A good probiotic should provide strain diversity — at least ten or more strains, since different species have different strengths, including lactobacilli (especially plantarum and brevis), bifidobacteria (longum and lactis), and soil-based/endospore organisms like Bacillus subtilis and Bacillus coagulans, which also support nutrient absorption, reduce inflammation, and lower harmful pathogens like candida, fungi, and parasites. Choose condition-appropriate strains where possible: L. rhamnosus, L. gasseri, and B. lactis for weight loss; Bacillus infantus, B. animalis, B. lactis, and L. plantarum for IBS; L. plantarum 299v for IBD; L. rhamnosus, L. reuteri, L. plantarum, and L. crispatus for vaginal/urinary health; L. acidophilus, L. rhamnosus, B. lactis, and B. longus for elevated cholesterol and blood sugar; add L. brevis if taking an antibiotic. Avoid supplements containing soy, dairy, gluten, grains, or artificial ingredients. Bacteriophages, Saccharomyces boulardii, and mushroom mycelia are useful adjuncts against pathogenic yeast such as Candida albicans; yeast-based probiotics may help SIBO, while spore-based strains can boost lactobacillus and gut diversity.","On strength, look for at least eight billion bacteria cells per gram; start around one billion organisms daily and build gradually to fifteen to one hundred billion CFU daily depending on tolerance and how you feel. Introducing probiotics can trigger a die-off reaction as pathogenic bacteria and viruses are destroyed and release toxins — fatigue, digestive upset, low-grade nausea, mild headache, and irritability are common and usually last a few days to a few weeks. Manage it by starting low and increasing gradually, holding the dose steady through a reaction until it clears, and spacing doses out if needed; Epsom salt, sea salt, seaweed, and baking soda baths (rotated day to day, half a cup for thirty minutes) can speed recovery. Plan on staying at your effective dose (often around twenty billion organisms daily) for about six months to reestablish normal flora before tapering; probiotic-rich foods remain important even once supplement dosing is reduced. If prescribed an antibiotic, take a probiotic with 50–100 billion CFU between doses and continue it for three times the length of the antibiotic course to reduce relapse risk.","Prebiotics — \"food\" for probiotics — nourish good bacteria and reduce disease-causing bacteria such as clostridia, klebsiella, and enterobacter. Getting them from whole vegetables and fruit (asparagus, carrots, garlic, leeks, raw onions, radishes, tomatoes, and leafy greens) is preferable to supplement form, which can cause significant gas and bloating.","Step four is Repair — once offending foods are removed and digestion and bacterial balance are supported, the gut wall itself needs healing. Continue bone broth (at least one cup daily) and prioritize foods rich in zinc (lean beef, poultry, cashews, almonds), vitamin A (carrots, dark leafy greens, winter squash, apricots, liver), vitamin C (bell peppers, kiwi, broccoli, citrus), vitamin D (canned tuna and sardines, liver, egg yolks, cod liver oil), omega-3, vitamin E, and selenium. Helpful repair supplements include L-glutamine (5 g twice daily with meals — also found in bone broth, animal protein, and raw spinach, red cabbage, and parsley) to fuel intestinal cells and support the IgA immune response tied to food sensitivities; omega-3 (2–4 g/day with meals); digestive enzymes with protease, amylase, lipase, lactase, and DPP-IV; zinc carnosine (30 mg/day); turmeric; slippery elm (200 mg/day); aloe vera (100 mg/day); licorice root/DGL (500 mg/day, especially for leaky gut worsened by emotional stress); frankincense essential oil; quercetin (500 mg three times daily with meals); colostrum powder; chamomile; MSM; boswellia powder; marshmallow root (100 mg/day); and butyric acid/butyrate.","For children: probiotics are especially worth considering after a C-section birth or a history of yeast, antibiotic, or steroid exposure during pregnancy, labor, or nursing, or with a family history of autoimmunity or metabolic syndrome — look for Lactobacillus acidophilus and Bifidobacterium infantis. Age-based dosing: up to twelve months, 1–2 billion CFU/day; one to two years, 2–4 billion/day; two to four years, 4–8 billion/day; four to ten years, 8–12 billion/day; twelve to sixteen years, 12–15 billion/day. Digestive enzymes: a pinch of powder per meal under age five, ½ teaspoon per meal over five. Glutamine powder: 1.5 g twice daily for children over four."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Bloating and gas","Irritable bowel syndrome","Candidiasis","Parasites, intestinal","Diarrhea","Constipation","Food intolerance"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_reintro_diets","chapter":"foundations","title":"Rebalancing, reintroducing foods, and choosing a structured gut-healing diet","body":["Step five is Rebalance: build in habits that add microbial diversity and lower stress, including gardening, walking barefoot outdoors (also helps sleep, energy, and inflammation), shopping at local farmers markets, swimming in the ocean or fresh water (helpful for skin inflammation, rheumatoid arthritis, and psoriasis), and getting a pet, which diversifies the microbes in the home.","Once you've followed the healing diet for a minimum of three to four weeks and symptoms have improved — often signaled by being ready to start weaning off medications under a doctor's supervision — you can begin reintroducing foods one at a time. Eat the food you missed most in its purest form for a couple of days, then stop it on day three and keep observing, since sensitivities can take up to seventy-two hours to appear; a food reintroduction log helps track this. Watch for digestive symptoms, headaches, joint pain, abdominal pain, fatigue, mood swings, congestion, or rashes — any of these signals a sensitivity to that food, so remove it again until the reaction clears (usually a day or two, sometimes longer) before trying the next food, roughly one new food every four days, until you know exactly which foods you tolerate.","Relapses happen — from eating a trigger food, or from other deficits like stress, infections, toxins, or insulin resistance. Antibiotic courses are a common trigger; pairing them with a 50–100 billion CFU probiotic between doses (continued for three times the antibiotic's length) helps prevent it. If you relapse: recommit to the full protocol, trim back suspicious foods, take two capsules of digestive enzymes with food if needed, maintain a maintenance dose of probiotics, and monitor symptoms for one to two weeks — if they aren't improving, restart the Gut Rx from the beginning or move to a stricter, more structured approach.","For more serious or persistent cases, several structured gut-healing diets go further than this basic protocol: the Gut and Psychology Syndrome (GAPS) diet offers a staged approach that accounts for individual sensitivities and glucose balance; the Specific Carbohydrate Diet, FODMAP, Autoimmune Protocol, and Paleo are other established frameworks. Everyone is different, so the right structured diet is whichever one works for your body and situation."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Food intolerance","Irritable bowel syndrome","Bloating and gas"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest_insulin_resistance","chapter":"foundations","title":"Insulin resistance — the diet-inflammation connection","body":["Insulin is a storage hormone made by the pancreas that acts as the \"key\" letting glucose enter cells for fuel and controls blood sugar levels and metabolism. Chronic inflammatory signaling gradually wears down that key: cells stop responding properly to the insulin that's always present, glucose can no longer enter efficiently, and the pancreas compensates by producing more insulin. Over time the beta cells can't keep up with demand, leaving excess glucose in the bloodstream. Prolonged insulin resistance drives fat storage, obesity, metabolic syndrome, prediabetes, diabetes, and premature aging — and worsens inflammation in a mutually reinforcing cycle, so managing insulin resistance is critical to balancing hormones and preventing chronic disease.","Diet is the biggest driver: refined sugars and flours are the largest trigger of insulin spikes, artificial sweeteners are linked to weight gain and diabetes, low fiber intake raises visceral fat, and an excess of omega-6 relative to omega-3 fatty acids contributes to systemic inflammation that worsens insulin resistance. Chronic stress raises cortisol, which in turn raises insulin levels and worsens resistance. Dysbiosis and leaky gut syndrome contribute directly — bad bacteria overgrowth increases inflammation and worsens insulin resistance, driving obesity and belly fat. Nutrient deficiencies matter too: vitamin D deficiency impairs glucose tolerance through its effects on insulin secretion and sensitivity (the lower the vitamin D, the higher the insulin resistance), and magnesium deficiency has also been linked to developing insulin resistance. Certain medications, including steroids and hydrochlorothiazide (a first-line blood pressure medication), can cause hyperglycemia and worsen insulin resistance — it's worth reviewing medications with your doctor and asking about alternatives if this is a concern."],"conditions":["Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Obesity","Fatigue and low energy","Leaky gut syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_healing_plate","chapter":"foundations","title":"Building the healing plate — macros, fats, and what to avoid","body":["Once the trigger foods are out, build every meal for success: half the plate is vegetables, about 4-6 oz. (roughly the size of a palm) is clean protein, and one to two teaspoons of healthy oil, a handful of nuts or seeds, or avocado supplies the fat. Eat the vegetables and protein first, before any starch, so the body gets the nutrient-dense foods it needs rather than filling up on refined carbohydrate. Aim for about 80-120 g of clean protein a day, spread across meals and snacks, with quality mattering more than quantity: pastured poultry and eggs, small wild-caught cold-water fish (salmon, mackerel, sardines, herring, anchovies), and grass-fed, grass-finished meat and dairy, which carry a better omega-6-to-omega-3 ratio and more conjugated linoleic acid than grain-fed versions.","Fat is not the enemy — it satiates by turning on leptin and is essential for cellular function. Favor saturated fat from coconut oil, butter, and ghee; monounsaturated fat from olive oil, avocado, and nuts; and omega-3-rich polyunsaturated fat from fish and flaxseed oil, while minimizing omega-6-heavy refined vegetable oils (corn, soybean, canola, safflower, sunflower), which are largely partially hydrogenated and skew the omega-6-to-omega-3 ratio toward inflammation; the ideal ratio is 4:1 to 1:1. Coconut oil and ghee tolerate high heat for cooking; olive, walnut, macadamia, sesame, and avocado oils, along with butter, are best kept to low or no heat to avoid oxidizing. Fiber target is about 25-30 g a day from vegetables, low-glycemic fruit, nuts, and seeds, which suppresses appetite, blunts the post-meal blood sugar and insulin rise, and feeds the healthy bacteria.","Foods and additives to avoid or limit while healing: all forms of gluten (wheat, barley, rye, durum, kamut, semolina, spelt, oats unless certified gluten-free) and hidden gluten in sauces and \"natural flavors\"; added sugar under any of its many names (agave, dextrose, high-fructose corn syrup, maltodextrin, and others); processed and fast food; soy unless fermented (tempeh); corn, which cross-reacts with gluten and is heavily genetically modified; alcohol, which behaves like sugar and provokes leaky gut; and industrial food additives — MSG, artificial sweeteners, soy protein isolate, sodium/calcium caseinate, carrageenan (linked to leaky gut and inflammation), sulfites, nitrates/nitrites in processed deli meat, and emulsifiers like polysorbate 80, several of which have been linked to autoimmunity. Glyphosate, the world's most widely used herbicide, disrupts the microbiome, impairs detoxification, depletes vitamin D, and has been linked to a fourfold increase in celiac disease and other autoimmune conditions in a 2013 MIT review of 270 studies.","Grains, legumes, nightshades, and caffeine are common secondary triggers worth eliminating for four weeks if symptoms persist. Grains contain phytates and lectins that impair mineral absorption and, in oats, corn, and quinoa specifically, can provoke leaky gut and dysbiosis; sprouting deactivates much of the phytic acid if grains are reintroduced later. Legumes (black beans, garbanzo beans, lentils, lima beans, soybeans, white beans) contain lectins, phytates, and saponins that can irritate the small intestine; presoaking, sprouting, or fermenting improves tolerance. Nightshades (tomatoes, white potatoes, bell peppers, eggplant, cayenne, paprika) contain solanine, chaconine, and lectins that can worsen leaky gut in sensitive people. Caffeine stimulates stress neurotransmitters and can worsen insomnia and anxiety; if weaning off, cut the dose in half daily starting on a weekend, hydrate well, and consider 1,000 mg of vitamin C to ease withdrawal headaches. Other individual sensitivities to watch for include histamine, sulfates, salicylates, and oxalates."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Irritable bowel syndrome","Food intolerance","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Bloating and gas","Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_key_supplements","chapter":"foundations","title":"Four key immune and gut-support supplements — vitamin D, K2, magnesium, and fish oil","body":["Vitamin D is really a prehormone, converted by the liver and kidneys into its active form, that regulates hundreds of disease-preventing proteins and enzymes, helps control serotonin and other neurotransmitters, supports calcium/magnesium/phosphorus absorption for bone, and is strongly linked — when deficient — to autoimmune disease. Lack of sun exposure and sunscreen use are the most common causes of deficiency. Check a 25-OH vitamin D lab value and aim for a range of about 50-80 ng/ml; once optimized, a maintenance dose of roughly twenty times body weight in pounds per day, or 2,000-4,000 IU/day, is typical — always as D3, not D2, taken with a fat-containing meal. Levels above 150 ng/ml can cause dangerous hypercalcemia, kidney damage, or psychosis, so this should be physician-supervised, and caution is needed with hyperparathyroidism, lymphoma, granulomatous disease (sarcoidosis, TB), kidney stones, kidney disease, or liver disease. Fermented cod liver oil (about 1 tsp) is an alternative source of D3 plus A, DHA, K, and E for those who don't tolerate straight vitamin D.","Vitamin K works alongside vitamin D. K1, found in leafy greens, supports blood clotting; K2 (including the MK-7 form), found in fermented foods and made by gut bacteria, directs calcium into bone and out of arteries and supports cardiovascular protection. Increasing vitamin D intake can create a relative K, A, and E insufficiency, so pair vitamin D or calcium supplementation with 100-250 mcg of K2 daily, or about 50 mcg for every 5,000 IU of D3 — don't combine K1 with warfarin.","About 75-80% of the population is thought to be magnesium deficient, largely from an acidic processed diet and chronic fluoride exposure. Magnesium is a cofactor for over three hundred enzymes and for ATP, DNA, and RNA synthesis, muscle contraction, and detoxification; deficiency shows up as fatigue, anxiety, muscle cramps, insomnia, numbness/tingling, palpitations, carbohydrate cravings, and poor blood sugar regulation, and is linked to heart disease. A typical dose is 350-400 mg of elemental magnesium daily — glycinate, chelate, or malate forms absorb best, while citrate is particularly useful for constipation and sleep (avoid magnesium oxide if constipated, and avoid supplementing at all with poor kidney function). Take magnesium and calcium at least two hours apart from a multivitamin/mineral.","Fish oil supplies the essential omega-3 fats EPA and DHA, needed for cell membranes and largely anti-inflammatory (unlike omega-6, which promotes pro-inflammatory prostaglandins). Benefits span immune function, blood chemistry, brain and heart health, insulin sensitivity, mood, skin/nail/hair strength, and lowering inflammation broadly; 60% of brain weight is fat and a quarter of that is DHA. General immune support runs 1,000-2,000 mg/day; rheumatoid or other inflammatory arthritis often benefits from 3,000 mg/day of combined EPA/DHA. Vegetarians can substitute 1,000-3,000 mg/day of flaxseed oil, though it's less potent. Choose a product tested clear of heavy metals, PCBs, and pesticides. Side effects (belching, heartburn, loose stools, easy bruising) are mostly seen at 3 g/day or higher, and anyone on blood thinners should have their physician monitor INR when starting."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Fatigue and low energy","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Depression","Anxiety","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Leaky gut syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_medication_depletions","chapter":"foundations","title":"Medications that deplete nutrients — a quick-reference list","body":["Many commonly prescribed medications quietly drain nutrients that the gut and immune system depend on, which compounds the deficiencies already caused by processed food, stress, and toxin exposure — worth knowing before assuming fatigue or slow healing is unrelated to a current prescription. Always check with a prescriber before adding or changing a supplement around a medication.","Proton pump inhibitors and H2 blockers deplete beneficial flora, calcium, digestive enzymes, folic acid, B12, vitamins C/D/E/K, biotin, chromium, and zinc (use calcium citrate, not carbonate, if supplementing on these). Antibiotics deplete good bacteria plus B1, B2, B6, B3, B12, vitamin K (with courses over ten days), calcium, magnesium, and potassium. Oral contraceptives deplete B2, B3, B5, B6, B12, folate, magnesium, zinc, vitamin C, and tyrosine (avoid grapefruit juice, which can raise estrogen levels up to 30%).","Anti-anxiety medications and antidepressants (including SSRIs) diminish melatonin, and SSRIs specifically also lower iodine, selenium, and folate — do not combine antidepressants with St. John's wort, 5-HTP, or SAMe without medical guidance. Anti-inflammatory drugs deplete iron, vitamin C, potassium, folate, melatonin, and zinc; aspirin specifically depletes B1, biotin, folate, C, iron, and zinc; stronger anti-inflammatories like steroids deplete potassium, vitamin C, zinc, vitamin D, selenium, calcium, folic acid, and magnesium.","Blood pressure medications deplete calcium, B1, B6, vitamin C, zinc, sodium, potassium, and magnesium; ACE inhibitors specifically deplete zinc, CoQ10, magnesium, and potassium; beta blockers deplete CoQ10, choline, melatonin, B1, and vitamin D (avoid oranges within two hours of atenolol, and separate vitamin D dosing by four hours); thiazide diuretics deplete B1, B2, folate, CoQ10, magnesium, potassium, sodium, and zinc; calcium channel blockers deplete melatonin and vitamin D. Cholesterol-lowering statins deplete CoQ10 (avoid grapefruit juice), and high-dose vitamin D may blunt atorvastatin's effectiveness. Bisphosphonates deplete calcium; albuterol depletes magnesium and potassium; hormone/estrogen therapy depletes vitamins A, B1, B2, B6, B12, C, magnesium, and zinc; Synthroid and other thyroid replacements deplete calcium; tranquilizers deplete CoQ10 and B2."],"conditions":["Fatigue and low energy","Chronic fatigue syndrome","GERD","Autoimmune conditions","Anxiety","Depression"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_liver_detox_overview","chapter":"foundations","title":"The body under toxic siege — why detoxification matters","body":["The liver is the body's primary detoxifying organ: it converts nutrients into usable forms, filters cholesterol and lipoproteins, metabolizes biological molecules, stores vitamins and iron, produces bile, destroys old blood cells, and processes and excretes toxins and spent hormones on their way out through the intestine, kidneys, skin, and lungs. When the liver can't fully process a given compound, it stores it locally rather than eliminating it — the toxin accumulates in tissue instead of leaving the body.","A toxin is anything eaten, breathed, or absorbed through the skin that the body reads as foreign or dangerous and mounts an inflammatory response against. In small amounts toxins are handled easily, but the modern world delivers a slow, cumulative overload — more than one hundred thousand new synthetic chemicals have entered daily life in recent decades — that can damage cells down to the mitochondria, the cell's energy-producing engine. A high body burden of toxins makes weight loss harder, damages metabolism, and disrupts the liver's ability to detoxify, worsening inflammation in a self-reinforcing cycle. Toxins also lower immunity (raising vulnerability to autoimmunity, cancer, and blood sugar problems), blunt the body's response to insulin and leptin (driving insulin resistance and weight gain), affect epigenetics, and inflame the gut lining, contributing directly to leaky gut.","Detoxification, like every other foundation of health, isn't a stand-alone fix — it works best alongside good sleep, stress management, and an anti-inflammatory diet, and shouldn't be pushed too aggressively before the gut and adrenal system are healthy enough to handle it (detoxing too early can backfire). The approach below follows four steps: identify your toxin exposures, keep elimination pathways (fluids, bowels, lymph) moving, eat foods that support detoxification, and swap out toxic products for clean ones at home."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Fatigue and low energy","Leaky gut syndrome","Chronic fatigue syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_heavy_metals","chapter":"foundations","title":"Identify your toxins, part 1 — heavy metals","body":["Mercury is released into the air largely from coal-burning industry, settles into soil and waterways, and bioaccumulates most heavily in large predatory fish like tuna and swordfish; it's also found in some dental amalgam fillings, cosmetics, pesticides, and a small number of vaccines. Health effects depend on the form and dose of exposure: in adults it can contribute to dementia, and prenatal exposure can cross the placenta and impair a child's cognitive development, with high-level exposure linked to developmental problems including intellectual disability, cerebellar ataxia, limb deformities, altered growth, sensory impairment, and cerebral palsy.","Arsenic commonly contaminates drinking water and shows up as pesticide residue on fruit, vegetables, chicken, and rice, as well as in household detergents, colored chalk, wine, rat poison, and automobile exhaust; it accumulates up the food chain from animal feed. Exposure is linked to diabetes and gout, and it interferes with the body's ability to clear mercury, compounding autoimmune and cancer risk.","Lead is a severe neurotoxin that accumulates in bone and can be stored there for years. It persists in older homes' paint and water pipes and occasionally turns up in imported cosmetics and pottery. Exposure is linked to cardiovascular disease and lowered IQ, particularly in children."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Fatigue and low energy"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_hormone_disruptors","chapter":"foundations","title":"Identify your toxins, part 2 — hormone-disrupting chemicals","body":["Hormone-disrupting chemicals (endocrine disruptors) are readily absorbed and mimic the body's own hormones, sending mixed signals that throw off the endocrine system's natural regulation. Xenoestrogens mimic estrogen, build up in fatty tissue, and are linked to early puberty, difficult or early periods, male infertility, obesity, cancer, and increased inflammation (in men, they can show up as increased breast tissue or low libido); BPA, phthalates, and parabens are all xenoestrogens. Phthalates and parabens are added to hairspray, perfume, body wash, shampoo, lotion, fast food packaging, plastics stamped #3, and detergents — often disguised on labels simply as \"fragrance\" — and are linked to cancer, endocrine disruption, ADHD, diabetes, and obesity.","Triclosan, an antimicrobial in antibacterial soap, deodorant, hairspray, and toothpaste (and found in some kitchenware, toys, clothing, and furniture), is correlated with higher rates of childhood allergy and antibiotic-resistant bacteria; the EPA has labeled it a pesticide that works no better than plain soap. Agricultural pesticides, even in small dietary amounts, disrupt hormones and the microbiome over time and are linked to weight gain, insulin resistance, diabetes, fatigue, irritability, depression, forgetfulness, and inflammation broadly.","Dioxins and PCBs, banned since the 1970s but still persistent in the environment and in fish, meat, and dairy, raise TSH and thyroid antibodies by displacing iodine and are linked to Hashimoto's thyroiditis, other autoimmune disease, cancer, diabetes, and heart attack. Chlorine (industrial products, pools, plastics, pesticides, cleaning products) is linked to rising antibiotic resistance. Brominated flame retardants (PBDEs) in furniture, mattresses, electronics, and some baked goods and drinks act as another iodine look-alike and are linked to Hashimoto's, ADHD, and diabetes. Fluoride, added to water supplies as a cavity-prevention measure without a controlled individual dose, disrupts the endocrine system, blocks iodine absorption, raises thyroid and cancer risk, and was classified a neurotoxin by Lancet Neurology in 2014; cavity prevention can be achieved with topical fluoride and good nutrition instead of systemic exposure.","Other notable exposures: DEA (diethanolamine) in foaming shampoos and toothpaste is linked to esophageal and stomach cancer in animal studies and remains legal in the U.S. despite bans elsewhere. Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) in perfume, air freshener, and household cleaners are neurotoxic. Perchlorate, used in fertilizer and fireworks, contaminates water and produce. Surfactants like sodium lauryl sulfate (SLS), sodium laureth sulfate (SLES), and dextran sodium sulfate (DSS) — found in roughly 90% of shampoo, toothpaste, and detergent — are notably toxic to the GI tract, increasing intestinal permeability and disrupting hormone function."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Leaky gut syndrome","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Eczema","Psoriasis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_keep_fluids_eat_detox","chapter":"foundations","title":"Detox steps 2 and 3 — keep the fluids moving, eat to detoxify","body":["Step two is keeping elimination pathways open. The skin is a major elimination organ, so sweating through exercise or a sauna, dry brushing/exfoliating, and alternating baths with Epsom salt, sodium bicarbonate, seaweed, or sea salt all support toxin clearance and can ease die-off reactions. Oil pulling and infrared saunas can help as well. Warm lemon water in the morning encourages urination, another elimination route. Regular bowel movements matter just as much — occasional enemas or colon cleanses can help those who aren't eliminating reliably. Massage turns the gel-like extracellular matrix into a more liquid state, improving lymphatic drainage.","Step three is eating to detoxify. Aim for roughly 80% alkaline, 20% acidic foods (acidic foods being dairy, processed food, sugar, flour, and meat), getting 30-40 g of fiber a day from raw fruits, vegetables, herbs, and spices, which are rich in sulfur and fiber that bind and carry out toxins through stool. Favor asparagus, bok choy, celery, citrus, green apples, kefir, kimchi, sauerkraut, olives, plums, and cruciferous vegetables (broccoli, Brussels sprouts, beets, cauliflower, watercress, cabbage, kale, Swiss chard, collards — raw is more alkaline than cooked). Salmon, berries, pumpkin seeds, olive oil, lentils, Brazil nuts, wheatgrass, and grapes support liver function directly, and apple cider vinegar, dandelion tea, milk thistle tea, and chamomile tea help cleanse the liver and gallbladder. Detox-supportive herbs include caraway, dill seed, curcumin (turmeric), rosemary, thyme, cumin, basil, poppy seed, oregano, black pepper, and cilantro, a natural chelator.","Glutathione is the body's master antioxidant, concentrated in the liver, and it binds and helps clear heavy metals (arsenic, cadmium, mercury) and other cellular waste; heavy environmental exposure depletes it and worsens inflammation. Support the body's own glutathione production with garlic, onions, cilantro, and cruciferous vegetables; N-acetyl cysteine (NAC) is a direct precursor. Alpha lipoic acid (ALA) recycles used glutathione, is a potent antioxidant that protects skin and balances blood sugar, and is specifically useful for mercury detoxification — food sources include dark leafy greens, animal foods, and organ meats. Blue-green algae (chlorella and spirulina) chelate heavy metals and support detoxification broadly: chlorella (buy \"cracked cell wall\" for absorbability) has been studied for removing dioxins, cadmium, lead, and radiation exposure, while spirulina enhances the microbiome, helps clear candida, and supports immunity. Anyone eating a lot of fish may benefit from blue-green algae to help keep mercury from accumulating in tissue.","For a higher toxic burden, a supervised supplement protocol (taken on an empty stomach) might include acetyl-glutathione (600-1,200 mg/day) or NAC (600-1,800 mg/day), vitamin C (2,000 mg/day), alpha lipoic acid (300-600 mg/day — R-ALA is more bioavailable, and can lower blood sugar in diabetics or interact with chemo/radiation), and milk thistle (150 mg twice daily). At doses of 1,200 mg/day or more, ALA can produce strong-smelling urine or rare allergic reactions and should be paired with a biotin-containing multivitamin, since it can interfere with biotin absorption. This should build on — not replace — the gut-healing groundwork of probiotics and lipase-containing digestive enzymes established earlier."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Leaky gut syndrome","Fatigue and low energy","Constipation","Candidiasis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_swap_toxic_for_clean","chapter":"foundations","title":"Detox step 4 — swap out toxic for clean at home","body":["Home and kitchen: House plants (spider plant, aloe vera, weeping fig, Chinese evergreen, bamboo, gerbera daisy, English ivy, chrysanthemum) help filter indoor air. Replace chemical cleaners with vinegar, baking soda, and hydrogen peroxide; over time swap synthetic carpets, window treatments, and furniture fabric or bedding for untreated natural fibers (wool, bamboo, organic cotton, hemp), avoid flame retardants, use low- or no-VOC paint, and skip air fresheners. In the kitchen, cook with glass, ceramic, uncoated stainless steel, or cast iron rather than nonstick coatings; eliminate plastics stamped #3, #6, or #7 in favor of glass, ceramic, or wood, use unbleached parchment instead of PVC-based cling wrap, and check canned goods for a BPA-free label. Avoid microwaving in plastic.","Bathroom and laundry: Choose natural soaps like castile and mildly antibacterial essential oils like tea tree; skip hand sanitizer; replace vinyl (PVC) shower curtains with organic cotton or hemp liners. Switch to nontoxic laundry detergent, skip fabric softener (a cup of baking soda in the wash does the job), use white vinegar instead of bleach, and choose 100% organic cotton sheets; leave the washer door open between loads to prevent mold, and seek out clean/organic dry cleaners since conventional dry cleaning is a significant toxin exposure.","Personal care and water: The average woman applies over 500 synthetic chemicals to her skin daily, so choose organic, gluten-free, preservative-free skin care, cosmetics, and feminine hygiene products, and look for aluminum-free natural deodorant (a light dusting of baking soda also works). For water, a reverse-osmosis system with triple filtration — or at minimum filtered water in glass bottles — reduces drug and toxin residue; a whole-house purification and fluoride-filtration system is worth considering, and the filter should target heavy metals, bacteria/viruses/parasites, nitrates, VOCs, sediment, medication residue, and copper (trace minerals can be added back for taste if needed).","Oral health, electronics, and mold: See a holistic or biological dentist for biocompatible materials — avoid amalgam fillings, replacing them with white resin or zirconium, and insist on a rubber dam and amalgam separator if a mercury filling is being removed, followed by an activated-charcoal rinse (the International Academy of Oral Medicine and Toxicology, daomt.org, lists qualified practitioners). Limit electromagnetic field exposure by turning off the wifi router at night, keeping the phone off the body and in airplane mode when possible, and using speaker or headsets. Mold thrives in damp, humid spaces — bathrooms, basements, crawl spaces, flat roofs, older homes with known leaks — and can also be ingested through pistachios, cashews, peanuts, aged cheese, vinegar, pickled foods, dried fruit, and mushrooms; symptoms of mold or mycotoxin exposure can include depression and other mood changes, ADHD-like symptoms, skin rashes, headaches, autoimmunity, sleep disruption, fibromyalgia, and fatigue, and it's worth investigating when someone isn't improving on the rest of this protocol."],"conditions":["Autoimmune conditions","Mold and mycotoxin illness","Eczema","Psoriasis","Fatigue and low energy"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_digest2_rx_at_a_glance","chapter":"foundations","title":"Digestive health and detoxification Rx at a glance","body":["The Gut Rx: build every meal around vegetables, clean protein, and healthy fat. Follow an elimination diet for about four weeks or until symptoms improve — removing grains, dairy, sugar, processed foods, and starchy vegetables, and, if needed, also legumes, soy, nuts, eggs, alcohol, nightshades, and caffeine. Add at least one cup of bone broth daily. Focus on probiotics (watching for a die-off reaction) and prebiotic foods. Add Epsom salt baths. If there's still no improvement, add targeted supplements and consider further testing.","The Nutrition/Supplement Rx: balance every meal around nutrient-dense foods that heal the gut and keep glucose and insulin stable. Fast three to four hours between meals, and stop eating at least three hours before bed; use intermittent fasting as appropriate. Add, as needed: probiotics, vitamin D or cod liver oil, magnesium, omega-3 fatty acids, and vitamin K2.","The Detox Rx: confirm and identify your toxin exposures. Keep the fluids moving — sweat, hydrate, and keep bowels regular. Eat vegetables and fruit to keep the body alkaline. Clean up the environment by swapping toxic products for clean ones. Add supplements if needed. And detoxify body and mind together — laughter, play, and joy are part of the protocol, not an afterthought."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Irritable bowel syndrome","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_stress_physiology","chapter":"foundations","title":"Calming the stress monster — what chronic stress does to the body","body":["Stress causes more than 80 percent of the complaints patients bring to their doctors, and it can exacerbate almost any chronic illness. Managing stress is essential to any healing and disease-prevention program: thoughts, feelings, and emotions affect physical health, and physical health in turn affects spiritual, emotional, and overall psychological well-being — everything is connected.","The stress response begins when the body feels threatened: the heart races, blood pressure rises, muscles tighten, and breathing quickens. The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis releases hormones, including cortisol, in the classic \"fight-or-flight\" mechanism meant to protect you and prevent injury. Once the threat passes, dopamine is released, giving a sense of accomplishment. This response can be lifesaving in an emergency, but constant bombardment by everyday stressors wears the body down. Cortisol keeps being secreted until the stressor resolves, the adrenal glands tire out (commonly called adrenal fatigue), or you learn to manage stress effectively. Short-term stress can cause fatigue, weight gain, frequent illness, hair loss, and skin, mood, digestive, hormonal, and sleep problems. With chronic stress the body feels under constant attack with little downtime between stressors, disrupting equilibrium and leading to immune suppression, inflammation, and chronic illness.","Chronic stress can affect every organ, not just the mind. Studies show it can double the chance of becoming obese, can cause diabetes, can lead to a five-fold increased risk of dying from heart-related problems, a 65 percent increased risk of developing dementia, an increased risk of breast cancer, and a 70 percent increased risk of disability in later life. The Journal of the American Medical Association found that too much stress can be as bad for the heart as smoking and high cholesterol.","Severe and acute stress raises cortisol and blood sugar, triggers leptin and insulin resistance, promotes weight gain around the middle, and can ultimately contribute to full-blown diabetes and further inflammation. Stress can also imbalance reproductive hormones, contributing to polycystic ovarian syndrome, which prevents ovulation and causes infertility.","Assessing your own stressors is the first step in managing them. They can be external (personal-life challenges) or internal (emotional issues, unrealistic expectations, low self-esteem, fears, guilt, mood issues). Make a list of past, current, and future stresses, and honestly evaluate whether you're taking care of yourself — diet, sleep, medication use, nutrient deficiencies. Chronic stress response can also be hardwired from childhood: early-life stress or trauma can subtly shift the microbiome, alter a healthy HPA axis, and cue the body to produce more cortisol and fewer beneficial bacteria, increasing stress reactivity and inflammation later in life."],"conditions":["Anxiety","Depression","Autoimmune conditions","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Hypertension","Fatigue and low energy","Chronic fatigue syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_stress_gut_immune","chapter":"foundations","title":"Stress, the gut-brain-microbiota axis, and the immune system","body":["The vagus nerve runs from the brain along the spinal column to the digestive system, forming the gut-brain-microbiota axis. The gut — the \"second brain\" — has its own receptors that respond to gut bacteria and their metabolites, and it also produces neurotransmitters (brain chemicals that carry messages between brain and body), including about 90 percent of the body's serotonin and 50 percent of its dopamine, the \"feel good\" chemical. Stress can negatively affect good bacteria, lowering probiotic levels even during acute stress and promoting overgrowth of bad bacteria in the small bowel; these pathogenic bacteria interfere with production of vital neurotransmitters. The more good bacteria you have, the more neurotransmitters you produce and the less pronounced your stress response — and the less depression, anxiety, and other mood disorders you experience.","High cortisol also lowers hydrochloric acid release, affects digestive-enzyme activity, lowers nutrient absorption, and increases food cravings that let bad bacteria thrive further. Studies show that adding back beneficial bacteria such as Bifidobacterium longum and Lactobacillus rhamnosus, along with prebiotics, can relieve stress and lower cortisol.","Stress also suppresses secretory IgA (an immune molecule), releases adrenaline and norepinephrine into the gut, and makes the digestive lining more permeable and inflammatory — that is, it worsens leaky gut. Even short bouts of stress can trigger or worsen leaky gut/intestinal permeability and make you susceptible to reactivation of latent infections (specifically Epstein-Barr virus) that may lead to autoimmunity.","A daily practice of relaxation techniques can decrease anxiety, lower blood pressure and heart rate, keep damaging hormones at bay, change gene expression, improve circulation and digestion, and enhance the immune system. Relaxation techniques trigger the vagus nerve and support the peripheral nervous system, making you more physically, emotionally, and spiritually resilient; research shows that toning down the fight-or-flight response can decrease the inflammatory response in autoimmune conditions like rheumatoid arthritis."],"conditions":["Leaky gut syndrome","Autoimmune conditions","Anxiety","Depression","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_relaxation_techniques","chapter":"foundations","title":"Relaxation techniques — breathing, visualization, and progressive muscle relaxation","body":["The relaxation response is a mentally active process (sitting in front of the TV or computer doesn't count) that leaves the body relaxed, calm, focused, and content. Most techniques fit into an existing schedule — at lunchtime, during a commute, or at bedtime — and need only about ten to thirty minutes a day. As you practice, you become more aware of muscle tension and other physical sensations of stress, so once the stress response is familiar you can use these tools the moment you start to feel stressed, anxious, or overwhelmed, before it spirals out of control. Try different techniques until one clicks — the ability to relax is a skill that improves with practice.","Belly breathing (4-7-8 breath): lie flat with legs straight or knees bent, or sit comfortably. Place one hand on the upper chest and the other on the abdomen so you can feel whether you're using the diaphragm rather than the chest muscles — the belly should rise more than the chest. Close your eyes and breathe in slowly through the nose to a count of 4, letting the stomach expand. Hold the breath for a count of 7. Exhale slowly and steadily through the mouth to a count of 8, letting the stomach fall as you release all the air; the hand on the chest should stay still. Pause for a count of 1, then repeat for ten breaths, keeping each breath as deep and slow as possible. Practicing this for five minutes two or three times a day helps establish equilibrium and is also a great weight-loss aid.","Visualization/guided imagery: sit in a quiet spot, loosen tight clothing, close your eyes, and imagine a peaceful, calming place where you feel completely content. Picture it vividly with all your senses — smell, sound, touch, sight — while focusing on your breath. This \"mental escape\" enhances coping skills by releasing tension and anxiety and creating harmony between body and mind.","Progressive muscle relaxation: deliberately tense a muscle group, then release the tension. Loosen clothing, take off your shoes, get comfortable, and breathe slowly and deeply. Shift attention to the right foot, slowly tense its muscles while inhaling, hold for a count of 10, then relax and notice the tension flowing away as the foot becomes limp. Move through the body in sequence — right foot, left foot, right and left calves, right and left thighs, hips and buttocks, stomach, chest, back, right and left arm and hand, neck and shoulders, and finally the face, forehead, and lips (left-handed people may prefer starting on the left). Combining progressive muscle relaxation with deep breathing gives an additional layer of stress relief.","Other relaxation techniques worth exploring include hypnosis, biofeedback, art therapy, neurofeedback, tapping/Emotional Freedom Techniques, float tanks, binaural beats, HeartMath, and relaxation apps."],"conditions":["Anxiety","Chronic pain","Fibromyalgia","Hypertension","Insomnia"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_stress_lifestyle_exercise_supplements","chapter":"foundations","title":"Lifestyle strategies for stress — diet, movement, and adaptogenic support","body":["Beyond formal relaxation practice, the foundations of good health lower stress on their own: heal the gut (probiotics help balance the peripheral nervous system); eat an anti-inflammatory diet and stay hydrated (vegetables, protein especially B12-rich sources like grass-fed beef, fermented foods, and wild-caught salmon, healthy fats, and plenty of water); avoid caffeine and alcohol, which interfere with the sleep cycle and slow adrenal recovery — try chamomile, nettle leaf, dandelion, or holy basil tea instead, and if you need caffeine, keep it to one serving before noon. Live in a clean environment to lower the body's toxic burden; add lavender oil to detox baths, spend time in nature, and consider a digital detox. Cultivate positive relationships and have fun — smiling and laughing lower cortisol and boost endorphins, and massage is a therapeutic, healing way to relieve inflammation and stress. Optimize sleep, connect with your higher purpose through meditation and gratitude, and time-manage: plan, prioritize, delegate, learn to say no, remove guilt, and declutter your physical and electronic environments.","Exercise is one of the biggest stress busters. Regular exercise lowers markers of systemic inflammation, reduces cravings, regulates appetite, improves insulin sensitivity, boosts self-confidence and digestive function by increasing microbial diversity, improves detoxification, boosts mood chemicals, sharpens memory, reduces fracture risk, and keeps chronic disease at bay. Stretch every morning and before/after exercising. Aim for at least thirty minutes of enjoyable exercise most days — intensity matters more than duration — mixing aerobic, strength training, core support, and fascia work, and listening to your body rather than overdoing it. Walking is the recommended starting point for most patients; aim for about ten thousand steps a day, ideally outdoors with deep cleansing breaths. Shorter high-intensity interval training (e.g., a five-minute warm-up, ten-minute interval of thirty seconds at 70–80 percent max heart rate followed by ninety seconds at target heart rate, then a five-minute cool-down) can boost metabolism and calorie burn for hours afterward. Strength training (yoga, tai chi, weights/resistance bands, two to three times a week) increases lean muscle mass, strengthens bone, and boosts resting metabolism; yoga and tai chi also reduce anxiety and stress by calming the mind and focusing on the breath and present moment. Exercise generally reduces inflammation, but chronic, excessive, or strenuous exercise can increase intestinal permeability and inflammation — if you feel exhausted or worse afterward rather than good and energized, you're doing too much.","In times of chronic stress the body needs extra nutrients to support the adrenals, since stress itself depletes magnesium, B vitamins (which help lower the toxic amino acid homocysteine), omega-3s, and vitamin C. Adaptogenic plant compounds help balance and modulate the stress response: ashwagandha (500 mg once or twice daily) lowers cortisol and balances hormones; Asian ginseng root extract (200 mg twice daily); Rhodiola root extract (50 mg twice daily); Cordyceps, containing cordycepic acid and adenosine (400 mg twice daily); and holy basil. Homeopathy and acupuncture are also options. Seek professional help if, despite these efforts, warning signs appear — inability to sleep, depression, using drugs or alcohol to cope, uncontrollable negative or self-destructive thoughts, or thoughts of suicide."],"conditions":["Anxiety","Depression","Fatigue and low energy","Autoimmune conditions","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_sleep_why_it_matters","chapter":"foundations","title":"Getting a quality snooze — why sleep matters","body":["One of the most critical aspects of keeping the body regulated isn't what happens during the day but what happens at night. Getting enough sleep normalizes cortisol levels, improves memory, and helps control weight and overall inflammation. The body consumes little energy while asleep, leaving more available over eight or nine hours for the body to remove toxins, make hormones, activate cellular repair, boost growth-hormone secretion, mend injury, and fight infections.","When sleep is insufficient, the body can't put out the effort needed for these tasks, leading to hormonal imbalance and inflammation. In the short term, sleep deprivation causes tiredness, irritability, decreased quality of life, attention deficits, depression, anger, and anxiety, along with decreased performance and alertness, impaired cognition and memory, increased appetite (via stimulated ghrelin, the hunger hormone), suppressed leptin (the satiety hormone), and a tendency to hold onto fat. Cutting sleep by just one and a half hours in a single night can reduce daytime alertness by as much as 32 percent.","Sleep regulates most hormone production, and 15 percent of our DNA is controlled by the circadian rhythm, including the body's repair mechanisms. Poor circadian rhythms are linked to numerous serious illnesses: being twice as likely to feel depressed or anxious and encounter other mood issues, quadrupling cancer risk, doubling the chance of obesity, and being linked with diabetes, insulin resistance, an increase in the inflammatory marker hsCRP (a strong predictor of heart disease), and increased risk of heart disease and stroke. Poor sleep is also related to frontal lobe syndrome — confusion, impulsivity, poor judgment, an inability to perform tasks, and catatonia.","To target sleep problems, first assess the likely cause, since stress and disease can cause inflammation that leads to sleep issues that then worsen the underlying problem. Short-term insomnia can stem from an upcoming exam, jet lag, a change in work schedule, emotional stress, or a sleep-hygiene problem. Chronic conditions such as sleep apnea, restless legs, chronic pain, and narcolepsy can also hinder sleep. Medications that can cause insomnia include antidepressants, cold and flu medications containing alcohol, pain relievers containing caffeine (such as Midol or Excedrin), diuretics, corticosteroids, thyroid hormone, high blood pressure medications (beta blockers, calcium channel blockers), anticonvulsants, bronchodilators, decongestants, and stimulants."],"conditions":["Insomnia","Fatigue and low energy","Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Depression","Anxiety","Hypertension"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_sleep_hygiene","chapter":"foundations","title":"Sleep hygiene — building the routine and environment for restful sleep","body":["Sleep hygiene means the practices that, when implemented, promote better-quality sleep. Sleep is a treat, not a punishment, and prioritizing it makes you more efficient during the day. Create a restful sanctuary: set the night room temperature to about 68°F, hang blackout curtains, clear clutter, and limit noise or use a white-noise fan. A supportive mattress pad can help regulate body temperature, and socks can keep extremities warm. A household plant can improve air quality and help offset electromagnetic fields (EMFs). Reserve the bedroom for sleep and sex only — keep the office out of the bedroom.","Keep a consistent bedtime routine, going to sleep and waking at roughly the same time every day (within about thirty minutes, or up to an hour on weekends) — this applies to adults just as much as children. If you're short on sleep one night, get back on schedule right away rather than sleeping in or taking a long nap. Going to sleep between 9:00 and 11:30 p.m. promotes the best hormonal environment for sleep, fat burning, and regeneration. Start winding down about two hours before bedtime with meditation or other stress-relieving strategies, and take a warm bath with Epsom salt and lavender oil. Write down worries before bed. Turn off electronics at least ninety minutes before bedtime to let melatonin and cortisol normalize; minimize blue light with amber lights/glasses or a blue-light-filtering computer program. Turn off Wi-Fi, put the phone six feet away on airplane mode, and turn off notifications to limit EMF exposure. Earthing sheets can mimic direct ground contact and support sleep. Avoid eating for about ninety minutes before bed; if you must eat, choose something high in fat and low in carbohydrate to limit an insulin spike. If you can't fall asleep after forty-five minutes, get up and try again in an hour rather than lying there.","The day shapes how you sleep. Daytime sleep disrupters include caffeine, sugar, stress, lack of movement, and too much artificial or fluorescent light exposure in the evening. Foods that help sleep include walnuts, avocados, and green leafy vegetables. Exercise regularly in the morning or afternoon, and spend time in direct contact with the ground to help create internal balance. Stay well hydrated, and replace caffeine with relaxing teas like chamomile or valerian (adding a teaspoon of gelatin). Limit caffeine to the morning; nap for only twenty to thirty minutes and before 4:00 p.m. Get bright light exposure for at least thirty minutes between 6:00 and 8:30 a.m. to cue the circadian rhythm and stay alert — light boxes or visors can substitute when stuck at a desk. After sunset, swap bright white lights for amber bulbs or amber glasses to help the body shift into sleep mode.","For shift work: adjust the sleep-wake cycle by getting bright light exposure while awake and wearing dark glasses on the way home, bunch night shifts together, avoid frequently rotating shifts, get extra sleep before a shift, eat well on the job, and destress after getting home; adaptogens such as eleuthero can help with shift-work insomnia if needed. For travel and jet lag: leave home well rested, and once at the destination stay awake until local bedtime; melatonin can help."],"conditions":["Insomnia","Fatigue and low energy","Chronic fatigue syndrome"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_social_health","chapter":"foundations","title":"Building social support — relationships as medicine","body":["Good company is good for your health. Feeling loved releases a flood of potent hormones that make us feel better emotionally and significantly strengthen the immune system — giving and receiving love inspires healing physically, emotionally, and spiritually. Humans are social creatures by nature and need each other to survive from a very young age. Positive relationships — from a partner, friends, family, or pets — improve the immune system and can be healing; friends prevent loneliness, offer companionship, increase a sense of belonging and purpose, boost happiness, reduce stress, improve self-confidence and self-worth, and help you cope with trauma.","Brain MRI and blood/saliva studies show that feeling loved and supported releases serotonin, relaxin, endorphins, and oxytocin — hormones that lower cortisol, improve blood circulation, lower blood pressure and heart rate, improve digestion, clear out toxins, increase natural killer cells and other white blood cells, red blood cells, IgA, and helper T cells, helping the immune system clear infections and renew energy to repair cells and fight cancer. This lowers overall inflammation, improves sleep, relieves restlessness, and decreases chronic pain as muscle tension and pain perception lower. Conversely, negative relationships and loneliness are linked to earlier death, increased cortisol, and depleted immunity. Studies show that pet ownership releases the same beneficial hormones, and pet owners tend to live longer than people without pets.","To find positive support: join groups (a class, support group, faith community, or volunteer opportunity) to meet people with similar interests and goals; reach out with small acts of kindness — smiling, holding a door, chatting with neighbors, calling someone you love just to say you were thinking of them, and don't be afraid to ask for help yourself. Surround yourself with people who lift you up rather than pull you down. If caring for someone with a chronic condition, remember to take care of yourself too — listen actively, be nonjudgmental, validate their feelings, allow for setbacks, and join a support group for your own sake.","To nurture relationships: be your own best friend first, building a positive self-image and self-esteem. Seek quality friendships, focus on the positive in each relationship, listen and share while respecting boundaries and privacy, and accept others as they are. It's never too late to build a new friendship or reconnect with an old one — a healthy relationship is always a give and take, and letting people know they're appreciated matters. Investing time in quality friendships helps heal chronic inflammation and disease."],"conditions":["Depression","Anxiety","Chronic pain","Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_spirituality_meditation","chapter":"foundations","title":"Cultivating spirituality — meditation, mindfulness, and gratitude","body":["Spirituality has a measurable relationship with health. Daily meditation and positivity cultivate calmness and optimal physical health: as spiritual energy flows, blood circulation improves, blood pressure goes down, cortisol and stress levels drop, digestion improves, the body detoxifies, unhealthy genes are switched off, and the immune system is enhanced. Religion isn't the same as spirituality — religion is a coded set of beliefs, while spirituality is about one's relationship with the soul, encouraging a deep bodily and emotional sensation of calm and peace, connectedness, and clarified goals.","Gratitude — appreciation for what one has — is central to nearly every spiritual tradition and one of the most effective ways to connect with the soul. Being thankful creates a subconscious world of positivity that governs 90 percent of our thoughts and actions, boosting optimism, which helps prevent disease and improves immune function, well-being, and happiness. Practiced daily rather than occasionally, gratitude lowers inflammatory markers, influences epigenetics, and adds years to life. \"Heart-felt\" emotions like gratitude, love, and caring produce coherent heart-rhythm patterns, while depleted emotions like frustration, anger, anxiety, and insecurity produce erratic patterns the brain reads as stress, raising the risk of heart disease and high blood pressure, weakening immunity, and impairing cognition. A 2015 American Psychological Association study found higher gratitude scores were proportional to better mood, higher-quality sleep, more self-efficacy, less inflammation, and improved heart health. Gratitude also makes people more optimistic, and optimism helps them bounce back faster from stress; optimistic cancer patients predicted less disruption of normal life, distress, and fatigue than pessimistic patients, who were more likely to experience thickening arteries.","Practical ways to build gratitude: on waking, take a moment to name ten things you're grateful for, which sets a positive tone for the day and trains the subconscious; place sticky notes reading \"I am grateful for this\" on objects around the house to subliminally reinforce positivity. Journal for two to five minutes daily about what you're thankful for — you can also list negative thoughts and then reframe them by finding what's positive in the situation. Express gratitude to people who've helped you, which motivates others and creates a peaceful environment; combining it with relaxation techniques like deep breathing or a walk outside amplifies the effect.","Meditation changes the brain, literally — studies show it alters gray matter volume, preserves the aging brain, and increases neuronal activity in emotional-regulation, self-control, and positive-emotion areas by reducing activity in the brain's \"me\" centers and enhancing connectivity between regions. Just a couple of weeks of meditation practice is linked to reduced symptoms of depression, anxiety (including social anxiety), and pain, and can help increase focus, memory, and productivity. It can also reduce heart-attack and stroke risk in at-risk people, increase longevity, decrease carotid atherosclerosis, increase insulin sensitivity, and help with delayed left-ventricular enlargement, headaches, migraines, and involuntary-movement disorders such as Parkinson's disease and Tourette syndrome; the American Heart Association endorses transcendental meditation as a complementary treatment for high blood pressure in appropriate circumstances.","Mindfulness — attention to how you're feeling right now, internally and externally — brings the nervous system back into balance and decreases stress; it limits worry about the past or future and can be applied to almost any activity (eating, exercising, walking, working). To practice: choose a quiet, comfortable environment, sit with the spine straight, pick a point of focus (an internal feeling, a word or phrase, a calming image, or an object), and keep eyes open or closed. Develop a nonjudgmental, observant attitude toward physical sensations; if distracted, gently return attention to the point of focus, concentrate on the breath, and be grateful. Mindfulness can be combined with progressive muscle relaxation or body-scan meditation (imagining hovering above yourself, noticing how each body part feels)."],"conditions":["Anxiety","Depression","Chronic pain","Hypertension","Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_forgiveness_prayer","chapter":"foundations","title":"Forgiveness, prayer, and finding a spiritual practice","body":["Unresolved hurt or offense can quietly smolder and increase inflammation, blocking full healing from chronic disease. Forgiveness is a decision to let go of resentment and thoughts of revenge, lessening the hurt and freeing attention for the positive parts of life; it can lead to understanding, empathy, and compassion for the person who caused the hurt, and learning to forgive is essential to spiritual health and inner peace. To forgive, try to understand the other person's actions with compassion; journaling can help reframe the hurt with new, positive meaning. Even being grateful for the people who hurt you — recognizing they shaped who you are — can help resolve the negative charge attached to the event, allowing you to forgive both yourself and others.","Daily prayer: set aside at least five quiet minutes to pray, concentrating on connecting to a divine energy or using affirmations and visualizations, and surrendering worries and hurt to a higher power. Combining a walking or breathing meditation with prayer deepens mental focus. Spiritual groups — a prayer circle or group meditation — provide accountability and support for spiritual growth; other inflammation-lowering spiritual practices include therapeutic touch, healing touch, and qigong, along with reading inspirational material, artistic self-expression, and finding a sense of purpose.","Finding a spiritual practice that works for you is key to healing chronic disease, whatever form it takes. Every day the mind is full of worries, thoughts, and dreams, but at a deeper level it begins to quiet — that deeper level is the core of spiritual practice. Slow racing thoughts by focusing on something else, like the breath, a special image, or prayer; find a quiet place, unplug from electronics, and set aside obligations, allowing thoughts to eventually dissipate rather than forcing stillness."],"conditions":["Depression","Anxiety","Chronic pain","Autoimmune conditions"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"},{"id":"hrx_found_fours_staying_motivated","chapter":"foundations","title":"Reality check — staying motivated to apply the foundations","body":["Once you know the foundations of good health, the real challenge is implementing them, especially when you don't feel well — motivation is hard. A few strategies help translate knowledge into practice: cultivate a healthy mindset and recognize that picking up this kind of guidance already means you're considering a better, happier life; discover your own reason for wanting change (pain-free living, achieving a dream); and remember that the microbiome's bad bugs can influence cravings and behavior — you are still in control of your own decisions.","Out with the old, in with the new: remove inflammatory foods from every hiding spot and stock up on nutrient-dense options — raw veggies, low-glycemic fruits, fresh meats, hardboiled eggs, and nuts for snacks; bake with coconut, nut, or seed flours and keep baked goods refrigerated. Set achievable, dated goals and targets, and identify the specific obstacles standing in your way so you can plan around them one at a time.","Build a weekly routine: grocery shop once a week, sticking to the perimeter of the store for veggies, clean protein, and healthy fats, and never shopping hungry; plan meals ahead, cooking staples for the week on one day; and use small spare moments — fifteen free minutes for a walk, deep breathing during a commute — since mindfulness and gratitude can be layered onto any activity. Build a positive support system by telling those around you what you're doing and asking for their support, bringing your own food to events, and finding friends who will hold you accountable. Don't overwhelm yourself — take it one meal and one day at a time — and have fun, rewarding progress with non-food rewards. Setbacks are steps forward, not failures: getting back on the wagon after a slip is itself the platform for a stronger comeback."],"conditions":["Obesity","Diabetes mellitus, type 2","Autoimmune conditions","Fatigue and low energy"],"source":"The Holistic Rx — Madiha Saeed, MD"}]};